## sw.fs.uploadFromRequest(req, { path, maxBytes?, allowedTypes?, fieldName?, public? })
// One-call upload handler for browser <input type="file"> forms. Parses
// multipart/form-data from the request, validates size + type, writes to
// storage, returns a short-lived signed URL for the private file by default.
// Pass public: true only for a world-readable file; then url is its permanent
// /storage URL. The result includes visibility so the caller can tell which.
//
// The whole point: the developer chooses the path, the helper handles
// every failure mode. Three lines instead of fifteen, and you never
// JSON-stringify raw bytes (which is what corrupts images on project_patch
// — use binary_files for asset deploys, sw.fs.uploadFromRequest for
// runtime uploads).

```ts
// api/upload.ts
export default async function handler(req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext) {
  if (req.method !== 'POST') return new Response('Use POST.', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'POST' } });
  const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(req)
  if (!user) return new Response('unauthorized', { status: 401 })
  const { url, path, size, contentType } = await sw.fs.server.uploadFromRequest(req, {
    path: `/uploads/${user.id}/${crypto.randomUUID()}.bin`,
    maxBytes: 5 * 1024 * 1024,                     // optional, 5 MB cap
    allowedTypes: ['image/png', 'image/jpeg'],     // optional whitelist
    fieldName: 'file',                             // optional, default 'file'
  })
  return Response.json({ url, path, size, contentType })
}
```

// On the browser: const fd = new FormData(); fd.append('file', input.files[0])
//                 const r = await fetch('/api/upload', { method: 'POST', body: fd })
//                 const { url } = await r.json(); img.src = url
//
// Throws on every failure with a stable code in the message:
//   UPLOAD_PATH_REQUIRED, UPLOAD_NOT_MULTIPART, UPLOAD_PARSE_FAILED,
//   UPLOAD_FIELD_MISSING, UPLOAD_TYPE_NOT_ALLOWED, UPLOAD_FILE_EMPTY,
//   UPLOAD_TOO_LARGE
// Wrap in try/catch and map to the HTTP status you want.
//
// For files larger than ~25 MB, mint a signed URL with sw.fs.uploadUrl
// instead and have the browser PUT direct — keeps the bytes off your
// function CPU.

## sw.fs.uploadUrl({ path, maxSize?, contentType?, expiresIn? })
// Mints a short-lived signed URL the browser can PUT bytes to directly
// — without ever seeing your platform key. Use this for big uploads
// (videos, large images, archives) so the bytes don't round-trip through
// your function.
```ts
// api/uploads/start.ts — a direct-upload URL for the signed-in user
export default async function(req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  if (req.method !== 'POST') return new Response('Use POST.', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'POST' } });
  const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(req)
  if (!user) return Response.json({ error: 'sign in' }, { status: 401 })
  const { url, multipart_url, expires_at } = await sw.fs.server.uploadUrl({
    path: `/uploads/${user.id}/photo.jpg`,
    maxSize: 10 * 1024 * 1024,         // optional, defaults to tier max
    contentType: 'image/jpeg',         // optional, '*' if omitted
    expiresIn: 300,                    // optional, seconds (default 300, max 3600)
  })
  return Response.json({ url, multipart_url, expires_at })
}
```
// Browser side:
//   await fetch(url, { method: 'PUT', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'image/jpeg' }, body: file })
// The PUT answers { path, size_bytes, content_type, version }.
//
// One request carries at most 100 MB. A bigger file (up to the plan's
// max_upload_mb) goes up in parts through the same URL — the response's
// multipart_url. Start, send each part, then complete:
//   const s = (await (await fetch(multipart_url, { method: 'POST',
//     headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
//     body: JSON.stringify({ size_bytes: file.size, content_type: file.type }) })).json()).data
//   // s = { upload_id, part_size, part_count, parts_url, complete_url, abort_url, expires_at }
//   for (let n = 1; n <= s.part_count; n++)
//     await fetch(s.parts_url + '/' + n, { method: 'PUT',
//       body: file.slice((n - 1) * s.part_size, n * s.part_size) })
//   await fetch(s.complete_url, { method: 'POST' })   // → { path, size_bytes, content_type, version }
// Every part except the last is exactly part_size bytes (at most 90 MB).
// Starting uses up the URL. An upload not completed within 6 hours is
// discarded, parts included; DELETE abort_url discards it sooner. A file
// over the plan's limit is refused at start with PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE.

## sw.fs.signedUrl(path, { expiresIn? })
// Mints a short-lived URL anyone can GET to download the file — no
// platform key, no app-user JWT. Use for email attachments, image
// previews, share links, "download once" handoffs. Default 1h, max 7d.
```ts
// api/invoices/link.ts — a one-hour download link for the signed-in user's invoice
export default async function(req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  if (req.method !== 'POST') return new Response('Use POST.', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'POST' } });
  const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(req)
  if (!user) return Response.json({ error: 'sign in' }, { status: 401 })
  const { url, expires_at } = await sw.fs.server.signedUrl(`/invoices/${user.id}/invoice-42.pdf`, {
    expiresIn: 3600,                   // optional, seconds (60..604800)
  })
  return Response.json({ url, expires_at })
}
```
// → url: 'https://api.somewhere.cloud/v1/fs-signed/<token>'
// → expires_at: ISO 8601 string
// Send the url in an email body, embed it in <img src=...>, etc.
// The URL stops working at expires_at; rotating JWT_SECRET invalidates
// every outstanding signed URL (bulk revocation lever).

For a smaller preview of a raster image, append `?w=384` to the signed URL.
The parameter is `w`, not `width`. Supported widths are 192, 384, 768 and 1536 pixels. Variants use WebP,
preserve aspect ratio and never upscale; the URL without `w` still returns
the original. Invalid `w` values and recognized unsupported image parameters
such as `width`, `height`, `format`, `quality` and `fit` return 400.
Other query parameters, including tracking tags and unrecognized names, are ignored.
Byte ranges work on originals; omit the `Range` header when requesting a variant.
An unsupported image type or failed transform returns the guarded original
with `X-Somewhere-Image-Variant: original`, rather than breaking the preview.
Signed downloads still verify the capability and current file revision before
using cached bytes. Cached bytes do not bypass expiry or permit another file.

## sw.fs.public_url(path, { makePublic? })
// Returns a file's permanent, unauthenticated public URL. Asking for the
// URL does NOT silently expose a private file:
//   - already-public file        → returns the URL, visibility unchanged.
//   - private file + makePublic   → publishes it, returns the URL.
//   - private file, no opt-in     → throws FILE_PRIVATE (file stays private).
// For a time-limited link to a private file WITHOUT making it world-readable,
// use sw.fs.server.signedUrl(path) instead.
```ts
// api/avatar/publish.ts — publish the signed-in user's avatar
export default async function(req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  if (req.method !== 'POST') return new Response('Use POST.', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'POST' } });
  const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(req)
  if (!user) return Response.json({ error: 'sign in' }, { status: 401 })
  // This avatar was written with sw.fs.server.write.
  const { public_url } = await sw.fs.server.public_url(`/uploads/${user.id}/avatar.png`, {
    makePublic: true,                  // required to publish a private file
  })
  return Response.json({ public_url })
}
```
// → public_url: 'https://myapp.somewhere.site/storage/uploads/<user id>/avatar.png'

## Public URL
Files written with `visibility: 'public'` are served (no auth) at:
https://{subdomain}.somewhere.site/storage/{path}
Private files (the default) return 404 here — they're reachable only from
your authenticated code (sw.fs.read) or a signed URL.

```html
<!-- Use the public URL directly in HTML -->
<img src="https://myapp.somewhere.site/storage/uploads/avatar.png" alt="Avatar" />
```

`sw.ai.chat` calls a model from a function with no provider key. Omit
`provider` and `model` to use the free default model, which draws only on your
account's included AI allowance and never on prepaid balance. Managed search
makes a user's own documents searchable by meaning. The platform extracts the
text, splits it and indexes it, so there's no embedding code or vector database
to run.

The free default, OpenAI-selected `sw.ai` calls, image generation and moderation use OpenAI. Claude-selected `sw.ai` calls and the free default's Claude backup use Anthropic. A provider-backed call is unavailable in regions where that provider does not operate. It returns `AI_REGION_UNAVAILABLE`, with no retry or charge. [OpenAI supported regions](https://help.openai.com/en/articles/7947663-chatgpt-and-openai-api-supported-countries-and-territories) · [Anthropic supported regions](https://www.anthropic.com/legal/supported-regions)

## Call a model

```ts
// api/suggest-title.ts
export default async function suggestTitle(
  request: Request,
  sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext,
): Promise<Response> {
  if (request.method !== 'POST') {
    return new Response('Use POST.', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'POST' } });
  }

  const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(request);
  if (!user) {
    return Response.json({ error: 'Sign in to get a suggestion.' }, { status: 401 });
  }

  const input: unknown = await request.json().catch(() => null);
  const text = typeof input === 'object' && input !== null && 'text' in input ? input.text : undefined;
  if (typeof text !== 'string' || !text.trim() || text.length > 4000) {
    return Response.json({ error: 'Send up to 4,000 characters of text.' }, { status: 400 });
  }

  // Model calls draw on the account's allowance: limit each user before calling one.
  const allowance = await sw.rateLimit.check(`suggest-title:${user.id}`, 20, 3600);
  if (!allowance.allowed) {
    return Response.json({ error: 'Too many suggestions. Try again later.' }, { status: 429 });
  }

  try {
    const reply = await sw.ai.chat({
      system: 'Reply with one short title for the text. No quotes.',
      messages: [{ role: 'user', content: text }],
    });
    return Response.json({ title: reply.text.trim() });
  } catch (error) {
    const code = error instanceof Error && 'code' in error ? String(error.code) : 'AI_FAILED';
    return Response.json({ error: 'No suggestion right now. Try again later.', code }, { status: 503 });
  }
}
```

Deploy, sign in, and POST `{ "text": "…" }` to `/api/suggest-title`. You get
back `{ "title": "…" }`, the model's suggestion. Read `reply.text` for prose
and `reply.content` for content blocks.

The free default refuses rather than charging, and each refusal has its own
code:

- `RATE_LIMITED`: its per-minute or per-day request limit, shared across the
  account owner's projects.
- `AI_ALLOWANCE_EXHAUSTED`: the account's included AI allowance is used up for
  the billing period.
- `AI_SPEND_CAP_REACHED`: the project's monthly AI spend cap, which counts
  allowance-paid calls too.

To use a larger or different model, name a `provider` and `model` from
`ai_catalog`. That paid path bills prepaid balance and counts toward the spend
cap, unless the call runs on your own provider key. Exact limits: `sw.ai` → `default-model-recommendation` and
`monthly-ai-spend-cap-per-project`.

## Search a user's documents

Declare per-user search once in `db/schema.ts`. Without it, managed search is
refused with `SEARCH_NOT_DECLARED`:

```ts
// db/schema.ts
import { owner, schema } from 'somewhere/db';

export default schema({}, { search: owner() });
```

Add your tables and file collections to the same call. From a signed-in
request, `await sw.search.add(path)` indexes one file that the user owns. It
refuses any other file, and it re-indexes when the file changes. Then search:

```ts
// api/search-documents.ts
export default async function searchDocuments(
  request: Request,
  sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext,
): Promise<Response> {
  if (request.method !== 'GET') {
    return new Response('Use GET.', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'GET' } });
  }

  const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(request);
  if (!user) {
    return Response.json({ error: 'Sign in to search your documents.' }, { status: 401 });
  }

  const query = new URL(request.url).searchParams.get('q')?.trim() ?? '';
  if (!query || query.length > 200) {
    return Response.json({ error: 'Search for 1–200 characters.' }, { status: 400 });
  }

  // The string form searches only the documents this user added.
  const { results } = await sw.search.query(query);
  return Response.json(results.map((hit) => ({ content: hit.content, score: hit.score, source: hit.source })));
}
```

Each hit has a passage, a score, and a `source` naming the file and where in
it the passage came from.

## Which search to use

| You want | Use |
|---|---|
| A user's own documents, by meaning | Managed search: `search: owner()`, `sw.search.add(path)`, `sw.search.query(text)` (above). Ownership comes from the session. |
| App content you compose, such as FAQs or products, by meaning | A named index: `sw.search.upsert({ index, items })` and `sw.search.query({ index, query })`. A named index is shared across the project, so your function decides what each caller may see. |
| Rows whose column contains some text | `sw.db.from(table, { where: { title: { contains: q } } })` in a function (**Data**) |
| Your project's source and text files, while developing | `sw.fs.dev.search`, developer `run_code` only, not in deployed functions |

