## Charge for one row (live only)

A checkout can pay for one row and mark it paid. The platform checks the
amount against the row and changes its status only after a verified payment.
Declare the binding in the schema options, beside the table:

```ts
schema(
  {
    orders: table(
      { id: id(), item: text(), amount_cents: integer(), currency: text(), status: text({ default: 'unpaid' }) },
      { scope: owner(), client: { read: true } },
    ),
  },
  {
    payments: {
      order: { table: 'orders', amount: 'amount_cents', currency: 'currency', onPaid: { status: { from: ['unpaid'], to: 'paid' } } },
    },
  },
);
```

Your functions create orders with the amount; the browser only reads them.
Start the checkout from the signed-in user's request:

```ts
// api/orders/pay.ts — POST { id }: live checkout for one of the caller's orders
export default async function payOrder(
  request: Request,
  sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext,
): Promise<Response> {
  if (request.method !== 'POST') {
    return new Response('Use POST.', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'POST' } });
  }
  if (!(await sw.auth.fromRequest(request))) {
    return Response.json({ error: 'Sign in first.' }, { status: 401 });
  }
  const body = (await request.json().catch(() => null)) as { id?: unknown } | null;
  if (typeof body?.id !== 'string' || body.id === '') {
    return Response.json({ error: 'Send the order id as a whole number.' }, { status: 400 });
  }
  const { data } = await sw.db.from('orders', { where: { id: body.id }, limit: 1 });
  const order = data[0] as { id: string; item: string; amount_cents: number; currency: string } | undefined;
  if (!order) return Response.json({ error: 'Order not found.' }, { status: 404 });

  const checkout = await sw.payments.checkout({
    row: { binding: 'order', id: order.id },
    env: 'prod',
    line_items: [{ name: order.item, amount: order.amount_cents, currency: order.currency }],
    // Your app's own address, including a custom domain, from the request it served.
    success_url: new URL('/orders?paid=1', request.url).href,
    cancel_url: new URL('/orders', request.url).href,
  });
  if ('url' in checkout && checkout.url) return Response.json({ url: checkout.url });
  return Response.json({ status: 'pending' }, { status: 202 });
}
```

- **Live payments only.** A row-bound checkout charges real money: it needs
  `env: 'prod'`, a deployed app and a completed payments onboarding. Test mode
  is refused with `ROW_TEST_TARGET_UNAVAILABLE`, and a preview or local run
  can't start one. Build and test your payment flow with an ordinary checkout
  (no `row`) in test mode, then add the binding for live orders.
- **The total must match the row.** The line items add up to the row's amount,
  in integer cents, in its currency; otherwise the checkout is refused with
  `ROW_TOTAL_MISMATCH`.
- **The status changes once, after payment.** A verified payment moves the
  status column from one of `from` to `to`, only if the amount and currency are
  unchanged. The amount, currency and status columns can't be in the browser's
  `client.create` or `client.update`, and the status column needs a default.
- **One open checkout per row.** The same request again returns the same
  session (`reused: true`); a different request while one is open is refused,
  and a paid row answers `ROW_ALREADY_PAID`. A result with `status: 'pending'`
  has no checkout URL yet; show the order as pending.
- **The signed-in user must be able to update the row** under its table's
  scope.
- **Return URLs stay on your app.** `success_url` and `cancel_url` must be on
  the project's own address or an attached custom domain; building them from
  `request.url` follows whichever of those served the request.

## Fees

The platform wires Stripe Connect for you. You get `payments_*` MCP
tools and `sw.payments` inside deployed functions. End-customers pay
through a Stripe Checkout Session and money settles to the developer's
connected account, minus Stripe's standard processing fee and a 0.5%
platform fee — each checkout response includes `fee_percent` so you can account for it.

## You DO NOT need to onboard to start building

Use a project in your Somewhere account for test-mode checkouts. Call payments_checkout (or
sw.payments.checkout) with env="dev" — when the developer hasn't
onboarded yet, the session runs directly on the platform's own Stripe
test account (no Connect destination, no application fee), so the test
charge with card 4242 4242 4242 4242 succeeds. A test purchase never
changes the user's live plan: gate test access on the signed
`subscription.*` notices or `payment.completed` your webhook receives
(see "What reaches your app in test and live" below). The response includes
`is_stand_in: true` and `platform_fee_cents: 0` so callers know
the fee won't apply on those sessions. Useful for dev demos,
integration tests, or showing the user the flow before they decide
to wire real bank info. Once the developer calls payments_onboard,
dev-mode checkouts use their own test connected account once it can
accept charges (still no live charges; same 4242 card).

`payments_status` explains this in `readiness`. It reads the owner's account, so it runs from MCP or the CLI, never from deployed app code:
On a normal live status read, `connected: true, onboarded: false` means
a live account exists but onboarding is unfinished. Live (`env: "prod"`) payments answer
`OWNER_NOT_ONBOARDED` until the owner finishes `payments_onboard`.
Test payments (`env: "dev"`) work without onboarding:
`readiness.test.account` says whether they run on your own test account
or on the platform's shared test account.

An agent calling the platform over MCP:

```text
payments_checkout({
  project_id: "my-store",
  env: "dev",
  mode: "payment",
  line_items: [{ amount: 4900, currency: "usd", name: "Premium plan" }],
  success_url: "https://my-store.somewhere.site/done",
  cancel_url:  "https://my-store.somewhere.site/cart"
})
```

```js
// Inside a deployed function — env is auto-detected from PROJECT_ENV.
// Use env="prod" for live charges (default once payments_onboard is done).
export default async function(req, sw) {
  const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(req);
  if (!user) return Response.json({ error: 'unauthorized' }, { status: 401 });

  const { url } = await sw.payments.checkoutForUser({
    plan: 'premium',
    mode: 'payment',
    line_items: [{ amount: 4900, currency: 'usd', name: 'Premium plan' }],
    success_url: new URL('/done', req.url).href,
    cancel_url: new URL('/cart', req.url).href
  })
  // url is null when no hosted checkout page was returned.
  if (!url) return Response.json({ error: 'checkout_unavailable' }, { status: 502 });
  return Response.redirect(url, 303)
}
```

For app-user entitlements, prefer checkoutForUser. The platform creates
a checkout-intent row and sends only that intent id through Stripe
metadata; webhooks derive app_user_id and plan from the platform
database. Do not pass browser-controlled metadata.app_user_id or
metadata.plan through a public handler.

## Quote-backed checkout

Use `sw.payments.quote` when the customer is buying a computed basket
(hotel nights, service fees, local/Stripe tax, promo discounts, deposit)
and checkout must use that immutable server-side snapshot instead of fresh
browser-supplied amounts.

```js
const quote = await sw.payments.quote(
  {
    id: 'cabin-7',
    name: 'Cabin 7',
    currency: 'usd',
    baseRate: { amount: 18000, unit: 'night' },
    fees: [
      { name: 'Cleaning fee', amount: 4500, kind: 'flat', taxable: true },
      { name: 'Guest fee', amount: 2000, kind: 'per_guest_per_night' },
    ],
    discounts: [{ code: 'WEEKLONG', percent: 10, min_nights: 7 }],
    tax: { rate_bps: 875 },
    deposit: { percent_bps: 2500, capture_method: 'manual' },
  },
  { start: '2026-08-01', end: '2026-08-04' },
  {
    guests: 2,
    promo: 'WEEKLONG',
    booking: {
      id: 'booking_123',              // optional; generated if omitted
      app_user_id: user.id,           // optional; must belong to this project
      external_id: 'PMS-42',
      metadata: { room: 'cabin-7' },
    },
  },
);
```

`resource.currency` is a 3-letter ISO code. `range` is date-only:
`{ start, end }`, `{ from, to }`, or `['YYYY-MM-DD', 'YYYY-MM-DD']`.
It must span 1 to 366 nights. Amounts are integer cents. Fee `kind` can
be `flat`, `per_night`, `per_guest`, or `per_guest_per_night`.
Discounts accept `amount`, `percent`, or `percent_bps`, plus optional
`min_nights` / `min_guests` and promo codes. `tax.rate_bps` computes
local tax; `tax.stripe: true` requires `opts.tax.customer_details` and
stores Stripe Tax's calculation id/breakdown on the quote.

The response is a 30-minute immutable quote snapshot. This three-night stay
falls short of the seven nights needed for the WEEKLONG discount.

```json
{
  "quote_id": "pqt_...",
  "booking_id": "booking_123",
  "expires_at": "2026-08-01T12:30:00.000Z",
  "resource": {
    "id": "cabin-7",
    "name": "Cabin 7"
  },
  "range": {
    "start": "2026-08-01",
    "end": "2026-08-04",
    "nights": 3
  },
  "guests": 2,
  "promo": "WEEKLONG",
  "currency": "usd",
  "subtotal": 70500,
  "taxes": {
    "amount": 6169,
    "source": "local",
    "calculation_id": null
  },
  "deposit": {
    "amount": 19167,
    "capture_method": "manual"
  },
  "total": 95836,
  "lineItems": [
    {"id": "base_18000", "kind": "base", "name": "Cabin 7 nightly rate", "amount": 54000, "currency": "usd", "quantity": 1, "taxable": true, "source": "base_rate"},
    {"id": "fee_1", "kind": "fee", "name": "Cleaning fee", "amount": 4500, "currency": "usd", "quantity": 1, "taxable": true, "source": "flat"},
    {"id": "fee_2", "kind": "fee", "name": "Guest fee", "amount": 12000, "currency": "usd", "quantity": 1, "taxable": true, "source": "per_guest_per_night"},
    {"id": "tax", "kind": "tax", "name": "Estimated tax", "amount": 6169, "currency": "usd", "quantity": 1, "taxable": false, "source": "local"},
    {"id": "deposit", "kind": "deposit", "name": "Deposit hold", "amount": 19167, "currency": "usd", "quantity": 1, "taxable": false, "source": "manual"}
  ],
  "discounts": []
}
```

Start checkout with the `quote_id`; do not pass `line_items` again.
The platform reloads the stored `lineItems`, verifies their total, and
atomically marks the quote used so two concurrent checkout calls cannot
create two Stripe sessions for one quote.

```js
const checkout = await sw.payments.checkout({
  env: 'prod',
  quote_id: quote.quote_id,
  booking_id: quote.booking_id,
  calendar_hold_token: hold.hold_token, // optional: compose quoted price + date hold
  success_url: 'https://my-app.somewhere.site/paid',
  cancel_url: 'https://my-app.somewhere.site/quote',
  customer_email: user.email,
});
// -> { session_id, url, amount_total_cents, platform_fee_cents,
//      fee_percent, stripe_mode, is_stand_in, checkout_intent_id,
//      quote_id, booking_id, capture_method, payment_intent_id }
```

Quote checkouts are one-time `mode: 'payment'` checkouts. They may include a
`calendar_hold_token`; existing quote-only and calendar-only checkouts keep
their prior behavior. For the combined flow, create the hold first and give it
at least 31 minutes of remaining TTL.

## Onboarding for live charges

Call payments_onboard when the developer is ready to take REAL money.
It's per-account (NOT per-project) — one onboard covers every project
the developer owns now or in the future. Returns a Stripe-hosted URL
for KYC + bank info.

```text
payments_onboard({
  return_url:  "https://my-store.somewhere.site/payments/done",
  refresh_url: "https://my-store.somewhere.site/payments/retry"
})
```

After the developer finishes onboarding, payments_status reports
charges_enabled=true and you can call payments_checkout({ env: "prod" }).

For your own test account, call the same tool with `mode: 'test'`. Test
onboarding is optional: dev-mode checkouts already work on the shared test
account. A ready test account returns `checkout_ready: true` and
`onboarding_url: null`; otherwise open the returned link. Both Payments
screens in the dashboard also offer **Test setup — no real charges**.
Omitting `mode` keeps live onboarding.

## Collaborators

Anyone with access to the project can run checkouts. Funds always go
to the project OWNER's connected account. If the owner has not
finished live onboarding and a collaborator calls
payments_checkout({ env: "prod" }), the platform returns 412 with code
OWNER_NOT_ONBOARDED and a message telling the caller the owner needs
to onboard first.

## What's NOT done for you

The user-facing pricing page, cart UI, and post-success receipts are
your code. Stripe Connect handles money + tax + payouts. You handle
"what does the customer click before they get to checkout".

## The moment payment succeeds — compose the built-ins

Do post-payment work from a trusted payment event/reconciliation handler,
not from the browser's success-page redirect. After the paid order or plan
state is durably recorded:

- Send the receipt or confirmation with `sw.email.send(...)` inside the
  handler, or `email_send(...)` from an agent/operator flow.
- Track `checkout_completed` with `sw.analytics.track(...)` or
  `analytics_track(...)`; track `checkout_started` when you create the
  session so the pair becomes a conversion funnel.
- Generate a downloadable invoice/receipt with `sw.render.pdf(...)` or
  `browser({ html, capture: { as: "pdf" } })` and store it in the project's files when it will be
  reused.
- Run generation or batch email in the background with
  `sw.jobs.create(...)` / `job_create(...)`, whether or not you read its
  status back.
- Update an open order screen by declaring the order read as a live view
  (`sw.rooms.live(name, sw.db.from('orders', …))`) — the write invalidates it
  and the browser re-reads. Notify a buyer whose tab is closed with
  `sw.push.send({ payload, user_id })` after they turn on notifications
  (`enablePush()` from `somewhere:push` — see the push topic).

Full setup and limits: `docs({ topic: 'sw.email' })`,
`docs({ topic: 'analytics' })`, `docs({ topic: 'render' })`,
`docs({ topic: 'sw.jobs' })`,
`docs({ topic: 'live' })`, and `docs({ topic: 'sw.push' })`.

## Webhook events — what each flow actually emits

The platform's own Stripe webhook (/v1/payments/webhook) is the receiver
for every flow below; you never register it yourself.

- **Connect status** — keeps charges_enabled / payouts_enabled in sync.
  Emits nothing to your app.
- **`checkoutForUser` plan tracking** — confirmed live checkout grants the
  selected plan; test grants stay separate. A granted one-time payment also
  emits `payment.completed` to the project webhook. A granted subscription
  emits signed `subscription.*` notices (below) when it starts and whenever its
  status, scheduled end or period end changes, in test and live. Unmatched or replaced paid checkouts
  are recorded for repair without emitting a successful plan grant.
- **Calendar checkout** — a completed quote checkout or a one-time checkout
  bound to a confirmed calendar hold emits `payment.completed` to the project webhook,
  including the reservation details (see the calendar section below).
- **Ad-hoc checkout without plan tracking, a quote or a calendar hold** — a
  settled one-time checkout emits `payment.completed` to the project
  webhook with `amount_total`, `currency`, `checkout_session_id`,
  `payment_intent_id`, `customer_id` and `mode`, plus the metadata you passed at
  checkout minus platform-reserved keys. It grants no plan and identifies no
  app user (`app_user_id` and `plan` are null): match
  `checkout_session_id`, amount, currency and test/live mode to the order you recorded
  when you created the checkout before fulfilling it.
- **Refunds of checkout-bound payments** — emit `payment.refunded` with
  the charge, payment intent, amount, cumulative refunded amount, currency and
  test/live mode. The original payment account and project owner must match.
  Recurring invoice refunds and older payments without that binding need your
  own Stripe event handler; they do not emit this callback.
- `payment_received`, `subscription_created` and
  `subscription_cancelled` are not webhook events: a subscription to them is
  refused and receives nothing; delete any existing subscription to them.

### Ad-hoc completion body

A settled ad-hoc one-time checkout sends this shape. The example values are
illustrative; nullable provider values can be null. The stable top-level
`id` identifies the event across retries. Fields and your metadata are
inside `payment`, not `data`; `mode` is `test` or `live`, not the
checkout kind `payment`.

```json
{
  "id": "pay_example",
  "type": "payment.completed",
  "payment": {
    "project_id": "your-project-id",
    "app_user_id": null,
    "plan": null,
    "amount_total": 1250,
    "currency": "usd",
    "checkout_session_id": "cs_test_example",
    "payment_intent_id": "pi_example",
    "customer_id": null,
    "mode": "test",
    "status": "completed",
    "completed_at": "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z",
    "calendar": null,
    "metadata": { "order_id": "order-123" }
  }
}
```

This is the ad-hoc flow above, not a closed schema for every checkout:
plan and calendar flows can carry their own fields. Check the recorded
session, amount, currency and mode before fulfilling the order.

For a copyable verifier, use [the existing lifecycle raw-body verifier](/docs/sw.auth#lifecycle-webhook-clean-up-your-own-tables-on-user-delete)
before parsing this body. Both deliveries use `X-Somewhere-Signature` with
`t=<milliseconds>,v1=<HMAC-SHA256>`, signing the exact UTF-8 bytes of
`${t}.${rawBody}` with the secret returned by `auth_webhook_set`.
Keep its timestamp window and Web Crypto signature check; do not reserialize
JSON before verification. After verification, handle `type === 'payment.completed'`
and read `payment`; the example lifecycle handler's auth branches do not
fulfill payments.

Set the project webhook URL with `auth_webhook_set` to receive these events,
and verify `X-Somewhere-Signature` on every delivery
([Webhook signatures](/docs/sw.auth#lifecycle-webhook-clean-up-your-own-tables-on-user-delete)).
`payment.completed` is sent only once the checkout is settled: a delayed
payment method sends it when the payment succeeds, and a failed one sends
nothing. Payment callbacks are saved before the provider event is acknowledged.
A temporary receiver failure is retried. While the original payment owner
still owns the project, deliveries can be inspected with
`webhook_deliveries_list` and retried with `webhook_delivery_redrive`.
Retries keep the same body and its top-level `id`; each attempt gets a fresh
signature timestamp. Use the payment signature format linked above, rather
than the bare signature used by webhook subscriptions.

Deliveries can repeat or arrive concurrently. In one database
transaction, insert a unique fulfillment record for `checkout_session_id`
and apply the order and stock changes; an existing record means those changes
already ran. On every valid delivery, including a duplicate, call
`sw.jobs.create` for follow-up work with the same `idempotency_key`
(for example, `'order-email:' + checkout_session_id`). This returns the
existing job or admits it if an earlier handler stopped before creating it.
Use your own metadata names; keys beginning with `somewhere_` are reserved
for platform bindings and can prevent an ad-hoc completion event.
A calendar checkout whose slot could not be
confirmed emits no completion event.
A new callback also requires the project to belong to the payment's recorded
original owner. If ownership changed or that owner is unknown, the callback
is withheld and recorded for review. Deliveries already recorded keep their
original destination and body, but pending callbacks tied to the previous
owner stop when the project changes owner. Withheld or stopped callbacks have
no automatic handoff to the new owner. The new owner cannot view their delivery
history or retry them, and the previous owner has no access to the project.

### Subscription notices

A subscription granted through `checkoutForUser` sends one signed notice per
applied change to the same webhook URL, with the same signature and retries
as `payment.completed`. A webhook URL needs its secret set for these to be
sent.

```json
{
  "id": "psub_example",
  "type": "subscription.updated",
  "subscription": {
    "project_id": "your-project-id",
    "app_user_id": "user-id",
    "subscription_id": "sub_example",
    "plan": "pro",
    "status": "active",
    "cancel_at_period_end": true,
    "current_period_end": "2026-11-08T18:21:12.000Z",
    "mode": "test",
    "grant_revision": 4
  }
}
```

- `subscription.activated` — the subscription started with access (`active`,
  `trialing` or `past_due`).
- `subscription.updated` — its status, `cancel_at_period_end` or
  `current_period_end` changed: a scheduled end, a renewal, a resumed plan,
  `past_due` while payment retries.
- `subscription.canceled` — it ended; access ends now.

Grant access while `status` is `active`, `trialing` or `past_due`. With
`cancel_at_period_end: true` the user keeps access until
`current_period_end`; remove access at that time even if delivery of the
`subscription.canceled` notice is delayed. Keep the highest `grant_revision` per project, app user and `mode`; ignore
an older revision that arrives late. Keep test access separate from live access.
The cancellation flag describes a period-end cancellation. A custom cancellation
date can leave it false; false alone does not mean the subscription will renew.
Custom cancellation dates are not yet included in this notice body.
An immediate cancellation through `sw.payments.cancelSubscription`
updates access at once; the provider's own event for that change sends no
second notice. Replacing a subscription with a new plan sends
`subscription.activated` for the new one and nothing more for the old one.

### What reaches your app in test and live

| | Test (`env: "dev"`) | Live (`env: "prod"`) |
|---|---|---|
| Onboarding needed | No | Yes (`payments_onboard`) |
| `payment.completed`, `payment.refunded` | Yes | Yes |
| `subscription.*` notices | Yes | Yes |
| User's live billing grant changes | No (test stays separate) | Yes |
| Billing portal (`portalForUser` with the same `env`) | Yes: cancel at period end, payment method | Yes |
| Current-user cancellation (`cancelForUser` with the same `env`) | Yes | Yes |

Pass the same `env` to `portalForUser`, `subscriptionForUser`,
`cancelForUser` and `transactions` as the checkout used; a deployed app
defaults to live. `cancelSubscription` is the trusted merchant operation
when your code already has an authorized subscription ID.

A second Stripe destination is not needed to receive `payment.completed`.
Add one for Stripe-native events outside the flows above, such as disputes
or recurring invoice refunds: register it in the developer's connected account, point it at one
of your deployed functions, and verify its signature with Stripe's own scheme:

### Verifying a second Stripe destination in your function

Stripe's signed content is three parts concatenated, nothing else: the
timestamp from the header in unix SECONDS, then a literal dot, then the exact
raw request body bytes — `${t}.${rawBody}`. The `stripe-signature`
header carries `t=<unix seconds>,v1=<hex>` (possibly several `v1`), and
`v1` is HMAC-SHA256 of that exact string with the endpoint's signing secret.
Milliseconds, a re-serialized body, or the body alone all produce a different
MAC.
Verification requires the exact raw request bytes, a freshness check on
`t` (Stripe's SDK default tolerance is 300 seconds) and a constant-time
compare; the SDK's `constructEventAsync` does all of that, and the platform
verifies its own webhook the same way. When a signature fails, diagnose the
endpoint secret (test vs live, the secret of THIS endpoint), the environment,
whether the body was re-serialized, and header parsing — a
timestamp-dot-body scheme is not evidence that the payload is "not Stripe".
Do not look for `X-Somewhere-Signature` here; that header belongs to the
platform's own flows (drain: `t=<ms>,v1=…` over `${ms}.${rawBody}`;
subscriptions: bare hex over the raw body). This example verifies and stops;
it does not fulfil anything.

A verified signature proves Stripe sent the event. Before fulfilling, match
the paid session to YOUR order: session id, amount, currency, live mode and
Stripe account, as the handler below does. The two files below are the shared, tested recipe
(`checkout-fulfillment`; the table name is its one parameter, shown as
`payment_requests`). The advisor renders the same bytes, and the platform's
fixtures run them against the real runtime and the real Stripe SDK. Use them
as they are; the checks they carry are the contract.

```ts
// db/schema.ts
import { schema, table, id, text, integer, boolean, timestamp, serverOnly } from 'somewhere/db';

export default schema({
  payment_requests: table({
    id: id({ uuid: true }),
    checkout_session_id: text({ unique: true }),
    amount_cents: integer(),
    currency: text(),
    livemode: boolean(),
    stripe_account_id: text({ nullable: true }),
    status: text({ default: 'pending' }),
    paid_event_id: text({ nullable: true }),
    paid_at: timestamp({ nullable: true }),
  }, { scope: serverOnly() }),
});
```

```ts
// api/payments/stripe.ts
import Stripe from 'stripe';

export default async function (req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext) {
  if (req.method !== 'POST') return new Response('method not allowed', { status: 405 });
  const raw = await req.text();
  const stripe = new Stripe(sw.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY);
  let event;
  try {
    event = await stripe.webhooks.constructEventAsync(
      raw, req.headers.get('stripe-signature') ?? '', sw.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET,
    );
  } catch {
    return new Response('bad signature', { status: 400 });
  }
  if (event.type !== 'checkout.session.completed') return new Response('ok');
  const session = event.data.object;
  if (session.mode !== 'payment' || session.payment_status !== 'paid' ||
      typeof session.id !== 'string' || !Number.isSafeInteger(session.amount_total) ||
      typeof session.currency !== 'string' || typeof session.livemode !== 'boolean' ||
      event.livemode !== session.livemode) return new Response('payment mismatch', { status: 409 });
  const found = await sw.db.from('payment_requests', { where: { checkout_session_id: session.id }, limit: 1 });
  const order = found.data[0];
  if (typeof event.id !== 'string' || (event.account !== undefined && typeof event.account !== 'string')) {
    return new Response('payment mismatch', { status: 409 });
  }
  const account = event.account ?? null;
  if (!order || order.checkout_session_id !== session.id ||
      order.amount_cents !== session.amount_total || order.currency !== session.currency ||
      ![true, false, 0, 1].includes(order.livemode) || Boolean(order.livemode) !== session.livemode || order.stripe_account_id !== account) {
    return new Response('payment mismatch', { status: 409 });
  }
  const where = {
    id: order.id, checkout_session_id: session.id, status: 'pending',
    amount_cents: order.amount_cents, currency: order.currency,
    livemode: order.livemode, stripe_account_id: account,
  };
  const updated = await sw.db.update('payment_requests', {
    set: { status: 'paid', paid_event_id: event.id, paid_at: new Date().toISOString() }, where,
  });
  if (updated.data.length === 1 && updated.data[0].id === order.id) return Response.json({ ok: true, transitioned: true });
  const current = (await sw.db.from('payment_requests', { where: { id: order.id }, limit: 1 })).data[0];
  if (current?.status === 'paid' && current.paid_event_id === event.id &&
      current.checkout_session_id === session.id && current.amount_cents === order.amount_cents &&
      current.currency === order.currency && current.livemode === order.livemode &&
      current.stripe_account_id === account) return Response.json({ ok: true, transitioned: false });
  return new Response('payment state unresolved', { status: 409 });
}
```

What the recipe enforces, and why each line is there:

- **This tested recipe's fulfilment table is declared `serverOnly()`.**
  Merge that table into your current `db/schema.ts` rather than replacing
  the file. A webhook has no signed-in user, so ordinary caller-scoped
  `sw.db.update` / `remove` cannot write another user's `owner()` row.
  On visitor-enabled tables, a caller-scoped conditional update may simply
  change zero rows. Use explicit server authority for the managed-row webhook
  write. Keep the managed scopes and client grants on your other tables. A webhook
  does not require converting them to `serverOnly()`: a function can use
  an explicitly authorized `sw.db.server` write on a managed table after
  verifying the event and matching it to the stored order. Use the tested
  recipe above unchanged for its receipt and fulfilment transition.
- **The stored order is the authority.** Session id, amount in minor units,
  lowercase currency, and `livemode` are recorded when the session is
  created and compared against both the event and the session. Mode is not a
  setting and not derived from a key prefix: the webhook is authenticated by
  its signing secret, and the order row says which mode it was created in.
- **Connect is a variant, not a check.** `stripe_account_id` is the
  connected account for Connect deliveries and `null` for a direct,
  account-specific endpoint, where `event.account` is absent. Its absence
  is not a failure.
- **Receipt and transition are ONE statement on the order row.** The
  composed `sw.db.tx` can commit several writes atomically, but a guarded
  update matching zero rows is a successful no-op: it does not abort sibling
  writes. A separate receipt insert therefore cannot use that update as its
  success condition. The
  guarded update binds id, session, status `pending` and every compared
  value; one returned row means this call fulfilled, none means re-read and
  classify: the same event already applied is a duplicate (200), anything
  else is unresolved (409). An events table written after the transition is
  fine as an audit log; it must never be the gate.
- **Limit:** side effects (email, PDF, stock) are outside the statement. A
  crash after the update and before the side effect is lost on retry, since
  the retry sees zero changes. Set a fulfilment marker in the same update and
  drain it from a scheduled job if that loss is unacceptable. On an unmanaged
  (SQL-mode) schema, `sw.db.batch` runs as one all-or-nothing batch.
- **Subscriptions** (`mode: 'subscription'`): treat
  `checkout.session.completed` as "subscription started" and take paid /
  unpaid state from `invoice.paid` / `invoice.payment_failed` for the
  subscription id, with the same single-row guard keyed on the invoice id.

## Automatic plan tracking on app_users

Want the current billing plan to become "premium" when the customer pays
and return to free when their paid access ends, without writing a webhook
handler? Use `checkoutForUser` from inside a deployed function.
The platform stores the verified user, price and payment account before
checkout, then verifies that record on completion. A browser cannot spoof
`app_user_id` or `plan`. Replace `price_PREMIUM_MONTHLY` with a recurring price
from your connected Stripe account. The pricing page posts to this handler and
opens the returned URL.

```ts
// api/subscribe.ts
export default async function subscribe(req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  if (req.method !== 'POST') {
    return new Response('Use POST.', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'POST' } });
  }
  const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(req);
  if (!user) return Response.json({ error: 'Sign in.' }, { status: 401 });

  const { url } = await sw.payments.checkoutForUser({
    plan: 'premium',
    mode: 'subscription',
    env: 'prod',
    line_items: [{ price: 'price_PREMIUM_MONTHLY', quantity: 1 }],
    success_url: new URL('/billing/success', req.url).href,
    cancel_url: new URL('/billing/cancel', req.url).href,
    customer_email: user.email,
  });
  if (!url) return Response.json({ error: 'Checkout is unavailable.' }, { status: 502 });
  return Response.json({ url });
}
```

The platform checks the selected checkout, original account and current
subscription before granting access. A pending replacement checkout keeps the
current paid grant. Older completions cannot replace a newer selection.

Read the effective live plan with `sw.billing.entitlements()`, which is current
on every call. Use it after checking sign-in.

```ts
// api/premium-content.ts
export default async function premiumContent(req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(req);
  if (!user) return new Response('Sign in', { status: 401 });
  const { plan } = await sw.billing.entitlements();
  if (plan !== 'premium') return new Response('Upgrade required', { status: 403 });
  return Response.json({ content: 'Welcome to Premium.' });
}
```

Active, trialing, canceling and past-due subscriptions retain access. Unpaid,
paused, canceled and expired subscriptions return `plan: "free"`.
Test-mode purchases never change this live plan. Historical payments without
a stored binding remain visible for reconciliation rather than granting access
from metadata.

## Manual-capture deposits

Set `deposit` to a cents number or use
`deposit: { amount: 5000, capture_method: 'manual' }` in the resource
passed to `sw.payments.quote`. Use `percent` or `percent_bps` for a percentage
instead. When a deposit is present, the capture method defaults to `'manual'`.

Manual deposits add a `kind: 'deposit'` line item and make quote checkout
create a manual-capture PaymentIntent. When Stripe reports
`checkout.session.completed`, the quote booking moves to `authorized`
instead of `completed`, and the project webhook receives
`payment.completed` with `capture_method: 'manual'`, `status:
'authorized'`, `quote_id`, `booking_id`, `payment_intent_id`, and the
quote/booking snapshots.

Capture or release the authorization exactly once from trusted server/operator
code using the REST endpoints. There are no dedicated `sw.payments.capture`
or MCP `payments_capture` helpers yet, so do not claim one exists.

```http
POST /v1/payments/capture
Authorization: Bearer smt_...
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "project_id": "my-app",
  "booking_id": "booking_123",
  "amount": 5000,
  "env": "prod"
}
```

`booking_id` or `payment_intent_id` is required. `amount` is optional;
when omitted, the platform captures the authorized deposit amount, not the
full quote total. If supplied, `amount` must be a positive integer cents
value and cannot exceed `deposit_cents`. The booking must be manual capture,
`authorized`, uncaptured, and unreleased. The response:

```json
{
  "ok": true,
  "data": {
    "booking_id": "booking_123",
    "quote_id": "pqt_...",
    "payment_intent_id": "pi_...",
    "status": "succeeded",
    "captured_cents": 5000,
    "stripe_mode": "live"
  }
}
```

Release cancels an uncaptured authorization:

```http
POST /v1/payments/release
Authorization: Bearer smt_...
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "project_id": "my-app",
  "booking_id": "booking_123",
  "env": "prod"
}
```

Response:

```json
{
  "ok": true,
  "data": {
    "booking_id": "booking_123",
    "quote_id": "pqt_...",
    "payment_intent_id": "pi_...",
    "status": "canceled",
    "stripe_mode": "live"
  }
}
```

Both API paths and Stripe webhook replays use conditional state transitions:
`authorized -> captured` or `authorized -> released` only, with
`captured_at IS NULL` and `released_at IS NULL`. A replay, double-click,
capture-after-release, or release-after-capture returns/settles as a state
conflict instead of charging twice.

## Refunds, cancels, transactions, portal, events

Five more endpoints round out the payments surface so you don't need
to drop down to the Stripe SDK or build a custom dashboard.

### Refund a charge

```js
// Inside a deployed function. Use the recorded payment_intent_id
// from the settled payment, and select the same environment as the charge.
const refund = await sw.payments.refund({
  env: 'dev', // Use 'prod' for a live charge.
  payment_intent_id: 'pi_3PqXYZ...',
  // amount: 1000,            // omit for full refund (in cents)
  // reason: 'requested_by_customer'
});
// → { refund_id, status, amount, currency, charge_id, ... }
```

Stripe's processing fees are handled by Stripe's standard refund policy.

### Cancel a subscription

```js
await sw.payments.cancelSubscription({ subscription_id: 'sub_...' });
// Default: cancel_at_period_end=true — customer keeps access until period end.

await sw.payments.cancelSubscription({ subscription_id: 'sub_...', immediately: true });
// Check cancellation and access_sync in the response before reporting success.
```

For a tracked subscription, the platform applies the provider-confirmed status
to access. A confirmed cancellation with `access_sync: 'pending'` means billing
stopped but the access update needs repair; do not cancel again. Historical
subscriptions without a stored payment binding require reconciliation.

### List recent transactions

```js
const { transactions, next_cursor } = await sw.payments.transactions({ limit: 20 });
// → transactions: [{ id, amount, currency, status, paid, refunded,
//                    created, customer_id, payment_intent_id, ... }]
// Paginate with: sw.payments.transactions({ limit: 20, starting_after: next_cursor })
```

### Open the Stripe Billing Portal for an end-customer

The portal lets customers update their card, switch plans, view
invoices, and cancel themselves — no support ticket required.

```ts
// api/billing-portal.ts — the page POSTs here from its "Manage billing" button
export default async function billingPortal(
  request: Request,
  sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext,
): Promise<Response> {
  if (request.method !== 'POST') {
    return new Response('Use POST.', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'POST' } });
  }
  const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(request);
  if (!user) return Response.json({ error: 'Sign in.' }, { status: 401 });

  // No user id argument: the runtime derives the signed-in user itself.
  const { url } = await sw.payments.portalForUser({
    return_url: new URL('/account/billing', request.url).href,
  });
  return Response.redirect(url, 303);
}
```

`portalForUser({ return_url, env })` opens billing for the signed-in user.
The runtime supplies verified user proof; the platform selects that user's
stored customer. Never accept `customer_id` from a browser body or URL.
Pass the same `env` used at checkout: `"dev"` for test payments,
`"prod"` for live payments.

### Show or cancel the signed-in user's subscription

In a signed-in function, read the user's plan subscription with
`sw.payments.subscriptionForUser({ env })`, using the same `env` as checkout
(`"dev"` for test, `"prod"` for live). It returns the plan, status, access,
period end and whether it is set to cancel at the end of the period. A user
without a subscription gets `NO_SUBSCRIPTION`.

For a confirmed cancellation action, call
`sw.payments.cancelForUser({ env })` from a POST handler. The default
keeps access until the period ends; `immediately: true` cancels now. Neither
method takes a user, customer or subscription ID: the verified session selects
whose subscription is read or changed.

Check the cancellation result: `cancellation: "confirmed"` with
`access_sync: "applied"` means billing and access are updated.
`access_sync: "pending"` means billing changed and access repair is pending;
do not send another cancellation. `already_canceled` means no provider call
was needed. For `not_confirmed` or `not_applied`, read the current subscription
before acting; do not loop cancellations.

The call throws `PAYMENTS_CANCEL_OUTCOME_UNKNOWN` when the provider gives no
definite answer. The stored status updates when its event arrives, so check
again later instead of immediately retrying cancellation.

Apps deployed before this change must be deployed again: until then,
`portalForUser` is refused and the new helpers are unavailable.

`sw.payments.portal({ customer_id, ... })` still exists for trusted
server/operator flows where your code already loaded the customer id from
your own database and checked that the caller may manage that customer. Do
not use it for "current signed-in user" account pages.

(Stripe auto-creates a default portal configuration on first use.
Advanced settings — what plans are switchable, custom branding —
need a one-time click in the connected account's Stripe dashboard.)

### Read the webhook event ledger

Every Stripe event the platform's webhook receives is persisted in a
ledger you can read back. The handler is idempotent — Stripe
redeliveries (which happen on transport failures) appear exactly
once. Use this to drive an in-product activity feed or to verify
delivery during integration.

```js
const { events, next_cursor } = await sw.payments.events({
  limit: 50,
  // type: 'checkout.session.completed',   // optional filter
  // before: 1715000000000,                // ms-epoch cursor
});
// → events: [{ id, type, mode, account_id, project_id,
//              amount_cents, currency, livemode, received_at }]
```

### One-time vs subscription

`payments_checkout` accepts `mode: 'payment'` (one-time charge)
or `mode: 'subscription'` (recurring billing). Same endpoint, same
automatic plan-tracking metadata. Default is
`'payment'`.

### Gating features after payment

Payments collect the money; `sw.billing` turns a paid plan into feature
yes/no answers. Pair them: `checkoutForUser({ plan: 'pro' })` sets the
user's plan on success, then `await sw.billing.has('export')` authorizes the
server operation. The browser can present an explicit billing observation
with `<Gate billing={billing} feature="export">` — no webhook code. See `docs({ topic:
'sw.billing' })`.

Call public HTTP and HTTPS APIs from server code with the standard global
`fetch`. In a deployed function the runtime wraps it with the platform's
outbound protections, and it returns a standard `Response`. `sw.fetch` is the
same function under a second name: same arguments, same `Response`.
Preview functions block outbound requests. Browser calls to your own functions
use same-origin `fetch('/api/...')`.

```ts
// api/weather.ts
export default async function (_req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  // The warning is awaited so it is sent before the function returns. A refused
  // log write throws (the daily log quota, for example); report that on the
  // console instead of letting it turn this 502 into a 500.
  const unavailable = async (message: string, data: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<Response> => {
    try {
      await sw.logs.warn(message, data);
    } catch (logError) {
      console.error('Weather warning was not logged', logError);
    }
    return Response.json({ error: 'Weather is temporarily unavailable' }, { status: 502 });
  };

  let response: Response;
  try {
    response = await fetch('https://api.example.com/weather?city=Oakland', {
      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${sw.env.WEATHER_API_KEY}` },
      signal: AbortSignal.timeout(8_000), // this call's own limit
    });
  } catch (err) {
    // No response at all: a refused destination, a failed lookup, a timeout or a network error.
    return unavailable('Weather request failed', { error: err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err) });
  }

  if (!response.ok) {
    return unavailable('Weather provider refused', { status: response.status });
  }

  let weather: unknown;
  try {
    weather = await response.json();
  } catch {
    return unavailable('Weather provider returned a body that is not JSON', { status: response.status });
  }
  return Response.json({ weather });
}
```

## What a call returns

- A standard `Response`. A 4xx or 5xx answer is still a `Response`: check
  `response.ok` or `response.status`, then read the body once with
  `response.json()` or `response.text()`.
- The call rejects only when there is no response to return. Platform refusals
  carry an `error.code`, for example `SSRF_BLOCKED`, `DNS_LOOKUP_FAILED`,
  `FETCH_TIMEOUT` or `DRAFT_EGRESS_DENIED`. Redirect failures and other
  network failures reject with a `TypeError`. Aborting your own `signal`
  rejects with its reason.
- Nothing is retried automatically. Retry only a request that is safe to
  repeat, for example one the upstream deduplicates with an idempotency key.

The rest of this topic describes deployed functions. `run_code` returns a
standard `Response` too, through its own guarded fetch.

## Time limits and cancellation

- The runtime rejects a call with `FETCH_TIMEOUT` when the response headers
  do not arrive within 120 seconds. That limit ends when the headers
  arrive; it never cuts off a response body you are still reading.
- Pass your own `signal` for a shorter limit or to cancel a call. Aborting
  it after the headers arrive also cancels reading the body.
- A hostname lookup that exceeds 3 seconds rejects with `DNS_LOOKUP_FAILED`.

## Destination safety

Before sending, the runtime refuses a private, loopback, link-local or
cloud-metadata address, and a reserved name such as `localhost` or a
`.local` or `.internal` host (`SSRF_BLOCKED`). It resolves any other
hostname and refuses it with `SSRF_BLOCKED` when an address it resolves to
is in a blocked range; a name that does not resolve to a public address
rejects with `DNS_LOOKUP_FAILED`. The connection then goes to an address the
platform checked, so a DNS change after the check cannot redirect it.
Resolution is checked per request, not continuously. A preview function cannot
make outbound requests at all (`DRAFT_EGRESS_DENIED`).

Behind the runtime, the platform's outbound guard refuses a request that
reaches it with a literal blocked target — a non-public address or internal
hostname, a URL with embedded `user:password`, a scheme other than
`http:`/`https:`, or a port other than 80, 443 or 1024 and above — with
a 403 carrying `x-sw-outbound-error`, before it leaves the platform. A
request the guard allows passes through unchanged. The guard classifies the
URL as written; it does not resolve names. Put credentials in an
`Authorization` header, never in the URL.

## Redirects

- By default (`redirect: 'follow'`) a 301, 302, 303, 307 or 308 is followed,
  and each new destination is checked exactly like the first, up to the
  limit of 20 redirects. Past the limit the call rejects with a
  `TypeError`.
- A hop to another origin drops the `Authorization`, `Cookie` and
  `Proxy-Authorization` headers.
- A 303 after any method other than GET or HEAD, and a 301 or 302 after a
  POST, continue as a GET without the body. Other redirects resend the body;
  a streaming (`ReadableStream`) body cannot be resent, so the call rejects
  with a `TypeError`.
- `redirect: 'manual'` returns the 3xx `Response` to you;
  `redirect: 'error'` rejects with a `TypeError`.

## Telemetry

The runtime sends a record to project logs with the method, host, status or
error code, duration and redirect count. It leaves out the full URL, headers
and body. Logging failures do not block the request.

Realtime in an app is one surface, rooms (`docs({ topic: 'rooms' })`):

- a room is one row that people change together (shared state, who is here,
  quick messages);
- a live list keeps a list of rows fresh in every open tab.

There is no caller-named channel API: nothing publishes or broadcasts on a
channel you pick. Application state and events belong in the database.

## Live lists

Declare the list in a function with `sw.rooms.live(name, sw.db.from(...))`
and follow it with `watchLive` from `/__sw/rooms/client.js`. Each change to
the list's rows sends a "refresh now" notice, and the browser re-calls your
function for fresh rows, so every access rule applies. No row, SQL, table
name, predicate or owner travels on the connection.

| Table rule | Who can follow the list |
|---|---|
| `owner()` | the signed-in user, for their own rows; with `owner({ visitors: true })`, an anonymous visitor for their own rows |
| `shared()`, `member()`, `group()`, `appRole()` | signed-in users who may read the rows |
| `serverOnly()` | nobody: deploy refuses the list |

The list below reads an `owner()` table, so each refresh returns only the
signed-in user's notes:

```ts
// db/schema.ts
import { schema, table, id, text, boolean, timestamp, owner } from 'somewhere/db'

export default schema({
  notes: table({
    id: id(), title: text(), done: boolean({ default: false }),
    created_at: timestamp({ default: 'now' }),
  }, { scope: owner() }),
})
```

```ts
// api/open-notes.ts
export default async function (_req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext) {
  return Response.json(await sw.rooms.live('notes.open', sw.db.from('notes', {
    where: { done: false },
    columns: ['id', 'title', 'done', 'created_at'],
    order: [['created_at', 'desc'], ['id', 'asc']],
    limit: 20,
  })))
}
```

```js
// browser
import { watchLive } from '/__sw/rooms/client.js'

const stop = watchLive(
  () => fetch('/api/open-notes', { credentials: 'include' }).then((r) => {
    if (!r.ok) throw new Error('Could not load notes (' + r.status + ').')
    return r.json()
  }),
  (rows) => render(rows),
  (err) => showProblem(err.message),   // optional; console.error otherwise
)
```

Anonymous visitors sharing one row (a game between two people with links, a
public board) use a room on that row instead of a list.

The declaration rules, what triggers a refresh, recovery after a dropped
connection, and `live_delivery` are in `docs({ topic: 'rooms' })` under
"Live lists".

## Platform system events

Two reserved, signed channels carry platform lifecycle events to developer
tooling. They are subscribe-only, developer-authority only, and carry no
application data — an app-user or visitor session is never admitted:

  events_subscribe_project({ project_id: 'my-app' })
  // → { channel: 'system:project', websocket_url, event_types: [...] }

  events_subscribe_user({})
  // → { channel: 'system:user', websocket_url, event_types: ['feedback_resolved'] }

The `system:project` channel emits deploy lifecycle, database health, quota
warning, and auth administration events. The `system:user` channel emits
`feedback_resolved` for tickets submitted by the authenticated developer.
The returned WebSocket URLs authenticate as that developer; keep them private.
On close code 4001 (`reauthorize`), reconnect with `since=<last received seq>`
so the new connection checks your current access and resumes available events.
A replay gap means you should reload the current state before continuing.

Use `browser` to capture a page or render HTML into a PDF. Use
`sw.render.pdf` when generating a PDF from app code.

```json
{
  "html": "<h1>Invoice #123</h1>",
  "capture": { "as": "pdf", "paper": "Letter", "print_background": true }
}
```

The PDF defaults to Letter, portrait, with backgrounds rendered. Use
`capture.paper` for `A4`, `A3`, `Letter`, `Legal`, or `Tabloid`.
Use `capture.landscape: true` to rotate the page.

Pass `storage` with `project_id` to save the PDF to project files.
For a PDF artifact response from a URL or HTML, call
`POST /v1/render/pdf` with `url` or `html`, and optionally `format`
for paper size, `landscape`, `print_background`, `wait_for`,
`project_id`, and `storage`. Use `somewhere api POST /v1/render/pdf
--data-file request.json --raw` to stream the response to a file.
For image artifacts use `POST /v1/render/screenshot` with `url` or
`html` and optionally `format`, `width`, `height`, `quality`,
`full_page`, `wait_for`, `project_id`, and `storage`.

For private documents, use the signed-link pattern below. The full job
(generate, store privately, preview in the page, merge and split) is
covered by `recipe-pdfs`.

## Common pattern

Generate a PDF invoice on demand for a signed-in user. Renders are metered,
so the caller is checked before the render. Replace `invoiceHtml` with your
own lookup that only returns invoices belonging to the signed-in user:

```ts
// api/invoice-preview.ts — the page posts { invoiceId }
const escapeHtml = (text: string) => text.replace(/[&<>"']/g, (c) => `&#${c.charCodeAt(0)};`);
const invoiceHtml = (invoiceId: string) => `<h1>Invoice ${escapeHtml(invoiceId)}</h1>`;

export default async function(req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext) {
  if (req.method !== 'POST') return new Response('Use POST.', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'POST' } });
  const user = await sw.auth.requireUser(req);
  const input: unknown = await req.json().catch(() => null);
  const invoiceId = typeof input === 'object' && input !== null && 'invoiceId' in input ? input.invoiceId : undefined;
  if (typeof invoiceId !== 'string' || !/^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,64}$/.test(invoiceId)) {
    return Response.json({ error: 'invoiceId must be letters, digits, - or _.' }, { status: 400 });
  }
  // This app allows each user 20 invoice renders per hour.
  const limit = await sw.rateLimit.check(`invoice-pdf:${user.id}`, 20, 3600);
  if (!limit.allowed) return Response.json({ error: 'Try again later.' }, { status: 429 });
  // sw.render.pdf returns a raw Response when no storage path is set —
  // pipe it straight to the client.
  return sw.render.pdf({ html: invoiceHtml(invoiceId), format: 'Letter' });
}
```

Or keep it as a private file and share a link that expires:

```ts
// api/invoice-link.ts — the page posts { invoiceId }; replace invoiceHtml with your own authorized invoice lookup
const escapeHtml = (text: string) => text.replace(/[&<>"']/g, (c) => `&#${c.charCodeAt(0)};`);
const invoiceHtml = (invoiceId: string) => `<h1>Invoice ${escapeHtml(invoiceId)}</h1>`;

export default async function(req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext) {
  if (req.method !== 'POST') return new Response('Use POST.', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'POST' } });
  const user = await sw.auth.requireUser(req);
  const input: unknown = await req.json().catch(() => null);
  const invoiceId = typeof input === 'object' && input !== null && 'invoiceId' in input ? input.invoiceId : undefined;
  // The id becomes part of a file path, so allow only plain id characters.
  if (typeof invoiceId !== 'string' || !/^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,64}$/.test(invoiceId)) {
    return Response.json({ error: 'invoiceId must be letters, digits, - or _.' }, { status: 400 });
  }
  // This app allows each user 20 invoice renders per hour.
  const limit = await sw.rateLimit.check(`invoice-pdf:${user.id}`, 20, 3600);
  if (!limit.allowed) return Response.json({ error: 'Try again later.' }, { status: 429 });
  const pdf = await sw.render.pdf({ html: invoiceHtml(invoiceId), format: 'Letter' });
  const path = `/invoices/${user.id}/${invoiceId}.pdf`;
  await sw.fs.server.write(path, await pdf.arrayBuffer(), { contentType: 'application/pdf' });
  const link = await sw.fs.server.signedUrl(path, { expiresIn: 3600 });
  return Response.json({ url: link.url });
}
```

A new file written with `sw.fs.server.write` is private unless you pass
`visibility: 'public'`; rewriting a file keeps its current visibility.

## Limits
- The page must finish loading within 30 seconds.
- `html` is at most 1,000,000 characters; `url` at most 2,048 characters.
- `wait_for` waits up to 5 seconds for its selector.
- Renders are metered and charged to the account balance.

## What NOT to do
- Don't pass a user-supplied `url` without your own allowlist. The renderer
  refuses private and internal addresses, and prints any public page it is
  given.
- Don't render on every page load — store the PDF once and serve the file.

Available inside any deployed function via the sw argument
provided to the handler.

sw.web fetches public web pages and returns their content as clean
markdown. Use it when an agent or feature needs to ingest content from
a URL it doesn't host — docs, blog posts, marketing pages, articles.
The platform makes the request, so your function needs no provider key.

## sw.web.scrape(url, opts?)

Fetch a URL and return its content as markdown by default. Strips
nav/footer/sidebar so you get the main article body.

```ts
// api/read-guide.ts — POST /api/read-guide
export default async (req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> => {
  if (req.method !== 'POST') return new Response('Method not allowed', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'POST' } })
  await sw.auth.requireUser(req)
  const page = await sw.web.scrape('https://somewhere.cloud/docs/deploy')
  return Response.json({
    url: page.url, title: page.title, status_code: page.status_code,
    markdown: page.markdown ?? '',
  })
}
```

`status_code` is the page's own HTTP status. An error page's content comes
back like any other page's, so check `status_code` before using the content.

opts:
  formats?    — array of: markdown | html | rawHtml | links | screenshot
                Default: ['markdown'].
  only_main?  — strip nav/footer/sidebar (default true). Set false to
                keep the full DOM.
  wait_for?   — ms to wait for the page to settle (max 30000). Use for
                SPAs that hydrate after initial load.

## sw.web.search(query, opts?)

Search the public web by keyword. Returns ranked result URLs +
titles + snippets — no page bodies. Pair with `sw.web.scrape` when
you want the full content of a specific result.

```ts
// api/web-search.ts — POST /api/web-search
export default async (req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> => {
  if (req.method !== 'POST') return new Response('Method not allowed', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'POST' } })
  const user = await sw.auth.requireUser(req)
  // This app allows each user 10 web searches per hour.
  const limit = await sw.rateLimit.check(`web-search:${user.id}`, 10, 3600)
  if (!limit.allowed) return Response.json({ error: 'Too many searches. Try again later.' }, { status: 429 })
  const hits = await sw.web.search('react server components streaming', { count: 5 })
  // hits = {
  //   query: '...', count: 5,
  //   results: [
  //     { url: '...', title: '...', description: '...', age: '2 days ago', source: 'blog.example.dev' },
  //     ...
  //   ]
  // }
  return Response.json(hits.results.map((hit) => ({ url: hit.url, title: hit.title })))
}
```

opts:
  count?      — 1 to 20, default 10.
  country?    — 2-letter code to bias results, e.g. 'us', 'gb', 'de'.
  freshness?  — 'pd' (past day) | 'pw' (past week) | 'pm' (past month)
                | 'py' (past year).
  safesearch? — 'off' | 'moderate' | 'strict'. Default 'moderate'.

## When to use what

- Find URLs by keyword → `sw.web.search`. Returns titles + snippets.
- One page → `sw.web.scrape`. Returns markdown ready for an LLM prompt.
- Search, then scrape the top results. Each scrape succeeds or fails on its
  own, so collect them with `Promise.allSettled`: one failed page keeps its
  error code and the other pages keep their content.

```ts
// api/research.ts — POST /api/research?q=...
export default async (req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> => {
  if (req.method !== 'POST') return new Response('Method not allowed', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'POST' } })
  await sw.auth.requireUser(req)
  const question = new URL(req.url).searchParams.get('q')?.trim()
  if (!question) return Response.json({ error: 'q is required' }, { status: 400 })

  const hits = await sw.web.search(question, { count: 3 })
  const urls = hits.results.map((hit) => hit.url)
  const settled = await Promise.allSettled(urls.map((url) => sw.web.scrape(url)))

  const pages = settled.map((result, i) => {
    if (result.status === 'fulfilled') {
      const page = result.value
      return { url: urls[i], ok: true, status_code: page.status_code, title: page.title, markdown: page.markdown ?? '' }
    }
    const err = result.reason as { code?: string; status?: number } | undefined
    return { url: urls[i], ok: false, code: err?.code ?? 'SCRAPE_FAILED', status: err?.status ?? null }
  })
  return Response.json({ query: hits.query, pages })
}
```

The handler requires sign-in before making search or scrape calls. A failed
search still throws. Uncaught, the request answers 500 `FUNCTION_ERROR`,
and the project’s logs record the search error’s code.

- A whole site → not supported; scrape pages individually.

## Errors

A refused or failed call throws an Error with `.code` and `.status`.
Rate-limit errors also carry `.retry_after_ms` when the platform sets one.

VALIDATION_ERROR (400)        — a bad argument: the url or query is missing
                                or too long, the url is not a public http(s)
                                address, or an option has an invalid value.
RATE_LIMITED (429)            — the project's per-minute limit for scrape or
                                search, or the provider is still busy after
                                the platform's one retry. Wait and call again.
UPSTREAM_QUOTA (402)          — the platform's scrape or search allowance
                                with the provider is used up.
UPSTREAM_ERROR (502)          — the provider failed, answered with an error,
                                returned an unreadable result, or could not
                                be reached.
UPSTREAM_NOT_CONFIGURED (503) — scrape or search has no provider set up in
                                this environment.

Define the plans YOUR app sells and the features each one unlocks, then gate
anything with a single check. You gate on a stable feature name (`'export'`),
never on a plan name or a Stripe price — so renaming or re-pricing a plan never
touches your gate code. This is the entitlement layer that pairs with
`sw.payments` (which collects the money): a confirmed live plan checkout
sets the user's plan, and `sw.billing` turns that plan into yes/no answers.
Test purchases are recorded separately and do not unlock these live features.

(Looking for OUR pricing tiers — what somewhere.cloud charges YOU? That's
`docs({ topic: 'billing' })`. This topic is about the plans YOUR app offers
ITS users.)

## 1. Define your plans (in code, once)

```js
// from a protected setup path (below); idempotent, so re-run it when plans change
await sw.billing.definePlans([
  { slug: 'free', name: 'Free', features: [] },
  { slug: 'pro',  name: 'Pro', price_cents: 2000, interval: 'month',
    stripe_price_id: 'price_...',   // required for live checkout; omit for a test monthly or yearly plan
    features: ['export', 'api_access', { feature: 'seats', limit: 10 }] },
]);
```

- Any caller of `definePlans` replaces the catalog, so run it only from a
  path you control: `POST /v1/entitlements/plans` with your developer key
  (body `{ project_id, plans }`), or a function that first checks
  `await sw.auth.requireUser(req, { role: 'admin' })`. Never from a handler
  any visitor can call.
- The list becomes your catalog. An omitted plan is removed unless a user
  still has it; those plans are kept and returned in `retained_plan_slugs`
  with a warning. Repeating the same definition is safe.
- `slug` is what a user's plan is set to. `features` are free-form names you
  invent and gate on; a feature can carry an optional numeric `limit`.
- `price_cents` and `interval` describe your plan. In test mode, a monthly
  or yearly plan can use them at checkout without a Stripe price ID. Defining
  a plan creates no charge; `sw.payments` starts checkout.
- View the catalog with `GET /v1/entitlements/plans?project_id=<id>`. Define plans in your code.

## 2. Gate a feature

Server side, in a function:

```js
if (await sw.billing.has('export')) {
  // ...do the gated thing
}
const ent = await sw.billing.entitlements();
// ent = { plan: 'pro', plan_defined: true, features: ['export','api_access','seats'], limits: { seats: 10 } }
```

`has()` and `entitlements()` read the currently recorded live billing
grant on every call. Checkout completion affects access after platform payment
processing; cancellation may retain access until the paid period ends.

Client side, render an explicit observation fetched from your authenticated
function. The browser's sign-in user does not contain paid assertions.
A minimal server route is:

```ts
export default async function billing(req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext) {
  await sw.auth.requireUser(req);
  const { plan, features } = await sw.billing.entitlements();
  return Response.json({ plan, features }, { headers: { 'Cache-Control': 'no-store' } });
}
```

Your UI fetches that route with its session, starting in `loading`, changing
to `ready` only after a successful validated answer, and to `unavailable`
on failure. Discard observations when the signed-in account changes; cancel
or ignore late answers from the previous account. Refresh after checkout.
Do not turn a failed billing request into a free plan or an upgrade prompt.

```tsx
import { useEntitlements, Gate, type BillingObservation } from '@somewhere-cloud/sdk/react';

type ExportActions = {
  billing: BillingObservation;
  onExport: () => void;
  onUpgrade: () => void;
};

export function ExportButton({ billing, onExport, onUpgrade }: ExportActions) {
  const access = useEntitlements(billing);
  if (access.status === 'loading') return <button type="button" disabled>Checking access</button>;
  if (access.status === 'unavailable') return <p>Access could not be checked. Try again.</p>;
  return access.has('export')
    ? <button type="button" onClick={onExport}>Export</button>
    : <button type="button" onClick={onUpgrade}>Upgrade to export</button>;
}

export function ExportGate({ billing, onExport, onUpgrade }: ExportActions) {
  return (
    <Gate billing={billing} feature="export"
      pending={<p>Checking access</p>}
      unavailable={<p>Access could not be checked. Try again.</p>}
      fallback={<button type="button" onClick={onUpgrade}>Upgrade to export</button>}>
      <button type="button" onClick={onExport}>Export</button>
    </Gate>
  );
}
```

These helpers choose presentation only. The function performing the operation
must still enforce `await sw.billing.has('export')`. Passing no observation
to `useEntitlements` or `Gate`, or calling the old synchronous
`auth.billing.has()` / `auth.billing.entitlements()`, throws
`BILLING_EXPLICIT_CHECK_REQUIRED` with the server-check remedy. No helper
silently reads paid fields from sign-in identity or starts a billing request.

`auth.billing.plans()` returns the catalog for rendering a pricing page.

## 3. Drop-in pricing + manage UI (React)

Pricing and manage-subscription components use the @somewhere-cloud/sdk/auth
session. Pass the current plan from your explicit billing observation; wait
for that observation before offering a new subscription:

```tsx
// src/pages/BillingPage.tsx
import { PricingTable, BillingPortal } from '@somewhere-cloud/sdk/react';

export function BillingPage({ currentPlan }: { currentPlan: string }) {
  return (
    <>
      {/* Your catalog plans, current plan highlighted; Subscribe → checkout */}
      <PricingTable currentPlan={currentPlan} />
      {/* One-click "manage/cancel" via the Stripe portal */}
      <BillingPortal />
    </>
  );
}
```

Both are logic-only/unstyled — theme with the `sw-*` class hooks
(`sw-pricing-table`, `sw-plan[data-current]`, `sw-plan-name/-price/-features/-cta`,
`sw-billing-portal`). Prefer to wire it yourself? The same actions are on the
client: `auth.billing.subscribe(planSlug)` and `auth.billing.openBillingPortal()`
(both redirect to Stripe; success/cancel/return default to the current page).

Subscribe checks out for the SIGNED-IN user (resolved server-side from the
session, never a client-supplied id) and the platform resolves the plan's price
from your active catalog. In test mode, define a plan with `price_cents`
and `interval: 'month'` or `'year'`; checkout creates the recurring test
price for you, including on the shared test account. An existing
`stripe_price_id` is used directly. For live checkout, set that ID to a
price on your connected Stripe account.

## 4. How a user gets a plan

You don't write webhook code. `sw.payments.checkoutForUser({ plan })`
(what `<PricingTable>` calls under the hood) starts a Checkout Session; when
a live payment is confirmed, the platform grants the selected plan and
`sw.billing` reflects it. Payment retries retain access. Scheduled cancellation
retains access until the subscription ends; unpaid, paused, canceled and expired
subscriptions have no paid access. Test checkout never changes live access.
(`docs({ topic: 'payments' })` covers the checkout side.)

## Notes

- For the current plan name, use `(await sw.billing.entitlements()).plan`
  in an authenticated function. Sign-in identity has no billing fields.
- A browser observation can become stale after it is fetched. It is for
  presentation; paid operations must check billing on the server.
- REST (if you're not in a function): `POST /v1/entitlements/plans` (define,
  developer key), `GET /v1/entitlements/plans` (catalog), `GET
  /v1/entitlements/me`, `GET /v1/entitlements/has?feature=…`.

Related topics: `payments`, `auth-client`, `sw.auth`.

Owners sign in and manage their own rows; a public form creates a row for an
owner and emails them; a daily job reminds owners of open rows. Shapes are
exact; names are yours to change. Every TypeScript file below passes
`somewhere typecheck` in a `somewhere init` project.

## 1. Start and sign in

Ask before destructive or paid actions unless the user already authorized them.

`somewhere init` in an empty directory: a React + TypeScript starter that
already signs users up and in. `api/auth/[...path].ts` is one line —
`export { somewhereAuth as default } from '@somewhere-cloud/sdk/server'` — and
`src/services/auth.ts` is the SDK client (`createSomewhereAuth()` from
`@somewhere-cloud/sdk/auth`). It has no email or cron code; the steps below
add them.
`POST /api/auth/signup` / `login` → `{ user: { id, email, role, display_name } }`
(for an account with email sign-in codes, login returns `{ mfa_required: true, mfa_token }`
and no session until the emailed code is entered: `sw.auth` → `email-sign-in-codes`); `GET /api/auth/me`
→ `{ user }` (`user: null` when signed out); errors are 4xx `{ error, message }`. In a function,
`await sw.auth.fromRequest(req)` → the user object itself, or `null`.

## 2. db/schema.ts

```ts
// db/schema.ts
import { schema, table, id, text, integer, owner } from 'somewhere/db';

export default schema({
  profiles: table(
    { id: id(), handle: text({ unique: true }), owner_email: text() },
    { scope: owner() },
  ),
  entries: table(
    {
      id: id(), title: text(), from_email: text(), owner_email: text(), status: text({ default: 'open' }),
      notify_status: text({ default: 'pending' }), notify_error: text({ nullable: true }),
      created_ms: integer(), reminded_ms: integer({ nullable: true }),
      reminder_claimed_ms: integer({ nullable: true }), reminder_status: text({ nullable: true }),
    },
    {
      scope: owner(),
      indexes: [['status']],
      client: { read: ['id', 'title', 'from_email', 'status', 'notify_status', 'created_ms'], update: ['status'] },
    },
  ),
});
```

`owner()` adds and scopes a `user_id` column. The `entries` grant lets each
signed-in owner read those columns of their own rows and change `status`
through the generated client. It grants no create or delete, so a new entry
arrives only through the public form in step 4. `profiles` has no `client`
block, so the browser reaches it only through your functions. `somewhere typecheck`
types the whole runtime context (`SomewhereRuntimeContext`, including
`sw.email`, `sw.jobs` and `sw.logs`) and each declared table's rows from
this file, so the functions below need no hand-written types.

## 3. Owner pages (signed in)

The owner's page calls the generated client. Each call is scoped to the
signed-in owner, so it sends no user filter and needs no API file:

```ts
// src/services/entries.ts
import { data } from 'somewhere:data';

export async function listEntries() {
  const page = await data.entries.list({ limit: 50 });
  return page.data;
}

export async function markDone(id: string) {
  const { data: entry } = await data.entries.update(id, { status: 'done' });
  return entry;
}
```

`update` returns `{ data: row, count, changes }`; a missing row and another owner's
row both throw `DataError` 404 `DATA_NOT_FOUND`.

Claiming a handle is how an owner opts into the public form: the handle is the
public address visitors submit to, and an owner without one receives nothing.
It stays a function, because it stores the verified session's email:
`sw.db.insert('profiles', { handle, owner_email: user.email }, { onConflict: 'ignore' })`
(`user` from `fromRequest`, none → 401 `AUTH_REQUIRED`); `changes === 0`
means it is taken → 409.

## 4. Public form: save first, then notify

The form addresses an owner by the handle they claimed in step 3. The function
looks the handle up and stores that owner's `user_id`; it never takes an owner
from the request.

```ts
// api/submit.ts — POST { handle, title, from_email } from a public page
export default sw.endpoint({
  auth: 'none', // the handler applies its own access check below
  handler: async ({ request: req }, sw) => {
    const body = ((await req.json().catch(() => null)) ?? {}) as { handle?: unknown; title?: unknown; from_email?: unknown };
    const { handle, title, from_email } = body;
    if (typeof handle !== 'string' || typeof from_email !== 'string' || typeof title !== 'string'
        || !title.trim() || title.length > 200 || /[\r\n]/.test(title)
        || from_email.length > 254 || !/^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+$/.test(from_email)) return Response.json({ error: 'bad input' }, { status: 400 });
    // Public and unauthenticated: cap each sender (the platform-set client IP), then each owner's inbox.
    const ip = req.headers.get('cf-connecting-ip') ?? 'unknown';
    const perSender = await sw.rateLimit.check(`submit-ip:${ip}`, 5, 600);
    if (!perSender.allowed) return Response.json({ error: 'too many submissions', retry_at: perSender.reset }, { status: 429 });
    const { data: [profile] } = await sw.db.server.from('profiles', { where: { handle }, limit: 1 });
    if (!profile) return Response.json({ error: 'unknown handle' }, { status: 404 });
    const perOwner = await sw.rateLimit.check(`submit-to:${profile.id}`, 50, 86_400);
    if (!perOwner.allowed) return Response.json({ error: 'too many submissions', retry_at: perOwner.reset }, { status: 429 });
    // Server authority writes a row for someone else: name the owner column.
    const saved = await sw.db.server.insert('entries', {
      user_id: profile.user_id, owner_email: profile.owner_email, title: title.trim(), from_email, created_ms: Date.now(),
    });
    const entry = saved.data[0];
    try {
      const job = await sw.jobs.create({
        handler: '/api/jobs/notify-entry',
        payload: { entry_id: entry.id },
        idempotency_key: `notify-entry-${entry.id}`,
      });
      // Job admission is awaited; the email is sent later by the signed handler.
      await sw.logs.info('entry notification queued', { entry_id: entry.id, job_id: job.job_id });
      return Response.json({ id: entry.id, received: true, notification: job.status }, { status: 201 });
    } catch (error) {
      const code = error instanceof Error && 'code' in error ? String(error.code) : 'JOB_ADMISSION_UNCONFIRMED';
      const status = error instanceof Error && 'status' in error ? Number(error.status) : 0;
      // A 4xx refusal (for example JOB_QUOTA_EXCEEDED or RATE_LIMITED) means no job
      // exists: record it on the row so the owner sees it. Any other failure may
      // still have admitted the job, so the row stays pending for its handler.
      const refused = status >= 400 && status < 500;
      await sw.db.server.update('entries', {
        set: refused ? { notify_status: 'not_queued', notify_error: code } : { notify_error: code },
        where: { id: entry.id },
      });
      await sw.logs.warn('entry notification not admitted', { entry_id: entry.id, code, refused });
      // The entry is already saved. Never ask the visitor to submit it again.
      return Response.json({ id: entry.id, received: true, notification: refused ? 'not_queued' : 'unconfirmed' }, { status: 201 });
    }
  },
});
```

Show **Saved; notification queued** only for `notification: 'queued'`.
Show **Saved; owner not notified** for `notification: 'not_queued'`. For any
other status, such as `indeterminate`, or `unconfirmed`, show
**Saved; notification not confirmed**. None of these means sent or delivered.
A burst of submissions can reach the account's job limits
(`JOB_QUOTA_EXCEEDED` or `RATE_LIMITED`, `sw.jobs` → `job-limits`). That refusal
is recorded on the saved entry as `notify_status: 'not_queued'` with its code in
`notify_error`, so the owner's list shows which entries were never notified.
An unconfirmed admission keeps `notify_status: 'pending'` and records the code,
because the job may still run. Keep the saved id and inspect the entry;
do not resubmit the form or replace its stable key after an unknown outcome.
The signed handler below observes the email result separately:

```ts
// api/jobs/notify-entry.ts
export default async function (req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  if (!(await sw.jobs.verifyInvocation(req))) return new Response('forbidden', { status: 403 });
  const input: unknown = await req.json().catch(() => null);
  const entryId = typeof input === 'object' && input !== null && 'entry_id' in input ? input.entry_id : null;
  if (typeof entryId !== 'string' || entryId === '') {
    return Response.json({ error: 'bad entry id' }, { status: 400 });
  }
  const { data: [entry] } = await sw.db.server.from('entries', { where: { id: entryId }, limit: 1 });
  if (!entry) return Response.json({ error: 'entry not found' }, { status: 404 });
  // A recorded outcome ends retries; an unknown send is not permission to send again.
  if (entry.notify_status !== 'pending') return Response.json({ status: entry.notify_status });
  let notify_status = 'unknown';
  let notify_error: string | null = null;
  try {
    const sent = await sw.email.send({
      to: entry.owner_email,
      subject: `New entry: ${entry.title}`,
      text: `${entry.title}\nFrom: ${entry.from_email}`,
      idempotency_key: `entry-${entry.id}`,
    });
    notify_status = sent.status;
  } catch (error) {
    const code = error instanceof Error && 'code' in error ? String(error.code) : 'EMAIL_FAILED';
    const status = error instanceof Error && 'status' in error ? Number(error.status) : 0;
    notify_status = status === 422 || status === 429 ? 'failed' : 'unknown';
    notify_error = code;
  }
  await sw.db.server.update('entries', { set: { notify_status, notify_error }, where: { id: entry.id } });
  await sw.logs.info('entry notification outcome', { entry_id: entry.id, status: notify_status, code: notify_error });
  return Response.json({ status: notify_status });
}
```

The recipient comes from the saved row, never the delivery payload. Keep the same email key on redelivery; inspect unknown results rather than
issuing a new key. Confirmed provider acceptance is `sent`, not delivery;
`captured` reaches only the controlled test inbox. `pending`,
`delivery_unknown` and `unknown` require inspection, not an automatic resend.
The row and project logs retain the outcome; a job whose runs keep failing
ends `failed` with its `error_code` (`sw.jobs`). The two rate limits still
protect submission and notification admission. The daily reminder below is separate.

## 5. Daily reminder (every plan)

Run this reminder on any plan. Set an IANA time zone when creating the
schedule, as shown below, or omit it to use UTC.

```ts
// api/jobs/remind.ts — called by the scheduler
export default async function (req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  if (!(await sw.jobs.verifyInvocation(req))) return new Response('forbidden', { status: 403 });
  const eligible = { status: 'open', reminder_claimed_ms: null, reminded_ms: null,
    created_ms: { lt: Date.now() - 86_400_000 } };
  // Server reads have their own limit; generated browser list() pages allow at most 100.
  const { data: due } = await sw.db.server.from('entries', {
    where: eligible, limit: 200,
  });
  for (const row of due) {
    const claim = await sw.db.server.update('entries', {
      where: { ...eligible, id: row.id },
      set: { reminder_claimed_ms: Date.now(), reminder_status: 'claimed' },
    });
    const entry = claim.data[0];
    if (!entry) continue;
    let reminder_status = 'review';
    let reminded_ms: number | null = null;
    let message_id: string | undefined;
    let sendError: { code?: string } | undefined;
    try {
      const sent = await sw.email.send({ to: entry.owner_email, subject: 'An entry is waiting for you.',
        text: entry.title, idempotency_key: `reminder-entry-${entry.id}` });
      reminder_status = sent.status;
      message_id = sent.message_id;
      if (['sent', 'delivered', 'opened', 'clicked', 'captured'].includes(sent.status)) reminded_ms = Date.now();
    } catch (err) {
      sendError = err as { code?: string };
    }
    await sw.db.server.update('entries', { set: { reminder_status, reminded_ms }, where: { id: entry.id } });
    await sw.logs.info('reminder outcome', { entry_id: entry.id, status: reminder_status, message_id, code: sendError?.code });
  }
  return Response.json({ checked: due.length });
}
```

A signed handler has no signed-in user, so it uses `sw.db.server.*`. Each
entry is claimed before sending, with its own stable email key. A competing
run gets no claimed row and skips it. `reminded_ms` records confirmed
acceptance or test capture, not delivery; `captured` reaches only the test inbox.

The actual send status stays on the row. A thrown send is recorded as `review`
because delivery could have occurred. Pending, unfamiliar or interrupted
attempts stay claimed for review before another attempt. Never clear a claim
automatically, including after an unknown send or a failed outcome update.

## 6. Deploy and verify

```bash
somewhere typecheck && somewhere deploy
somewhere cron create "0 9 * * *" /api/jobs/remind --name remind --timezone America/Los_Angeles
somewhere verify --url https://<project>.somewhere.site/<form-page> --flow flow.json
```

`flow.json` (one call, every step, one viewport: each viewport runs the whole
flow as its own session, so two would submit the form twice):

```json
{ "actions": [{ "fill": "#title", "value": "Verify entry" }, { "fill": "#from_email", "value": "v@example.com" },
  { "click": "#send" }, { "expect": { "selector": "#status", "text": "Received" } }], "viewports": ["desktop"] }
```

The flow expects your form page to have inputs `#title` and `#from_email`, a
`#send` button, and a `#status` element that shows "Received". For the owner
page add `"auth": { "user_id": "<test-user-id>" }` to a second flow, with a
managed test user (`somewhere docs browser`).
Mail to `<name>@<project>.test.somewhere.site` is read with
`somewhere email test-inbox <address>`. The inbox holds both sign-in email
and your own `sw.email.send` to a registered test user. That send returns
`status: 'captured'`: read the alert in that test inbox. For real addresses,
`sent` records acceptance by the email provider; `sw.email.status(id)`
reports delivery events. Treat `captured` and `sent` as successful
submission when displaying the form result. Use a real mailbox you control
when checking arrival.

### One owner, browser → reminder → inbox → database

Reuse a controlled owner who signed up through your UI as
`<name>@<project>.test.somewhere.site`, and sign it in at the start of
`owner-flow.json`. A seeded `auth: { user_id }` session is only for managed
test users, which expire after 24 hours, before any entry is due. The page below
must render that owner's entry titled "Verify reminder". Use an isolated
fixture with one open entry older than 24 hours and no reminder claim;
a newly submitted entry is not eligible for this daily reminder. The signed
handler claims each due row once, so inspect an unknown run instead of replaying
it or clearing its claim. Keep the test user's other due rows out of this fixture.

```json
{ "actions": [
    { "fill": "#auth-email", "value": "<name>@<project>.test.somewhere.site" },
    { "fill": "#auth-password", "value": "<test-password>" },
    { "click": "form:has(#auth-email) button[type=submit]" },
    { "expect": { "selector": "body", "text": "Verify reminder" } }],
  "viewports": ["desktop"] }
```

With the recipe deployed and its `remind` schedule already created above,
replace the project, address and row id with that same fixture's values:

```bash
# Set PROJECT, OWNER_PAGE (such as /entries), TEST_EMAIL and ENTRY_ID for your fixture.
set -euo pipefail
somewhere verify --project "$PROJECT" --url "https://${PROJECT}.somewhere.site${OWNER_PAGE}" --flow owner-flow.json
somewhere cron run remind --project "$PROJECT" --wait
somewhere email test-inbox "$TEST_EMAIL" --project "$PROJECT"
somewhere db rows entries --project "$PROJECT" --where "id=$ENTRY_ID" --where "owner_email=$TEST_EMAIL" --json | node -e "const { rows } = JSON.parse(require('node:fs').readFileSync(0, 'utf8')); if (rows.length !== 1 || rows[0].title !== 'Verify reminder' || rows[0].reminder_status !== 'captured' || !rows[0].reminded_ms) process.exit(1)"
```

The inbox must contain this entry's title in the reminder message. The database
assertion checks the same row's recorded test capture, not inbox delivery or
owner isolation: `db rows` is developer inspection of managed production data;
its `--where` filters only the columns it returns, which omit the owner's user id.
The authenticated browser check exercises the owner-facing app. These are
separate CLI commands in one workflow, not invented `verify` step kinds.
Keep `owner-flow.json` with its test password out of the deployed source
(`echo "owner-flow.json" >> .somewhereignore`).

## 7. Use it from Claude (preview feature)

The same signed-in app can serve MCP tools at `https://<your-app-address>/mcp`,
so an owner can add it to Claude and work with their own rows from a chat. The
steps, tool examples and current limits are in `somewhere docs app-mcp`.

Users sign up and in with the starter's email-and-password page, your
functions know who is calling, and each user sees only their own rows. Add
Google, GitHub or Discord, sign-in links or a second factor only when the app
needs them.

## 1. What the starter already does

`somewhere init` writes sign-in end to end:

| File | What it does |
|---|---|
| `api/auth/[...path].ts` | One line, `export { somewhereAuth as default } from '@somewhere-cloud/sdk/server'`, serving `/api/auth/signup`, `login`, `logout`, `me`, `magic-link`, `magic-link/verify`, `verify-email`, `request-email-verification` and the Google/GitHub/Discord sign-in routes. |
| `src/services/auth.ts` | Creates and shares one browser client with `createSomewhereAuth()`. |
| `src/auth/hooks.ts`, `src/pages/SignInPage.tsx`, `src/ui/AuthCard.tsx` | The sign-in and sign-up form, the signed-in check and sign-out. |
| `src/auth/useEmailVerification.ts`, `src/ui/VerifyEmail.tsx` | The "Verify your email" panel a password account sees until it enters the emailed code. |

Signing in sets the session as HttpOnly `__Host-` cookies, so page
JavaScript, including an injected script, can't read the session token or
send it elsewhere. A script running in the page can still make requests that
carry the cookie, so keep untrusted HTML out of your pages. Password
sign-in already limits attempts per IP address and per email, with progressive
lockout: `sw.auth` → `automatic-password-sign-in-protection`.

## 2. Your endpoint and the signed-in user

Ask before destructive or paid actions unless the user already authorized them.

Run `somewhere init --name my-app --features private-data`. The starter's
`db/schema.ts` declares a `notes` table owned by each user.

```ts
// db/schema.ts
import { id, owner, schema, table, text } from 'somewhere/db';

export default schema({
  notes: table(
    { id: id(), title: text(), body: text({ default: '' }) },
    {
      scope: owner(),
      client: { read: ['id', 'title', 'body'], create: ['title', 'body'], update: ['title', 'body'], delete: true, identity: 'authenticated' },
    },
  ),
});
```

A function reads the caller from the request and answers with their data:

```ts
// api/summary.ts — GET: the signed-in user's own summary
export default sw.endpoint({
  auth: 'required',
  handler: async ({ user }, sw) => {
    // notes is owner(): the count covers only the caller's rows.
    const { data: notes } = await sw.db.count('notes');
    return { email: user.email, notes };
  },
});
```

The page calls it through the same client, which carries the session:

```ts
// src/services/summary.ts
import { auth } from './auth';

export interface Summary { email: string | null; notes: number }

export async function loadSummary(): Promise<Summary | null> {
  const response = await auth.fetch('/api/summary');
  if (response.status === 401) return null; // signed out
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Summary failed (${response.status})`);
  return (await response.json()) as Summary;
}
```

`auth: 'required'` verifies the session before calling the handler and returns
401 for a signed-out request. Owner scopes, shared rows and server authority:
`declared-data`.

