A signed-in user asks for an email, your function sends it to their own
address once they have verified it, and the page shows what the platform
answered. No sender account or domain is needed to start. Then add your own
sender domain, receiving, bounces or many recipients when the app needs them.

## 1. The function: verified recipient, one send per action

A password sign-up can name any address. This function sends custom content
only after the account's owner has verified that address: it checks that
`user.email_verified` is true, which a sign-in link or the verification
code sets (`sw.auth` → `email-verification`, `magic-links`).

```ts
// api/email-me.ts — POST { title, body, request_id }
export default sw.endpoint({
  auth: 'none', // the handler applies its own access check below
  handler: async ({ request }, sw) => {
    if (request.method !== 'POST') {
      return new Response('Use POST.', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'POST' } });
    }
    const refuse = (error: string, status: number, code?: string) =>
      Response.json({ outcome: 'request-refused', error, code }, { status });

    const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(request);
    if (!user) return refuse('Sign in to email yourself.', 401);
    if (user.email_verified !== true) return refuse('Verify your email address first.', 403);

    const input: unknown = await request.json().catch(() => null);
    const fields = typeof input === 'object' && input !== null ? input : {};
    const title = 'title' in fields ? fields.title : undefined;
    const body = 'body' in fields ? fields.body : undefined;
    const requestId = 'request_id' in fields ? fields.request_id : undefined;
    if (typeof title !== 'string' || !title.trim() || title.length > 120
        || typeof body !== 'string' || body.length > 10000
        || typeof requestId !== 'string' || !/^[\w-]{8,64}$/.test(requestId)) {
      return refuse('Send a title, a body and a request_id.', 400);
    }

    // 10 per hour per user is this example's choice.
    const allowance = await sw.rateLimit.check(`email-me:${user.id}`, 10, 3600);
    if (!allowance.allowed) return refuse('Too many requests. Try again later.', 429);

    try {
      const sent = await sw.email.send({
        to: user.email,
        subject: `Your note: ${title.trim()}`,
        text: body,
        // Reuse this key when checking the outcome of the same action.
        idempotency_key: `email-me:${user.id}:${requestId}`,
      });
      return Response.json({ id: sent.id, status: sent.status });
    } catch (error) {
      const code = error instanceof Error && 'code' in error ? String(error.code) : 'EMAIL_FAILED';
      const status = error instanceof Error && 'status' in error ? Number(error.status) : 0;
      // The email service refused this request (422 or 429). Anything else is unconfirmed.
      if (status === 422 || status === 429) return refuse('The email service refused this request.', status, code);
      return Response.json({ outcome: 'unconfirmed', error: 'Could not confirm the email.', code }, { status: 502 });
    }
  },
});
```

The recipient is the session's own address, never one from the request body,
and the checks run before the send. Every refusal says only that this request
was refused; it says nothing about an earlier request with the same
`request_id`.

## 2. Call it from the page

```ts
// src/services/email-me.ts
import { auth } from './auth';

export type EmailResult =
  | { kind: 'status'; status: string } // the platform's state for this message
  | { kind: 'request-refused'; error: string } // this request was refused
  | { kind: 'unconfirmed' }; // nothing settled the outcome

/** One request_id per user action. Check again with the same id, title and body. */
export async function emailMe(title: string, body: string, requestId: string): Promise<EmailResult> {
  let response: Response;
  try {
    response = await auth.fetch('/api/email-me', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
      body: JSON.stringify({ title, body, request_id: requestId }),
    });
  } catch {
    return { kind: 'unconfirmed' }; // the request may have arrived
  }
  const result: unknown = await response.json().catch(() => null);
  const fields = typeof result === 'object' && result !== null ? result : {};
  if (response.ok && 'status' in fields && typeof fields.status === 'string') {
    return { kind: 'status', status: fields.status };
  }
  if ('outcome' in fields && fields.outcome === 'request-refused') {
    const error = 'error' in fields && typeof fields.error === 'string' ? fields.error : 'This request was refused.';
    return { kind: 'request-refused', error };
  }
  return { kind: 'unconfirmed' };
}
```

Create the id when the user acts, for example
`const requestId = crypto.randomUUID()`, and keep it with that action's title
and body. Reuse that id to check the same message; create a new id for a
new note.

- **`status`**: show it. `sent` means the provider accepted the message,
  and `captured` that it is in the test inbox, not delivered. A repeat
  shows the message's later state: `delivered`, `opened` or `clicked`
  happened. `bounced` reports a delivery failure; `complained` reports
  a spam complaint. `suppressed`, `preference_blocked` and `failed`
  report that the send was blocked or failed. `pending`,
  `claimed` or `delivery_unknown` mean the platform could not confirm it
  yet.
- **`request-refused`**: show "This request was refused" and the error. It
  covers this request only; a repeat keeps the same `request_id`.
- **`unconfirmed`**: show "We couldn't confirm the email" and offer **Check
  again**, which calls `emailMe` with the same `requestId`, title and body.
  Nothing is sent automatically. A check can stay unconfirmed; `sw.email` →
  `result-and-errors` explains the states.

