## Your own sender domain

The platform's sender reaches this project's users and its owner. To email
anyone else, or to send from your own address, pass `from` on a verified
sender domain of the project:

```ts
// Fragment, inside a function, once the domain is verified.
await sw.email.send({ to: 'alice@example.com', from: 'hello@yourapp.com', subject: 'Welcome', text: 'Thanks for joining.' });
```

- A domain bought through the platform is set up for sending automatically.
- For a domain registered elsewhere, [add and verify a sender domain](https://somewhere.cloud/docs/sw.email.txt#your-own-sender-domain).
  Sender domains need a paid plan. On Free the call answers `UPGRADE_REQUIRED` (402).
- A send with that `from` returns `SENDER_DOMAIN_NOT_FOUND` if the
  domain has not been added, or `SENDER_DOMAIN_NOT_VERIFIED` while verification
  is pending. Once verified, sign-in emails use your domain too.

## Receive email

Mail arrives at addresses on a custom domain of the project:

1. Turn on inbound mail for a domain claimed on the project:
   `domain_enable_email({ id })`, with the domain id from `domain_owned`. Its
   `receiving_host` is the domain itself, or reply.<domain> when the domain
   already has a mail provider (whose records stay unchanged).
2. Register addresses on that `receiving_host` and read the mail they
   receive through the mail API (`/v1/mail`).

Inbound addresses need a custom domain; the test inbox captures mail your
project sends and receives no outside mail.

## A follow-up that sends once and stops on reply

A day-two nudge takes a schedule, a query on your own data, and two options on
`sw.mail.project.send`. It needs no workflow engine.

- **`once: '<step>'`** sends this step to this person at most once. A second
  call for the same step and recipient returns the first result with
  `duplicate: true` and sends nothing, so a schedule that runs twice is safe.
  The first attempt counts whatever its outcome: a refused or waiting first
  attempt is not retried for that person. `once` needs exactly one address in
  `to`.
- **`unless_replied: true`** sends nothing to someone the project mailbox has
  already received mail from. The answer is then `status: 'skipped'` with
  `skipped: 'replied'`.

A reply already recorded stops the next nudge. A reply still in transit while
the nudge goes out cannot stop it.

```ts
// api/jobs/day-two.ts: runs daily and nudges people who signed up the day before yesterday
export default sw.endpoint({
  auth: 'none', // The platform invocation signature authorizes this route.
  handler: async ({ request: req }, sw) => {
    if (!(await sw.jobs.verifyInvocation(req))) return new Response('forbidden', { status: 403 });
    const day = 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;
    const { data } = await sw.db.server.from('signups', {
      where: { created_at: [{ gte: new Date(Date.now() - 3 * day).toISOString() }, { lt: new Date(Date.now() - 2 * day).toISOString() }] },
      limit: 500,
    });
    let sent = 0;
    for (const person of data) {
      const r = await sw.mail.project.send({
        from: 'hello@example.com', // an address of this project on a verified sending domain
        to: String(person.email),
        subject: 'Did it work for you?',
        text: 'If anything got in the way, reply to this email and I will help.',
        once: 'day-two',
        unless_replied: true,
      });
      if (r.status !== 'skipped' && !r.duplicate) sent += 1;
    }
    return { sent };
  },
});
```

```bash
somewhere deploy
somewhere cron create "0 16 * * *" /api/jobs/day-two --project <project> --name day-two
somewhere cron run day-two --project <project> --wait   # run it once now
```

The sending address must be on a domain the project has verified for
sending. Replies come back to the same address and are recorded in the
project mailbox, which is what `unless_replied` reads.

## Delivery status and bounces

`status: 'sent'` means the provider accepted the message. What happened next
is on the message:

- `sw.email.status(id)` returns one message's timeline: delivered, opened,
  clicked, bounced or complained.
- A permanent bounce pauses that address for 30 days, a temporary one for
  24 hours, and a spam complaint permanently. Sends to a paused address throw
  `EMAIL_SUPPRESSED` before anything is sent.
- `sw.email.checkSuppression(address)` checks one address before you send;
  `sw.email.bounces({ days })` returns the list to skip for a mailing.
- A project whose mail bounces or draws complaints too often is paused for
  every send until the rate recovers: `sw.email` → `sending-reputation`.

## Send to a list

There is no batch send call: each recipient is one `sw.email.send`. These
rules keep a mailing fast, repeat-safe and out of spam folders:

- **Start sends together in small groups, never one awaited send after
  another.** Each send waits for the provider, so a loop of awaited sends
  takes as many round trips as there are recipients.
- **One `idempotency_key` per recipient per mailing**, such as
  `` `digest-${digestId}-${user.id}` ``, so a retried batch never emails
  anyone twice.
- **Skip bounced and opted-out addresses first.** Remove
  `sw.email.bounces()` addresses, and keep an opt-out column of your own that
  a link in every mailing sets.
- **Escape every value you put into `html`.** A user's name is user input.
- **Stay under the send rate.** It's counted per account across its
  projects; above it a send throws `RATE_LIMITED` (429). For a list longer
  than one minute's worth, create one job per batch, each with a later
  `run_at`, and send that batch in its handler
  (`recipe-background-jobs` → `8-work-that-starts-later`).
- **Use your own sender domain for newsletters and announcements.** The
  platform's sender is for transactional mail.

The send-rate number and a grouped-send example: `sw.email` →
`sending-to-many-recipients`.

A signed-in user turns on notifications in their browser, your function
notifies them, and you check delivery in the send history. The platform
serves the service worker and the subscribe helper, holds the signing keys
and encrypts each message, so these two files connect your page and function.

## 1. The page: a button that turns notifications on

```ts
// src/services/notifications.ts — browser
import { enablePush, disablePush, pushStatus } from 'somewhere:push'

/** Call from a click handler: browsers show the permission prompt only after a user gesture. */
export async function turnOnNotifications(): Promise<string> {
  try {
    await enablePush()
    return 'Notifications are on for this browser.'
  } catch (error) {
    const code = error instanceof Error && 'code' in error ? String(error.code) : ''
    if (code === 'PUSH_PERMISSION_DENIED') return 'Notifications are blocked in this browser\'s site settings.'
    if (code === 'PUSH_UNSUPPORTED') return 'This browser cannot receive notifications. On iPhone, add the app to the Home Screen first.'
    if (code === 'AUTH_REQUIRED') return 'Sign in first.'
    throw error
  }
}

export async function turnOffNotifications(): Promise<void> {
  await disablePush()
}

export async function notificationsAreOn(): Promise<boolean> {
  return (await pushStatus()).subscribed
}
```

`enablePush()` registers this browser for the user of the session cookie.
Call `turnOffNotifications()` before signing out on a shared computer; if
another user signs in and turns notifications on, the browser moves to them.

## 2. The function: notify the signed-in user

```ts
// api/notify.ts — send a test notification to the signed-in user
export default sw.endpoint({
  auth: 'none', // the handler applies its own access check below
  handler: async ({ request: req }, sw) => {
    if (req.method !== 'POST') return new Response(null, { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'POST' } })
    const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(req)
    if (!user) return Response.json({ error: 'Sign in first.' }, { status: 401 })

    const push = await sw.push.send({
      user_id: user.id,
      payload: { title: 'Notifications are ready', body: 'Your browser is connected.', url: '/' },
    })
    return Response.json({ notified_devices: push.sent + push.captured, push })
  },
});
```

`sw.push.send({ user_id })` reaches every browser that user turned on, and
reports one result per device. A missing or `null` `user_id` is refused, never
sent to everyone; a broadcast is `audience: 'all'`. To notify a different user (a comment on
their post, a task assigned to them), the function first checks that the
signed-in user is allowed to reach that user, then passes that user's id.

Tapping the notification opens `url` in your app, or focuses the tab already
showing it. Only paths on your own origin open; anything else opens `/`.

