A signed-in user starts work that shouldn't hold up their request, checks its
status from the page, and the result is stored for that user. This job counts
the user's notes and saves a summary. A retried start with the same
`operationId` and a repeated delivery each leave exactly one summary.

Jobs work on every plan with no queue server to run. The platform signs each
delivery and retries failed runs. The start request takes the user from the
session and stores it in the job's payload. The handler verifies the delivery,
then reads and writes with server authority, scoped by hand to that user.
Sections 6 to 9 add a live result on the page, many items at once, work that
starts later, and failures.

## 1. db/schema.ts

```ts
// db/schema.ts
import { id, integer, owner, schema, table, text, timestamp } from 'somewhere/db';

export default schema({
  notes: table(
    { id: id(), title: text(), body: text({ default: '' }), created_at: timestamp({ default: 'now' }) },
    { scope: owner(), client: { read: ['id', 'title', 'body', 'created_at'], create: ['title', 'body'], update: ['title', 'body'], delete: true } },
  ),
  note_summaries: table(
    { id: id(), summary_id: text({ unique: true }), note_count: integer(), created_at: timestamp({ default: 'now' }) },
    { scope: owner(), client: { read: ['id', 'note_count', 'created_at'] } },
  ),
});
```

## 2. Start the job and report it

```ts
// api/summaries.ts — POST { operation_id } starts a summary; GET ?id=<job_id> reports it
export default sw.endpoint({
  auth: 'none', // the handler applies its own access check below
  handler: async ({ request }, sw) => {
    const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(request);
    if (!user) return Response.json({ error: 'Sign in.' }, { status: 401 });

    if (request.method === 'GET') {
      const id = new URL(request.url).searchParams.get('id');
      if (!id) return Response.json({ error: 'Pass ?id=<job_id>.' }, { status: 400 });
      let job: Awaited<ReturnType<typeof sw.jobs.status>>;
      try {
        job = await sw.jobs.status(id); // only the user who created the job can read it
      } catch (error) {
        if (error instanceof Error && 'status' in error && error.status === 404) {
          return Response.json({ error: 'No such summary for this account.' }, { status: 404 });
        }
        throw error;
      }
      const recoveryError = job.recovery?.recovery_error ?? null;
      const failure = { error: job.error ?? null, error_code: job.error_code ?? null };
      const summaryId = (job.payload as { summary_id?: unknown } | null)?.summary_id;
      if (job.status !== 'complete' || typeof summaryId !== 'string') {
        return Response.json({ status: job.status, recovery_error: recoveryError, note_count: null, ...failure });
      }
      const { data } = await sw.db.from('note_summaries', { where: { summary_id: summaryId }, limit: 1 });
      return Response.json({ status: job.status, recovery_error: recoveryError, note_count: data[0]?.note_count ?? null, ...failure });
    }
    if (request.method !== 'POST') return new Response('Use GET or POST.', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'GET, POST' } });

    const input: unknown = await request.json().catch(() => null);
    const operationId = typeof input === 'object' && input !== null && 'operation_id' in input ? input.operation_id : undefined;
    if (typeof operationId !== 'string' || !/^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{8,64}$/.test(operationId)) {
      return Response.json({ error: 'Send an operation_id of 8–64 letters, digits, _ or -.' }, { status: 400 });
    }
    // 10 per hour per user is this example's choice.
    const allowance = await sw.rateLimit.check(`summaries:${user.id}`, 10, 3600);
    if (!allowance.allowed) return Response.json({ error: 'Try again later.' }, { status: 429 });

    // Same user + operation_id → same key and payload → the same job, never a second one.
    // user_id comes from the verified session, never from the request body.
    const job = await sw.jobs.create({
      handler: '/api/jobs/summarize-notes',
      idempotency_key: `summary:${user.id}:${operationId}`,
      payload: { summary_id: `${user.id}:${operationId}`, user_id: user.id },
    });
    return Response.json({ job_id: job.job_id }, { status: 202 });
  },
});
```

## 3. The handler

```ts
// api/jobs/summarize-notes.ts — runs only when the platform delivers the job
export default async function summarizeNotes(request: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  // First, before reading the payload or any data.
  if (!(await sw.jobs.verifyInvocation(request))) return Response.json({ error: 'forbidden' }, { status: 403 });

  const payload: unknown = await request.json().catch(() => null);
  const fields = typeof payload === 'object' && payload !== null ? payload : {};
  const summaryId = 'summary_id' in fields ? fields.summary_id : undefined;
  const userId = 'user_id' in fields ? fields.user_id : undefined;
  if (typeof summaryId !== 'string' || !summaryId || typeof userId !== 'string' || !userId) {
    return Response.json({ error: 'Invalid payload.' }, { status: 400 });
  }

  // Server authority, scoped by hand to the user the start request put in the payload.
  const { data: noteCount } = await sw.db.server.count('notes', { where: { user_id: userId } });
  await sw.db.server.insert(
    'note_summaries',
    { summary_id: summaryId, note_count: noteCount, user_id: userId },
    { onConflict: 'ignore' },
  );
  return Response.json({ ok: true });
}
```

`verifyInvocation` is true only for a platform-signed delivery of this job;
anything else gets 403 before it can touch data. The delivery carries the
payload your start request stored, and that payload carries the
creator's verified identity, so the handler names `user_id` on every read and
write. A browser can't reach this handler with its own payload: a direct call
fails verification. Plain `sw.db` calls here are refused with
`ROW_JOB_REQUIRED`, because a job acts as its user on managed data only
through a row binding (`sw.jobs` → `act-on-one-row-for-the-signed-in-user`),
and this job reads many rows. The platform can run a handler more than once;
the unique `summary_id` makes a repeat insert nothing.

## 4. The page

Two small calls: one starts the summary, one reads its status.

```ts
// src/services/summaries.ts
import { auth } from './auth';

export type SummaryState = {
  status: string;
  note_count: number | null;
  recovery_error: string | null;
  error: string | null;
  error_code: string | null;
};

/** Start one summary, or reach it again with the same operationId. Returns its job id. */
export async function startSummary(operationId: string): Promise<string> {
  const response = await auth.fetch('/api/summaries', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ operation_id: operationId }),
  });
  const result: unknown = await response.json().catch(() => null);
  const jobId = typeof result === 'object' && result !== null && 'job_id' in result ? result.job_id : undefined;
  if (!response.ok || typeof jobId !== 'string') throw new Error(`Could not start the summary (${response.status}).`);
  return jobId;
}

/** Read one summary's current state. */
export async function readSummary(jobId: string): Promise<SummaryState> {
  const response = await auth.fetch(`/api/summaries?id=${encodeURIComponent(jobId)}`);
  if (response.status === 404) throw new Error('No such summary for this account.');
  const result = (await response.json().catch(() => null)) as Partial<SummaryState> | null;
  if (!response.ok || typeof result?.status !== 'string') throw new Error(`Could not read the summary (${response.status}).`);
  return {
    status: result.status,
    note_count: result.note_count ?? null,
    recovery_error: result.recovery_error ?? null,
    error: result.error ?? null,
    error_code: result.error_code ?? null,
  };
}
```

```tsx
// src/pages/SummaryPanel.tsx
import { useState } from 'react';
import { readSummary, startSummary, type SummaryState } from '../services/summaries';

const FINISHED = ['complete', 'failed', 'cancelled'];

export function SummaryPanel() {
  const [operationId, setOperationId] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const [jobId, setJobId] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const [state, setState] = useState<SummaryState | null>(null);
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const [busy, setBusy] = useState(false);

  async function run(work: () => Promise<void>) {
    setBusy(true);
    setError(null);
    try { await work(); } catch (e) { setError(e instanceof Error ? e.message : String(e)); } finally { setBusy(false); }
  }
  // One click is one operation: retrying a failed start reuses its id.
  const start = () => run(async () => {
    const id = operationId ?? crypto.randomUUID();
    setOperationId(id);
    setJobId(await startSummary(id));
  });
  const check = () => run(async () => { if (jobId) setState(await readSummary(jobId)); });
  const reset = () => { setOperationId(null); setJobId(null); setState(null); setError(null); };
  const finished = state !== null && FINISHED.includes(state.status);

  return (
    <section className="panel">
      <button className="button" onClick={start} disabled={busy || jobId !== null}>
        {operationId && !jobId ? 'Retry start' : 'Start summary'}
      </button>
      <button className="button" onClick={check} disabled={busy || !jobId}>Check status</button>
      {finished && <button className="button" onClick={reset} disabled={busy}>Start another</button>}
      {state && <p>Status: {state.status}{state.status === 'complete' ? (state.note_count === null ? ' — result unavailable' : ` — ${state.note_count} notes`) : ''}</p>}
      {state && (state.status === 'failed' || state.status === 'cancelled') && (
        <p role="alert">{state.error ?? `The summary was ${state.status}.`}{state.error_code ? ` (${state.error_code})` : ''}</p>
      )}
      {state && !finished && <p className="muted">Not finished yet{state.recovery_error ? ` (${state.recovery_error})` : ''}. Check again later.</p>}
      {error && <p role="alert">{error}</p>}
    </section>
  );
}
```

The page starts nothing on its own: **Start summary** creates one
`operationId` per click and keeps it, so **Retry start** after a failed start
reaches the job that may already exist. Both buttons are disabled while a call
is in flight, and the job id survives a failed **Check status**. The page
treats only `complete`, `failed` and `cancelled` as finished. Any other
status, `indeterminate` included, means check again; read `sw.jobs` →
`recovery-status-what-a-job-handle-can-tell-you` before starting new work
for it.

