## 7. Many items at once

Give each item its own job and its own key, derived from the user action and
the item, so a retried request reaches the same jobs. This is a sketch:
`/api/jobs/summarize-note` is your own one-note handler, not published here.
Write it like section 3's handler, reading the note named by the payload's
`note_id` and scoping its server reads and writes to the payload's
`user_id`:

```ts
// Fragment, inside the signed-in POST handler of section 2: one job per selected note.
for (const noteId of noteIds) {
  await sw.jobs.create({
    handler: '/api/jobs/summarize-note',
    idempotency_key: `summary:${user.id}:${operationId}:${noteId}`,
    payload: { note_id: noteId, user_id: user.id },
  });
}
```

Each account can have at most 100 jobs queued, scheduled or running at
once, on every plan, and create 600 a minute. At the
limit, `sw.jobs.create` throws `JOB_QUOTA_EXCEEDED` or `RATE_LIMITED`
(429). For more items than that, create one job per batch of items instead.
This is also a sketch: `/api/jobs/summarize-batch` is your own handler, not
published here. Write it like section 3's handler, looping over the payload's
`note_ids` and scoping every server read and write to the payload's
`user_id`. A run is one function request under the usual time limits
(`limits`), so keep each batch small enough to finish in one run:

```ts
// Fragment, inside the signed-in POST handler of section 2: one job per batch of notes.
const BATCH = 10; // size it so one run finishes within the function time limit
for (let i = 0; i < noteIds.length; i += BATCH) {
  await sw.jobs.create({
    handler: '/api/jobs/summarize-batch',
    idempotency_key: `summary:${user.id}:${operationId}:batch-${i / BATCH}`,
    payload: { note_ids: noteIds.slice(i, i + BATCH), user_id: user.id },
  });
}
```

Have the batch handler record each item's result in your table and skip items
already recorded, so a repeated run does not redo finished work. A live view
(section 6) shows progress.

## 8. Work that starts later

A job starts as soon as it's created. To run something later, such as a
reminder two hours after sign-up, give it a `run_at`:

```ts
// Fragment, inside a signed-in handler.
await sw.jobs.create({
  handler: '/api/jobs/send-reminder',
  payload: { reminder_id: reminderId },
  idempotency_key: `reminder:${reminderId}`,
  run_at: new Date(Date.now() + 2 * 60 * 60 * 1000).toISOString(),
});
```

`run_at` is an ISO-8601 time with an explicit offset and can be any future
time; a past time gets `RUN_AT_NOT_IN_FUTURE`. The job reports `scheduled`
until then and holds one of the account's job slots while it waits
(`sw.jobs` → `start-once-at-a-future-time`). The handler verifies with
`sw.jobs.verifyInvocation(request)` (`sw.jobs` → `job-handler-function`).

## 9. When a job fails

The platform retries a failed run on its own. A run that keeps failing ends
as `failed`, and `sw.jobs.status` carries `error` and `error_code`. To try
again, start a new job with a new `operationId`; reusing the old one reaches
the failed job. `indeterminate` is different: the job may still be
recovering, so read `recovery` before starting new work (`sw.jobs` →
`recovery-status-what-a-job-handle-can-tell-you`).

For a job started by a signed-in user, call `sw.jobs.cancel(jobId)` from a
handler that requires sign-in. The platform uses the request's verified user;
another user's job returns `JOB_NOT_FOUND`. The result reports
`status: 'cancelled'`. A job that is complete, failed, cancelled or
indeterminate returns `JOB_ALREADY_COMPLETE`.

`job_cancel({ job_id })` is the developer-tool path for project-owned work,
such as a scheduled run. It keeps refusing user-owned jobs. Without a
signed-in user, `sw.jobs.cancel` also acts on project-owned work: only
expose that call behind your own authorization check. Cancelling queued work
prevents it from starting. An ordinary handler already running finishes, and
its side effects remain; keep those effects idempotent. An agent run stops
at its next step boundary.

To test a forced redelivery in a test project, see
`somewhere docs sw.jobs --section job-handler-function`.

A signed-in user writes a note and asks for a title. Your function calls a
model with `sw.ai.chat`, with no provider key, and the page shows the
suggestion or says plainly that none is available. Then pick the next
capability from the table at the end.

## Choose the shape and the model

- **A chatbot, tools or several steps:** start from `recipe-ai-agent`, which
  runs `sw.agent` with conversation history and tools. Don't assemble that
  loop from single calls.
- **A label, routing choice or score:** use `sw.ai.evaluate` (`sw.ai` →
  `typed-decisions`). Text uses Jev; images use Clef-flash.
- **One prose answer from one input,** such as a title or a summary: one
  `sw.ai.chat` call, as in this recipe.

Choose the model for each call with two questions: is the task easy, and is
a person waiting? If either answer is yes, use a fast model. If the task is
hard and nobody is waiting, use a reasoning model and run the call in a job
(`recipe-background-jobs`).

- **Fast:** omit `provider` and `model`. That's the free default. It uses
  your account's included AI allowance and refuses instead of charging when
  the allowance is used up.
- **Reasoning:** choose a `complete` entry in `ai_catalog` whose `label`
  says reasoning, and pass its `provider` and `model`. That's the paid path:
  it uses eligible included allowance, then prepaid balance, and counts toward
  the project's AI spend cap. Calls using your own provider key are billed by
  that provider and sit outside this cap.

`ai_catalog` lists which models are available now, with each one's price and
notes; the supplier's documentation has the rest. Tell the app's owner your
choice and its trade-off in one sentence, for example: "Titles use the free
default model because someone is waiting and the task is easy; when the
included allowance runs out, suggestions pause instead of charging."

## 1. The function

```ts
// api/suggest-title.ts — POST { text } → { title }
export default sw.endpoint({
  auth: 'none', // the handler applies its own access check below
  handler: async ({ request }, sw) => {
    if (request.method !== 'POST') {
      return new Response('Use POST.', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'POST' } });
    }

    const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(request);
    if (!user) {
      return Response.json({ error: 'Sign in to get a suggestion.' }, { status: 401 });
    }

    const input: unknown = await request.json().catch(() => null);
    const text = typeof input === 'object' && input !== null && 'text' in input ? input.text : undefined;
    if (typeof text !== 'string' || !text.trim() || text.length > 4000) {
      return Response.json({ error: 'Send up to 4,000 characters of text.' }, { status: 400 });
    }

    // 20 per hour per user is this example's choice. It runs before any model call.
    const allowance = await sw.rateLimit.check(`suggest-title:${user.id}`, 20, 3600);
    if (!allowance.allowed) {
      return Response.json({ error: 'Too many suggestions. Try again later.' }, { status: 429 });
    }

    const unavailable = (code: string) =>
      Response.json({ error: 'No suggestion right now. Try again later.', code }, { status: 503 });
    try {
      // No provider or model: the free default.
      const reply = await sw.ai.chat({
        system: 'Reply with one short title for the text. No quotes.',
        messages: [{ role: 'user', content: text }],
      });
      const title = reply.text.trim();
      return title ? Response.json({ title }) : unavailable('EMPTY_SUGGESTION');
    } catch (error) {
      const code = error instanceof Error && 'code' in error ? String(error.code) : '';
      const status = error instanceof Error && 'status' in error ? Number(error.status) : 0;
      // A plan or project limit (402, 429) or the model provider (5xx).
      if (status === 402 || status === 429 || status >= 500) {
        try {
          await sw.logs.warn('Title suggestion unavailable', { code, status });
        } catch (logError) {
          console.error('Could not write AI availability log.', logError);
        }
        return unavailable(code);
      }
      throw error; // Other failures reach the platform's normal error handling.
    }
  },
});
```

The checks run in order: sign-in, input, then this user's limit, so a
signed-out or oversized request never reaches a model.

When the model can't answer, the function says so with 503 and keeps the
platform's `code` in the response for you. It also writes the code and status
to your project's logs without including the user's text. If logging fails,
the same unavailable response still reaches the page. The page never shows
billing details. The free
default answers 429 for each of its limits: `RATE_LIMITED` for its request
limit, which is shared across the owner's projects, `AI_ALLOWANCE_EXHAUSTED`
for the included allowance and `AI_SPEND_CAP_REACHED` for the project's
monthly cap. A provider failure answers 5xx. On the paid path a balance
refusal answers 402. When the project reaches its spend cap, the platform also
writes one line to the project's logs and emails you, once that month.

Nothing retries the model call, and nothing switches to a paid model. Any
other error is thrown again, so the platform logs it as a function error
instead of hiding a bug behind "unavailable". `sw.ai` → `error-codes` lists
every code.

## 2. Call it from the page

```ts
// src/services/suggest-title.ts
import { auth } from './auth';

export type Suggestion =
  | { kind: 'title'; title: string } // the model's suggestion
  | { kind: 'refused'; error: string } // sign in, shorten the text, or wait
  | { kind: 'unavailable' }; // no suggestion this time

export async function suggestTitle(text: string): Promise<Suggestion> {
  let response: Response;
  try {
    response = await auth.fetch('/api/suggest-title', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
      body: JSON.stringify({ text }),
    });
  } catch {
    return { kind: 'unavailable' };
  }
  const result: unknown = await response.json().catch(() => null);
  const fields = typeof result === 'object' && result !== null ? result : {};
  if (response.ok && 'title' in fields && typeof fields.title === 'string' && fields.title) {
    return { kind: 'title', title: fields.title };
  }
  if ([400, 401, 429].includes(response.status) && 'error' in fields && typeof fields.error === 'string') {
    return { kind: 'refused', error: fields.error };
  }
  return { kind: 'unavailable' };
}
```

```tsx
// src/pages/TitleSuggestion.tsx
import { useState } from 'react';
import { suggestTitle, type Suggestion } from '../services/suggest-title';

export function TitleSuggestion() {
  const [text, setText] = useState('');
