## Try it

Sign in and choose a receipt you can share with the model. The app uploads it
and shows **Reading the receipt…** while extraction runs. Check and edit every
extracted value, then save. Ask your agent to inspect the saved row through the
generated data client; this example does not include a saved-receipts list. Sign in as a second user: the
first user's upload and receipt should be unavailable.

The reading can fail or be inaccurate. Keep the original file and ask the
user to check totals and currency before relying on a saved record. Use a
smaller image if an upload exceeds the example's limit.

Related: `declared-files` for upload rules, `declared-data` for owner tables,
`sw.ai` for models and costs, and `recipe-background-jobs` for longer work.

> **Verified end to end on 2026-11-05:** this recipe, copied unchanged into a
> new `somewhere init` app and deployed, imported a three-row file as one user;
> a repeat, an overlapping file, a file missing an email and a second user each
> behaved as section 5 states.

A signed-in user uploads a CSV of contacts and sees how many rows were stored.
The browser sends the file straight to storage, and one endpoint reads it,
checks every row, then writes them in atomic batches as that user. Importing
the same file again, or another file with some of the same addresses, updates
those contacts instead of adding duplicates. Every write goes through the
tables' declared access, so a user can only ever create or update their own
contacts.

## 1. db/schema.ts

```ts
// db/schema.ts
import { files, id, integer, owner, schema, table, text, timestamp } from 'somewhere/db';

export default schema({
  imports: table(
    {
      id: id(), file_id: text({ unique: true }), status: text({ default: 'running' }),
      rows_stored: integer({ default: 0 }), error: text({ nullable: true }), created_at: timestamp({ default: 'now' }),
    },
    { scope: owner(), client: { read: ['id', 'file_id', 'status', 'rows_stored', 'error', 'created_at'] } },
  ),
  contacts: table(
    { id: id(), email: text(), name: text(), import_id: text() },
    { scope: owner(), unique: [['user_id', 'email']], indexes: [['import_id']], client: { read: ['id', 'name', 'email'] } },
  ),
}, {
  files: {
    csv_uploads: files({
      path: '/csv-uploads',
      scope: owner(),
      client: { read: true, upload: true },
      limits: { maxSize: '2MB', types: ['text/csv'] },
    }),
  },
});
```

`owner()` on all three gives each user their own uploads, import rows and
contacts. The browser can upload and read; only your functions write rows.
`unique: [['user_id', 'email']]` makes an email address one contact per user:
`user_id` is the owner column the platform fills, so two users can each have
a contact with the same address. The collection accepts no browser replace or
delete, so the file an import reads is the file the user uploaded. The page
stores every upload as `upload.csv` under its own file id, so the function
builds the file's path from the id alone, never from a name the browser sends.
`'2MB'` is this example's cap; your plan's per-file ceiling is in `limits`.

## 2. The CSV reader

```ts
// api/_lib/csv.ts — comma-separated text: quoted fields, "" escapes, commas and line breaks inside quotes, LF or CRLF
export class CsvError extends Error {}

/** maxRecords counts the header row. */
export function parseCsv(text: string, maxRecords: number): string[][] {
  const records: string[][] = [];
  let record: string[] = [];
  let field = '';
  let state: 'start' | 'plain' | 'quoted' | 'closed' = 'start';
  const endField = () => { record.push(field); field = ''; state = 'start'; };
  const endRecord = () => {
    endField();
    if (record.length > 1 || record[0] !== '') records.push(record); // skip blank lines
    if (records.length > maxRecords) throw new CsvError(`The file has more than ${maxRecords - 1} rows below the header.`);
    record = [];
  };
  for (let i = text.charCodeAt(0) === 0xfeff ? 1 : 0; i < text.length; i++) {
    const ch = text[i];
    if (state === 'quoted') {
      if (ch !== '"') field += ch;
      else if (text[i + 1] === '"') { field += '"'; i++; }
      else state = 'closed';
    } else if (ch === ',') endField();
    else if (ch === '\n') endRecord();
    else if (ch === '\r' && text[i + 1] === '\n') { endRecord(); i++; }
    else if (ch === '"' && state === 'start') state = 'quoted';
    else if (ch === '"' || ch === '\r' || state === 'closed') throw new CsvError(`Row ${records.length + 1} is not valid CSV.`);
    else { field += ch; state = 'plain'; }
  }
  if (state === 'quoted') throw new CsvError('A quoted field is not closed.');
  if (state !== 'start' || field !== '' || record.length > 0) endRecord();
  return records;
}

export type ContactRow = { name: string; email: string };

/** Checks every row before any is written; a CsvError names the row to fix. */
export function contactsFromCsv(text: string, maxRows: number): ContactRow[] {
  const [header, ...rows] = parseCsv(text, maxRows + 1);
  if (header?.map((h) => h.trim().toLowerCase()).join(',') !== 'name,email') throw new CsvError('The first row must be: name,email');
  return rows.map((row, index) => {
    const [name = '', email = ''] = row.map((value) => value.trim());
    if (row.length !== 2 || !name || name.length > 200 || !/^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(email)) {
      throw new CsvError(`Row ${index + 2}: expected a name and an email address.`);
    }
    return { name, email: email.toLowerCase() };
  });
}
```

A file under `api/_lib/` is bundled into the functions that import it and is
never served as a route. Values come back as strings, unchanged. The
request below and the background job in **Larger files** both use
`contactsFromCsv`, so they accept and refuse the same files.

## 3. Import the file and report it

```ts
// api/imports.ts — POST { file_id } imports one uploaded CSV; GET ?file_id= reports it
import { type ContactRow, CsvError, contactsFromCsv } from './_lib/csv';

const MAX_ROWS = 2000; // this example's cap: one request reads, checks and writes the whole file
const BATCH = 100; // sw.db.tx takes 1–100 writes

export default sw.endpoint({
  auth: 'none', // the handler applies its own access check below
  handler: async ({ request }, sw) => {
    const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(request);
    if (!user) return Response.json({ error: 'Sign in.' }, { status: 401 });

    if (request.method === 'GET') {
      const fileId = new URL(request.url).searchParams.get('file_id');
      if (!fileId) return Response.json({ error: 'Pass ?file_id=<file id>.' }, { status: 400 });
      // Runs as this user: another user's import is not found.
      const { data: found } = await sw.db.from('imports', { where: { file_id: fileId }, limit: 1 });
      const row = found[0];
      if (!row) return Response.json({ error: 'No such import for this account.' }, { status: 404 });
      return Response.json({ status: row.status, rows_stored: Number(row.rows_stored), error: row.error });
    }
    if (request.method !== 'POST') return new Response('Use GET or POST.', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'GET, POST' } });

    const input = (await request.json().catch(() => null)) as { file_id?: unknown } | null;
    const fileId = input?.file_id;
    if (typeof fileId !== 'string' || !/^[A-Za-z0-9-]{8,64}$/.test(fileId)) {
      return Response.json({ error: 'Send the file_id from the upload result.' }, { status: 400 });
    }
    // 20 per hour per user is this example's choice.
    const allowance = await sw.rateLimit.check(`csv-import:${user.id}`, 20, 3600);
    if (!allowance.allowed) return Response.json({ error: 'Try again later.' }, { status: 429 });

    let text: string;
    try {
      // Runs as this user: another user's upload is not found.
      const file = await sw.fs.read(`/csv-uploads/${fileId}/upload.csv`);
      text = new TextDecoder('utf-8', { fatal: true }).decode(await file.arrayBuffer());
    } catch (error) {
      if (error instanceof Error && 'status' in error && error.status === 404) {
        return Response.json({ error: 'No such upload for this account.' }, { status: 404 });
      }
      if (error instanceof TypeError) return Response.json({ error: 'Save the file as CSV UTF-8 and upload it again.' }, { status: 422 });
      throw error;
    }
    await sw.db.insert('imports', { file_id: fileId }, { onConflict: 'ignore' });
    const finish = async (status: 'complete' | 'failed', rowsStored: number, error: string | null, httpStatus: number) => {
      await sw.db.update('imports', { where: { file_id: fileId }, set: { status, rows_stored: rowsStored, error } });
      return Response.json({ status, rows_stored: rowsStored, error }, { status: httpStatus });
    };

    let contacts: ContactRow[];
    try {
      contacts = contactsFromCsv(text, MAX_ROWS);
    } catch (error) {
      if (error instanceof CsvError) return finish('failed', 0, error.message, 422);
      throw error;
    }

    // Each batch is one atomic write as this user. An address already stored for this user is updated.
    for (let start = 0; start < contacts.length; start += BATCH) {
      await sw.db.tx(contacts.slice(start, start + BATCH).map((contact) => ({
        op: 'insert' as const,
        table: 'contacts',
        values: { ...contact, import_id: fileId },
        options: { onConflict: 'update' as const },
      })));
    }
    return finish('complete', contacts.length, null, 200);
  },
});
```

The import checks every row before it writes any, so a bad file stores
nothing and the import row says which row to fix. Each batch is atomic. The
insert runs as the signed-in user: the platform fills `user_id`, and
`onConflict: 'update'` updates the user's existing contact with the same
email (its name and `import_id`) instead of adding a second one. Two rows of
one file with the same address leave one contact with the later row's name.
Repeating the request, or importing an overlapping file, never creates a
duplicate. A lost connection part-way through leaves the batches that landed
stored; sending the same `file_id` again finishes the import, and
`rows_stored` counts the rows of the file.

The conflict update follows the table's declared access, like every
caller-scoped write. Under `owner()` the matching row is always the user's
own, because `user_id` is part of the unique key. If the contacts table is
shared with `anyOf(owner(), grant({ ... }))`
(`docs({ topic: 'declared-data', section: 'per-owner-sharing' })`), the same
code works unchanged: each person's import still updates only their own
contacts. With a unique key that does not include `user_id`, an address
stored by someone else refuses the batch with 403 `POLICY_AUTHORITY_REQUIRED`
unless their rule lets this user update it: see `sw.db` → Upserts.

## 4. The page

```ts
// src/services/csv-import.ts
import { files } from 'somewhere:files';

export type ImportState = { status: string; rows_stored: number | null; error: string | null };

/** Upload a CSV and import it. Returns the import's result. */
export async function importCsv(file: File): Promise<ImportState & { file_id: string }> {
  if (!file.name.toLowerCase().endsWith('.csv')) throw new Error('Choose a .csv file.');
  const { data: uploaded, error } = await files.csv_uploads.upload(file, { name: 'upload.csv', contentType: 'text/csv' });
  if (error) throw new Error(error.message);
  const response = await fetch('/api/imports', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ file_id: uploaded.id }),
  });
  const result = (await response.json().catch(() => null)) as (ImportState & { error?: string | null }) | null;
  if (response.status === 422 && result) return { ...result, file_id: uploaded.id };
  if (!response.ok || typeof result?.status !== 'string') throw new Error(result?.error ?? `Could not import the file (${response.status}).`);
  return { ...result, file_id: uploaded.id };
}

/** Read one import's recorded result. */
export async function readCsvImport(fileId: string): Promise<ImportState | null> {
  const response = await fetch(`/api/imports?file_id=${encodeURIComponent(fileId)}`);
  if (response.status === 404) return null;
  const result = (await response.json().catch(() => null)) as ImportState | null;
  if (!response.ok || typeof result?.status !== 'string') throw new Error(`Could not read the import (${response.status}).`);
  return result;
}
```

Browsers label `.csv` files differently, so the page checks the extension and
sends the declared `text/csv` type itself, under the fixed name `upload.csv`.
The import still rejects anything that is not valid UTF-8 CSV. Call
`importCsv` from a file input:

- `complete` shows `rows_stored`.
- `failed` shows `error` (a bad header, an invalid row, not CSV). Nothing was
  stored; upload the corrected file, which gets a new file id.
- A thrown error (a lost connection, a timeout) may come after some batches
  were stored. `readCsvImport` shows `running` for an import that did not
  finish; send the same file id again to finish it.

## 5. Check it

```bash
somewhere typecheck && somewhere deploy
somewhere db rows contacts
```

`contacts` is a declared table in the managed database. Use
`somewhere db rows contacts` to read its rows. `somewhere db query` reads the
separate raw SQL database.

Upload a three-row file: the import shows `complete` and `rows_stored: 3`,
and `db rows contacts` ends with `3 of 3 rows`. Upload the same file again,
or one that repeats an address with a new name: the count stays the same and
the name changes. Upload a file with a missing email on row 3: it shows
`failed` with `Row 3: expected a name and an email address.` and stores
nothing. A second user importing the same addresses gets their own contacts
and sees none of the first user's imports or contacts.

## Larger files

Each import above runs in one function request under the request limits in
`limits`. This recipe's caps (2 MB, 2,000 rows) keep its read, check and 20
batched writes small. Raise them only after timing a full import on your
deployed function. For files too large for one request, run the same import
as a background job. The file stays in file storage: the job reads it by its
file id, and the database holds only the import row and the contacts.

A job started from a signed-in user's request reads files as that user, so
the handler reads the upload with plain `sw.fs`, exactly as `api/imports.ts`
does, and another user's upload is not found
(`docs({ topic: 'sw.fs' })`, which also covers `sw.fs.server`).
That user authority lasts 10 minutes from `sw.jobs.verifyInvocation`, so the
handler reads the file first. Writes use `sw.db.server`, which needs no user
authority and lasts the whole job, and which does not apply the tables'
declared access: every write names `user_id`, taken from the verified session
when the job was started. Add one endpoint and one handler beside the files
above; the page keeps reading the result from `GET /api/imports?file_id=`.

```ts
// api/import-jobs.ts — POST { file_id } queues the import of one uploaded CSV
export default async function importJobs(request: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  if (request.method !== 'POST') return new Response('Use POST.', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'POST' } });
  const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(request);
  if (!user) return Response.json({ error: 'Sign in.' }, { status: 401 });
  const input = (await request.json().catch(() => null)) as { file_id?: unknown } | null;
  const fileId = input?.file_id;
  if (typeof fileId !== 'string' || !/^[A-Za-z0-9-]{8,64}$/.test(fileId)) {
    return Response.json({ error: 'Send the file_id from the upload result.' }, { status: 400 });
  }
  const allowance = await sw.rateLimit.check(`csv-import:${user.id}`, 20, 3600);
  if (!allowance.allowed) return Response.json({ error: 'Try again later.' }, { status: 429 });
  try {
    // Runs as this user, reading only the first line: another user's upload is not found.
    await sw.fs.read(`/csv-uploads/${fileId}/upload.csv`, { lines: [1, 1] });
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof Error && 'status' in error && error.status === 404) {
      return Response.json({ error: 'No such upload for this account.' }, { status: 404 });
    }
    throw error;
  }
  await sw.db.insert('imports', { file_id: fileId }, { onConflict: 'ignore' });
  // One job per user and file: a repeated POST returns the same job.
  const job = await sw.jobs.create({
    handler: '/api/jobs/import-csv',
    idempotency_key: `csv-import:${user.id}:${fileId}`,
    payload: { file_id: fileId, user_id: user.id },
    timeout_seconds: 900,
  });
  return Response.json({ job_id: job.job_id, file_id: fileId }, { status: 202 });
}
```

```ts
// api/jobs/import-csv.ts — runs only when the platform delivers the job
import { type ContactRow, CsvError, contactsFromCsv } from '../_lib/csv';

const MAX_ROWS = 50_000; // this example's cap: time a full import on your deployed app before raising it
const BATCH = 100; // sw.db.server.tx takes 1–100 writes

export default async function importCsvJob(request: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  if (!(await sw.jobs.verifyInvocation(request))) return Response.json({ error: 'forbidden' }, { status: 403 });
  const payload = (await request.json().catch(() => null)) as { file_id?: unknown; user_id?: unknown } | null;
  const fileId = payload?.file_id;
  const userId = payload?.user_id;
  if (typeof fileId !== 'string' || !fileId || typeof userId !== 'string' || !userId) {
    return Response.json({ error: 'Invalid payload.' }, { status: 400 });
  }
  const finish = async (status: 'complete' | 'failed', rowsStored: number, error: string | null) => {
    await sw.db.server.update('imports', { where: { file_id: fileId, user_id: userId }, set: { status, rows_stored: rowsStored, error } });
    return Response.json({ status, rows_stored: rowsStored, error });
  };

  let contacts: ContactRow[];
  try {
    // First, while the starting user's file authority is fresh.
    const file = await sw.fs.read(`/csv-uploads/${fileId}/upload.csv`);
    contacts = contactsFromCsv(new TextDecoder('utf-8', { fatal: true }).decode(await file.arrayBuffer()), MAX_ROWS);
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof CsvError) return finish('failed', 0, error.message);
    if (error instanceof TypeError) return finish('failed', 0, 'Save the file as CSV UTF-8 and upload it again.');
    throw error;
  }
  // Server authority, scoped by hand to the user who started the job.
  for (let start = 0; start < contacts.length; start += BATCH) {
    await sw.db.server.tx(contacts.slice(start, start + BATCH).map((contact) => ({
      op: 'insert' as const,
      table: 'contacts',
      values: { ...contact, import_id: fileId, user_id: userId },
      options: { onConflict: 'update' as const },
    })));
  }
  return finish('complete', contacts.length, null);
}
```

To use it, raise `limits.maxSize` on `csv_uploads` within your plan's
per-file ceiling (`limits`), and have the page post the upload's `file_id`
to `/api/import-jobs` instead of `/api/imports`, then call
`readCsvImport(file_id)` every few seconds until the status is no longer
`running`. A bad file ends the import `failed` with the same messages as
above and stores nothing; that is the import's answer, so the handler returns
200 and the job is not retried. An error the handler throws is retried within
the job's 900-second deadline, and every retry is safe: `unique: [['user_id',
'email']]` with `onConflict: 'update'` turns a repeated batch into the same
contacts. An import still `running` after that deadline did not finish (for
example `JOB_TIMEOUT`): the batches that landed are kept, and uploading the
file again, which gets a new file id, finishes it without duplicates. Job
retries, timeouts and status: `docs({ topic: 'sw.jobs' })`.

To load a dataset once as the developer, not from the app,
use the `db_import_csv` tool with a CSV already in your project files. If a batch
fails, the error gives the number of rows already imported and the CSV line to
resume from. Keep the header and import only the remaining rows. If the error says
a write could not be confirmed, check the table before trying again.

Write the document as HTML and CSS, and `sw.render.pdf` prints it in a real
browser: web fonts, flexbox, tables and page breaks render the way they do on
screen. This recipe makes an invoice for the signed-in user, stores it as a
private file, previews it in the page and merges PDFs in a function.

## 1. Generate and store an invoice

```ts
// db/schema.ts
import { id, integer, owner, schema, table, text } from 'somewhere/db';

export default schema({
  invoices: table(
    { id: id(), number: text(), customer: text(), amount_cents: integer() },
    { scope: owner(), client: { read: ['id', 'number', 'customer', 'amount_cents'] } },
  ),
});
```

```ts
// api/invoices/[id].ts — POST renders the invoice PDF; GET returns it
const escape = (value: string) =>
  value.replace(/[&<>"']/g, (ch) => ({ '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;', '"': '&quot;', "'": '&#39;' })[ch]!);

export default async function invoicePdf(request: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(request);
  if (!user) return Response.json({ error: 'Sign in.' }, { status: 401 });
  const id = new URL(request.url).pathname.split('/').pop() ?? '';
  if (!/^\d+$/.test(id)) return Response.json({ error: 'Unknown invoice.' }, { status: 404 });
  const path = `/invoices/${user.id}/${id}.pdf`;

  if (request.method === 'GET') {
    try {
      return await sw.fs.server.read(path); // streams the stored PDF with its content type
    } catch (error) {
      if (error instanceof Error && 'status' in error && error.status === 404) {
        return Response.json({ error: 'Generate the PDF first.' }, { status: 404 });
      }
      throw error;
    }
  }
  if (request.method !== 'POST') return new Response('Use GET or POST.', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'GET, POST' } });

  // owner(): only this user's invoice is found.
  const { data } = await sw.db.from('invoices', { where: { id }, limit: 1 });
  const invoice = data[0];
  if (!invoice) return Response.json({ error: 'Unknown invoice.' }, { status: 404 });

  const html = `<!doctype html><html><head><style>
    body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; margin: 48px; }
    h1 { font-size: 24px; } td { padding: 8px 0; }
  </style></head><body>
    <h1>Invoice ${escape(invoice.number)}</h1>
    <table><tr><td>Billed to</td><td>${escape(invoice.customer)}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Total</td><td>$${(Number(invoice.amount_cents) / 100).toFixed(2)}</td></tr></table>
  </body></html>`;

  const pdf = await sw.render.pdf({ html, format: 'A4', print_background: true });
  await sw.fs.server.write(path, await pdf.arrayBuffer(), { contentType: 'application/pdf' });
  return Response.json({ path }, { status: 201 });
}
```

`sw.render.pdf({ html })` returns the PDF as a `Response`. `sw.fs.server.write`
stores it as a private file, so only your functions read it. The path holds
the signed-in user's id, taken from the session, so one user's request can
never name another user's file. Escape every value you put into the HTML.
Render once and store the file; serve the stored copy after that.

Options: `format` is `'A4'`, `'A3'`, `'Letter'` (the default), `'Legal'` or
`'Tabloid'`; `landscape: true` rotates the page; `print_background: true`
prints background colours and images; `wait_for: '<css selector>'` waits up to
5 seconds for an element before printing. Pass `url` instead of `html` to
print a public page. `html` is at most 1,000,000 characters, and the page must
finish loading within 30 seconds. Renders are metered and charged to the
account balance.

## 2. Preview it in the page

Load the stored PDF from your own function, same origin, and draw it with
PDF.js. It draws to a canvas, so the preview looks the same on phones and
desktops.

```tsx
// src/ui/InvoicePreview.tsx — npm install pdfjs-dist (version 5 or later)
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from 'react';
import * as pdfjs from 'pdfjs-dist';

pdfjs.GlobalWorkerOptions.workerSrc = `https://unpkg.com/pdfjs-dist@${pdfjs.version}/build/pdf.worker.min.mjs`;

export function InvoicePreview({ invoiceId }: { invoiceId: string }) {
  return <InvoicePreviewPage key={invoiceId} invoiceId={invoiceId} />;
}

function InvoicePreviewPage({ invoiceId }: { invoiceId: string }) {
  const canvas = useRef<HTMLCanvasElement>(null);
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    let cancelled = false;
    let renderTask: { cancel(): void } | null = null;
    const loadingTask = pdfjs.getDocument({ url: `/api/invoices/${invoiceId}` });
    (async () => {
      const doc = await loadingTask.promise;
      const page = await doc.getPage(1);
      const target = canvas.current;
      if (cancelled || !target) return;
      const viewport = page.getViewport({ scale: 1.5 });
      target.width = viewport.width;
      target.height = viewport.height;
      const task = page.render({ canvas: target, viewport });
      renderTask = task;
      await task.promise;
    })().catch((reason: unknown) => {
      if (cancelled) return; // a replaced or unmounted preview reports nothing
      setError(reason instanceof Error ? reason.message : 'Could not load the PDF.');
    });
    return () => {
      cancelled = true;
      renderTask?.cancel(); // cancels the previous render
      loadingTask.destroy().catch(() => {}); // releases the loading task and worker
    };
  }, [invoiceId]);

  return (
    <>
      {error && <p role="alert">{error}</p>}
      <canvas ref={canvas} hidden={error !== null} style={{ maxWidth: '100%' }} />
    </>
  );
}
```

The worker script loads from a CDN at the same version as the library you
installed. To serve it yourself, copy `pdfjs-dist/build/pdf.worker.min.mjs`
into `public/` and set `workerSrc = '/pdf.worker.min.mjs'`. `react-pdf` works
the same way: set `pdfjs.GlobalWorkerOptions.workerSrc` as above rather than
the `new URL(..., import.meta.url)` form from its README.

## 3. Share a download link

```ts
// in a function, after authorizing the caller
const link = await sw.fs.server.signedUrl(`/invoices/${user.id}/${id}.pdf`, { expiresIn: 7 * 24 * 3600 });
// link.url opens the PDF for anyone holding it, until link.expires_at
```

A signed link is for email, a download button or a new tab. `expiresIn` is
clamped to between 60 seconds and 7 days (default 1 hour). It is served from the platform's file
host, so a page's own `fetch` or PDF.js loads the file through your function
as in step 2, not through the link.

## 4. Merge and split with pdf-lib

```ts
// api/invoices/merge.ts — POST { ids: [1, 2, 3] } → one PDF of the user's stored invoices
import { PDFDocument } from 'pdf-lib/dist/pdf-lib.esm.js';

export default async function mergeInvoices(request: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  if (request.method !== 'POST') return new Response('Use POST.', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'POST' } });
  const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(request);
  if (!user) return Response.json({ error: 'Sign in.' }, { status: 401 });
  const input = (await request.json().catch(() => null)) as { ids?: unknown } | null;
  const raw = input?.ids;
  const ids = Array.isArray(raw) ? raw.filter((v): v is number => Number.isInteger(v)) : [];
  if (ids.length < 2 || ids.length > 20) return Response.json({ error: 'Send 2–20 invoice ids.' }, { status: 400 });

  const merged = await PDFDocument.create();
  for (const id of ids) {
    let file: Response;
    try {
      file = await sw.fs.server.read(`/invoices/${user.id}/${id}.pdf`);
    } catch (error) {
      if (error instanceof Error && 'status' in error && error.status === 404) {
        return Response.json({ error: `Generate invoice ${id} first.` }, { status: 404 });
      }
      throw error;
    }
    const source = await PDFDocument.load(await file.arrayBuffer());
    const pages = await merged.copyPages(source, source.getPageIndices());
    for (const page of pages) merged.addPage(page);
  }
  return new Response(await merged.save(), { headers: { 'content-type': 'application/pdf' } });
}
```

In a function, pdf-lib loads from `pdf-lib/dist/pdf-lib.esm.js`; the plain
`'pdf-lib'` specifier fails to build. To split, copy only the pages you want
into a new document with `copyPages` and save it. pdf-lib also
fills form fields, adds pages and stamps text onto existing PDFs. It does not
open password-protected PDFs: `PDFDocument.load` throws `EncryptedPDFError`.
`ignoreEncryption: true` loads such a file without decrypting it, so its
pages stay unreadable.

## Check it

```bash
somewhere typecheck && somewhere deploy
```

Create an invoice row, `POST /api/invoices/<id>`, and open the page with
`<InvoicePreview invoiceId={id} />`. A second user's `GET` of the same id
returns 404.

A function's status code and the headers it sets reach the caller unchanged.
A `402 Payment Required` or `401 Unauthorized` with its
`WWW-Authenticate` challenge arrives intact, so the HTTP challenge flows that
agent payment protocols and OAuth-protected APIs use work from an ordinary
function in `api/`.

## 1. Answer with a payment challenge

```ts
// api/report.ts — free to describe, paid to fetch
import { verifyPayment } from './_lib/payment';

const PRICE = { amount: '0.01', currency: 'usd' }; // this example's price

export default sw.endpoint({
  auth: 'none', // the handler applies its own access check below
  handler: async ({ request }, sw) => {
    const credential = request.headers.get('authorization')?.match(/^Payment\s+(.+)$/i)?.[1];
    if (!credential) {
      return new Response(JSON.stringify({ error: 'payment_required', price: PRICE }), {
        status: 402,
        headers: {
          'content-type': 'application/json',
          'www-authenticate': `Payment realm="reports", amount="${PRICE.amount}", currency="${PRICE.currency}"`,
        },
      });
    }

    const receipt = await verifyPayment(credential, PRICE, sw);
    if (!receipt) return Response.json({ error: 'payment_invalid' }, { status: 402 });

    // A receipt pays for one call: the unique receipt_id refuses a replay.
    const saved = await sw.db.server.insert('paid_calls', { receipt_id: receipt.id, path: '/api/report' }, { onConflict: 'ignore' });
    if (saved.changes === 0) return Response.json({ error: 'payment_already_used' }, { status: 402 });

    return Response.json({ report: 'The paid content.' });
  },
});
```

```ts
// api/_lib/payment.ts — verifyPayment: the provider's verification call
export async function verifyPayment(
  credential: string,
  price: { amount: string; currency: string },
  sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext,
): Promise<{ id: string } | null> {
  const response = await fetch(sw.env.PAYMENT_VERIFY_URL, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { authorization: `Bearer ${sw.env.PAYMENT_SECRET}`, 'content-type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ credential, ...price }),
  });
  if (!response.ok) return null;
  const result = (await response.json()) as { valid?: boolean; receipt_id?: string };
  return result.valid && result.receipt_id ? { id: result.receipt_id } : null;
}
```

```ts
// db/schema.ts
import { id, serverOnly, schema, table, text, timestamp } from 'somewhere/db';

export default schema({
  paid_calls: table(
    { id: id(), receipt_id: text({ unique: true }), path: text(), created_at: timestamp({ default: 'now' }) },
    { scope: serverOnly() },
  ),
});
```

The agent calls once, reads the challenge, pays, and retries with the
credential. Your payment provider defines the challenge parameters and the
verification call, so the `www-authenticate` value and `verifyPayment` are the
provider-specific parts. Save the secret with
`somewhere env set PAYMENT_SECRET --stdin`, which keeps it out of your shell
history, and the URL with `somewhere env set PAYMENT_VERIFY_URL <url>`. Recording each receipt in a
`serverOnly()` table with a unique column makes a replayed credential a no-op.

## 2. Protect an API with OAuth

```ts
// api/data.ts — accepts tokens from your OAuth authorization server
export default async function data(request: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  const origin = new URL(request.url).origin;
  const metadata = `${origin}/api/oauth-protected-resource`;
  const token = request.headers.get('authorization')?.match(/^Bearer\s+(.+)$/i)?.[1];
  const subject = token ? await introspect(token, `${origin}/api/data`, sw) : null;
  if (!subject) {
    return new Response(JSON.stringify({ error: 'invalid_token' }), {
      status: 401,
      headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json', 'www-authenticate': `Bearer resource_metadata="${metadata}"` },
    });
  }
  return Response.json({ subject, items: [] });
}

// RFC 7662 token introspection at your authorization server. Accept only an
// active token issued for this API: its audience must name this resource.
async function introspect(token: string, resource: string, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<string | null> {
  const response = await fetch(sw.env.OAUTH_INTROSPECTION_URL, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {
      authorization: `Basic ${btoa(`${sw.env.OAUTH_CLIENT_ID}:${sw.env.OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET}`)}`,
      'content-type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded',
    },
    body: new URLSearchParams({ token }),
  });
  if (!response.ok) return null;
  const result = (await response.json()) as { active?: boolean; sub?: string; aud?: string | string[] };
  const audience = Array.isArray(result.aud) ? result.aud : [result.aud];
  return result.active && result.sub && audience.includes(resource) ? result.sub : null;
}
```

```ts
// api/oauth-protected-resource.ts — the metadata the 401 points to (RFC 9728)
export default async function protectedResource(request: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  const origin = new URL(request.url).origin;
  return Response.json({
    resource: `${origin}/api/data`,
    authorization_servers: [sw.env.OAUTH_ISSUER],
    bearer_methods_supported: ['header'],
  });
}
```

A client without a token gets the 401, follows `resource_metadata` to learn
which authorization server issues tokens, signs in there, and retries with
`Authorization: Bearer <token>`. MCP clients that implement the MCP
authorization spec take this same path. Functions answer paths under `/api/`,
so the metadata lives at `/api/oauth-protected-resource` and the challenge
names that URL. Accept only tokens whose `aud` names this API, so a token
issued for another API is refused. Register the API's public URL as the token
audience; the check uses the host the request arrived on. Your authorization server is the one you
already use; the platform does not issue OAuth tokens for your API.

## Rules that keep the challenge intact

- Return the 401 yourself. `sw.auth.requireUser` answers a signed-out caller
  with its own JSON 401, which carries no `WWW-Authenticate` header; use
  `sw.auth.fromRequest`, or your own token check, when a client needs the
  challenge.
- Agents and servers send no `Origin` header, so browser CORS rules never
  apply to them. A browser app on another origin needs two things: its origin
  on the project's allow-list
  (`somewhere project allowed-origins set`), because the platform refuses a
  request that carries `Authorization` from any other origin before your
  function runs; and your own `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` plus
  `Access-Control-Expose-Headers: WWW-Authenticate` on the response, which
  then replace the platform's CORS headers (`cors`).

## Check it

```bash
somewhere deploy
# Set APP_URL to your app's HTTPS origin, without a trailing slash.
curl -si "$APP_URL/api/report" | grep -iE '^HTTP|^www-authenticate'   # 402 and its challenge
curl -si "$APP_URL/api/data" | grep -iE '^HTTP|^www-authenticate'     # 401 and resource_metadata
```

Build a chatbot that holds conversation history, looks things up in
your project's database, and answers grounded questions. Uses
bounded inline `sw.agent.run` with tools and server-side conversation history.
See `sw.agent` for durable execution, hooks, and limits.

## Database schema

```ts
// db/schema.ts — deploy this declaration with the app source.
import { schema, table, id, text, timestamp, owner } from 'somewhere/db'

export default schema({
  knowledge: table({
    id: id({ uuid: true }),
    title: text(),
    body: text(),
    created_at: timestamp({ default: 'now' }),
  }, {
    scope: owner(),
    indexes: [['title']],
  }),
})
```

## Function: api/chat.ts

The generated runtime types infer the inputs to each `sw.endpoint` handler.
Deploy this raw source with the schema above.

```ts
// api/chat.ts
export default sw.endpoint({
  auth: 'required',
  handler: async ({ request: req }, sw) => {
    if (req.method !== 'POST') return new Response('Method not allowed', { status: 405 })
    let body: unknown
    try { body = await req.json() } catch { return new Response('Invalid JSON', { status: 400 }) }
    if (!body || typeof body !== 'object' || Array.isArray(body)
        || !('message' in body) || typeof body.message !== 'string' || !body.message.trim()
        || !('conversation_id' in body) || typeof body.conversation_id !== 'string' || !body.conversation_id.trim()) {
      return new Response('Message and conversation_id required', { status: 400 })
    }
    const result = await sw.agent.run({
      conversation_id: body.conversation_id,
      messages: [{ role: 'user', content: body.message }],
      system: 'Use lookup_knowledge to ground your answers. If nothing matches, say so.',
      maxSteps: 4,
      maxSpendCents: 25,
      tools: {
        lookup_knowledge: {
          description: 'Search this signed-in user’s knowledge rows.',
          inputSchema: { type: 'object', properties: { query: { type: 'string' } }, required: ['query'] },
          execute: async (input) => {
            if (typeof input.query !== 'string') throw new Error('Query required')
            const rows = await sw.db.from('knowledge', {
              columns: ['id', 'title', 'body'],
              where: { $or: [{ title: { contains: input.query } }, { body: { contains: input.query } }] },
              limit: 5,
            })
            return rows.data
          },
        },
      },
    })
    return Response.json({ reply: result.text ?? '', completion_reason: result.completion_reason })
  },
});
```

## Conversation and data ownership

After `requireUser(req)`, inline agent model calls derive the verified user
from that same request. Reuse `conversation_id` on later turns to replay that
user’s server-side conversation. The declared owner scope on `sw.db.from`
uses that same verified request user; do not switch this tool to server-wide
data access. This recipe is inline; do not turn it into a durable callback by
assuming the original request and its closures will still be present.
A bounded run can stop without a final answer; expose `completion_reason`.

## Ground each message in the current state

An agent that changes something (a page, a row, a setting) can claim a change
it never made: with its own earlier replies in the conversation and no fresh
facts, a model may answer "done" without calling the tool. Read the current
state on the server and put it at the start of each person message, for
example `Current state: {...}` followed by what they typed, and tell the model
in `system` to trust that line over the earlier conversation and to say a
change happened only when the tool reported it. Forced tool choice is not a
substitute: some models refuse it (`MODEL_CAPABILITY_UNSUPPORTED`).

## Variations

- **Structured output instead of tool-use:** pass `response_schema`
  and read `result.parsed`. The free default makes no repair call on parse failure.
- **Free model:** omit `provider` and `model` to use
  `gpt-6-luna` — no activation required, rate-limited and
  capped, but free to the user.
- **Meaning-based knowledge lookup:** create an index once with
  `search_index_create`, then `search_upsert` whenever a knowledge row
  changes and query it through `sw.search`. Use `ai_embed` only when the
  agent needs custom vectors or clustering outside the managed search index.
- **Voice input:** run uploaded audio through `sw.ai.transcribe` or the
  `ai_transcribe` MCP tool, then pass the returned text into this same chat
  handler.
- **Generated visual output:** use `sw.ai.generateImage` or
  `ai_generate_image` when the product asks the agent for a hero, thumbnail,
  social card, or other visual asset; store reusable output in the project's
  files.

Related topics: `sw.ai`, `sw.auth`, `sw.db`, `search`.

Calendar slot reservation with availability hints and payment checkout. Each
appointment locks a provider calendar range with `sw.calendar` and binds that
hold to checkout. This recipe stops before app-level fulfillment.

Pick the booking shape first:

- **Paid time slots, no customer account.** The first part of this recipe:
  `sw.calendar` prevents double booking, checkout confirms the slot, and the
  customer is identified by their checkout email.
- **One business whose staff all see every booking.** Store bookings in your
  own table with `anyOf(owner(), appRole(...))`: signed-in customers see
  their own bookings and every staff member sees all of them. Without payment,
  follow "One business, no payment" below; `sw.groups` → `app-wide-roles`
  grants staff.
- **Independent providers who each see only their own bookings.** Give each
  booking row a per-booking provider access row, not an app-wide provider
  role (`sw.db` → `provider-or-booker-and-owner-or-staff-recipes`).

## Database schema

Only provider configuration is server-only here: your functions own it, and
no customer or staff member ever reads it under their own name. Rows a
customer or a staff member reads (appointments, bookings) take a declared
rule: "One business, no payment" below, or the slots-and-bookings shape in
`sw.db` → `provider-or-booker-and-owner-or-staff-recipes`.

```ts
// db/schema.ts — trusted functions own provider configuration; browsers
// reach only the narrow availability and booking handlers below.
import { schema, table, id, text, integer, timestamp, serverOnly } from 'somewhere/db'

export default schema({
  providers: table({
    id: id({ uuid: true }),
    user_id: text(),
    name: text(),
    slot_minutes: integer({ default: 60 }),
    price_cents: integer({ default: 0 }),
    timezone: text({ default: 'UTC' }),
    created_at: timestamp({ default: 'now' }),
  }, { scope: serverOnly() }),
})
```

## Function: api/availability.ts — list open slots

Set each provider's weekly hours once with `sw.calendar.setPolicy(resource,
{ hours: { timezone, weekly } })` from your provider-setup function
(`calendar` → `appointments-hours-and-slots`). `slots` then returns the
starts inside those hours, in absolute time, clear of every hold and booking.

```ts
// api/availability.ts — list open slots for the next two weeks
export default sw.endpoint({
  auth: 'none',
  handler: async ({ request }, sw) => {
    const url = new URL(request.url)
    const providerId = url.searchParams.get('provider_id')

    // provider ids identify the public booking calendar; expose only timing.
    const { data: providers } = await sw.db.from('providers', {
      columns: ['slot_minutes'], where: { id: providerId }, limit: 1, asServer: true,
    })
    if (!providers[0]) return Response.json({ error: 'not_found' }, { status: 404 })

    const now = Date.now()
    const { slots } = await sw.calendar.slots({
      resource: `provider:${providerId}`,
      range: { start: now, end: now + 14 * 24 * 3600 * 1000 },
      duration_minutes: providers[0].slot_minutes,
    })
    // Start and end times only: never forward reservation ids to the browser.
    return { slots: slots.map(s => ({ starts_at: s.start_ms, ends_at: s.end_ms })) }
  },
})
```

## Function: api/book.ts — reserve + checkout

```ts
// api/book.ts — reserve + checkout
export default sw.endpoint({
  auth: 'none',
  body: { provider_id: 'string', customer_email: 'email', starts_at: 'number' },
  // Each call holds a slot for 45 minutes without payment: cap holds per caller.
  rateLimit: '5/hour',
  handler: async ({ body, request }, sw) => {
    const { provider_id, customer_email, starts_at } = body
    if (customer_email.length > 254 || !Number.isSafeInteger(starts_at) || starts_at <= Date.now() ||
        starts_at > Date.now() + 90 * 24 * 3600 * 1000) {
      return Response.json({ error: 'bad_request' }, { status: 400 })
    }
    // This public endpoint deliberately reads the selected provider's booking
    // terms, then exposes only the checkout URL and hold expiry.
    const { data: providers } = await sw.db.from('providers', {
      columns: ['slot_minutes', 'price_cents', 'timezone'],
      where: { id: provider_id }, limit: 1, asServer: true,
    })
    const provider = providers[0]
    if (!provider) return Response.json({ error: 'not_found' }, { status: 404 })

    // hold refuses a range outside the provider's hours (CALENDAR_OUTSIDE_HOURS).
    const ends_at = starts_at + provider.slot_minutes * 60 * 1000

    let hold
    try {
      hold = await sw.calendar.hold({
        resource: `provider:${provider_id}`,
        range: {
          start: starts_at,
          end: ends_at,
          timezone: provider.timezone,
        },
        ttl_seconds: 45 * 60,
      })
    } catch (err) {
      // A caught value is `unknown`: narrow it before reading its code.
      if (typeof err === 'object' && err !== null && 'code' in err && err.code === 'CALENDAR_CONFLICT') {
        return Response.json({ error: 'slot_taken' }, { status: 409 })
      }
      if (typeof err === 'object' && err !== null && 'code' in err &&
          (err.code === 'CALENDAR_OUTSIDE_HOURS' || err.code === 'CALENDAR_ADVANCE_NOTICE')) {
        return Response.json({ error: 'not_a_slot' }, { status: 400 })
      }
      throw err
    }

    try {
      const checkout = await sw.payments.checkout({
        mode: 'payment',
        calendar_hold_token: hold.hold_token,
        line_items: [{ amount: provider.price_cents, currency: 'usd', name: 'Appointment' }],
        success_url: new URL('/checkout-return', request.url).toString(),
        cancel_url: new URL('/book', request.url).toString(),
        customer_email,
      })

      return {
        reservation_id: hold.reservation.id,
        status: 'pending_payment',
        checkout_url: checkout.url,
        hold_expires_at: hold.expires_at,
      }
    } catch (err) {
      await sw.calendar.release(hold.hold_token, { release_reason: 'checkout_setup_failed' }).catch(() => {})
      throw err
    }
  },
})
```

Checkout uses test payments in development and live payments in production.
Live checkout requires completed payment onboarding. A preview can't start a
checkout with a calendar hold (`PREVIEW_PAYMENT_SHARED_RECORD_REFUSED`),
because holds are shared with the live app; test this flow in development.

The checkout binds the hold to the session. The platform confirms the
reservation on checkout completion and releases the hold when the session
expires. Read the reservation with `sw.calendar.get(reservation_id)` in a
function to show its current status on a return page.

To receive calendar checkout events, set your project's webhook URL with
`auth_webhook_set`; the same URL receives account events. See
[Payment events](/docs/payments#webhook-events-what-each-flow-actually-emits)
and [Webhook signatures](/docs/sw.auth#lifecycle-webhook-clean-up-your-own-tables-on-user-delete).
`payment.completed` reports checkout completion. Delayed payment methods
can still be unpaid at that point, so keep fulfillment behind a verified paid
payment status. This recipe ends at checkout and reservation status.

## One business, no payment

Signed-in customers book a slot, see their own appointments, and every staff
member sees all of them. Your `appointments` table is the record;
`sw.calendar` only stops two customers taking the same slot.

The calendar and the database are separate services: no call writes both
atomically, and a failed call does not always mean nothing happened. Order the
steps so that every failure leaves a state you can see and settle:

1. **Hold** the slot. `CALENDAR_CONFLICT` means it is taken; nothing was
   written. A hold that is never confirmed ends by itself at its TTL.
2. **Record** the appointment row with the hold's reservation id and status
   `held`. Only `DATA_CONFLICT` proves the row was not written: release the
   hold then. Any other failure, such as `DB_WRITE_OUTCOME_UNKNOWN` or
   `DB_COMMITTED_READ_FAILED`, may have saved the row, so keep the hold and
   answer "unknown". If nothing was saved the hold runs out by itself; a saved
   row is settled in step 4. Do not book again automatically: the hold still
   owns the slot.
3. **Confirm** the hold. Confirming an already confirmed hold returns it
   again. `CALENDAR_HOLD_EXPIRED` or `CALENDAR_HOLD_NOT_ACTIVE` means the slot
   is no longer held. Any other failure is an unknown outcome: leave the row
   `held`.
4. **Settle** a row only from `held`: `confirmed` when the reservation is
   confirmed, `released` when its hold has ended. Make the update conditional
   on `status: 'held'` and re-read the row, so a status someone else set in
   the meantime (for example staff marking it done) is never overwritten and
   the answer is what is stored.

```ts
// db/schema.ts — customers read their own appointments; staff read all of them
import { anyOf, appRole, id, integer, owner, schema, table, text } from 'somewhere/db'

export default schema({
  appointments: table({
    id: id(),
    reservation_id: text({ unique: true }),
    starts_at: integer(),
    ends_at: integer(),
    status: text({ default: 'held' }), // held, then confirmed or released; staff: done or no_show
  }, {
    scope: anyOf(owner(), appRole({ roles: { read: ['staff'], update: ['staff'] } })),
    client: { read: ['id', 'starts_at', 'ends_at', 'status'] },
  }),
}, { appRoles: { staff: { grantableBy: [] } } })
```

```ts
// api/appointments/book.ts — hold the slot, record it, then confirm
const SLOT_MS = 30 * 60 * 1000

function errorCode(err: unknown): unknown {
  return typeof err === 'object' && err !== null && 'code' in err ? err.code : null
}

// Moves a still-held row to its settled status, then answers with the row as stored.
async function settle(sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext, reservationId: string, status: 'confirmed' | 'released') {
  await sw.db.update('appointments', { set: { status }, where: { reservation_id: reservationId, status: 'held' } })
  const { data } = await sw.db.from('appointments', { where: { reservation_id: reservationId }, limit: 1 })
  return data[0] ?? null
}

export default sw.endpoint({
  auth: 'required',
  body: { starts_at: 'number' },
  rateLimit: '10/hour',
  handler: async ({ body }, sw) => {
    const startsAt = body.starts_at
    if (!Number.isSafeInteger(startsAt) || startsAt % SLOT_MS !== 0 || startsAt <= Date.now()) {
      return Response.json({ error: 'not_a_slot' }, { status: 400 })
    }
    // 1. Hold: the calendar refuses an overlapping hold or booking.
    let hold
    try {
      hold = await sw.calendar.hold({
        resource: 'salon',
        range: { start: startsAt, end: startsAt + SLOT_MS, timezone: 'UTC' },
        ttl_seconds: 5 * 60,
      })
    } catch (err) {
      if (errorCode(err) === 'CALENDAR_CONFLICT') return Response.json({ error: 'slot_taken' }, { status: 409 })
      throw err
    }
    const reservationId = hold.reservation.id
    // 2. Record: the signed-in customer becomes the row's owner.
    try {
      await sw.db.insert('appointments', {
        reservation_id: reservationId, starts_at: startsAt, ends_at: startsAt + SLOT_MS, status: 'held',
      })
    } catch (err) {
      if (errorCode(err) === 'DATA_CONFLICT') {
        // Refused, so nothing was written: free the slot now.
        await sw.calendar.release(hold.hold_token, { release_reason: 'record_refused' }).catch(() => {})
        return Response.json({ error: 'not_recorded' }, { status: 409 })
      }
      // The row may have been saved. Keep the hold: it runs out if nothing was.
      return Response.json({ reservation_id: reservationId, status: 'unknown' }, { status: 503 })
    }
    // 3. Confirm.
    try {
      await sw.calendar.confirm(hold.hold_token)
    } catch (err) {
      const code = errorCode(err)
      if (code === 'CALENDAR_HOLD_EXPIRED' || code === 'CALENDAR_HOLD_NOT_ACTIVE') {
        return Response.json({ error: 'hold_expired', appointment: await settle(sw, reservationId, 'released') }, { status: 409 })
      }
      // Unknown outcome: the row stays held until its reservation says otherwise.
      return Response.json({ reservation_id: reservationId, status: 'pending' }, { status: 202 })
    }
    // 4. Settle.
    return { appointment: await settle(sw, reservationId, 'confirmed') }
  },
})
```

```ts
// api/appointments/[id].ts — one appointment; a held row is settled from its reservation
export default sw.endpoint({
  auth: 'required',
  handler: async ({ params }, sw) => {
    const read = async () => (await sw.db.from('appointments', { where: { id: params.id }, limit: 1 })).data[0]
    const row = await read()
    if (!row) return Response.json({ error: 'not_found' }, { status: 404 })
    if (row.status !== 'held') return row
    const { reservation } = await sw.calendar.get(row.reservation_id)
    const active = (reservation.status === 'hold' || reservation.status === 'pending_payment')
      && reservation.expires_at !== null && reservation.expires_at > Date.now()
    if (active) return row // still held: it is confirmed or runs out later
    const status = reservation.status === 'confirmed' ? 'confirmed' : 'released'
    // Only a row that is still held moves; a status set meanwhile stands.
    await sw.db.update('appointments', { set: { status }, where: { id: row.id, status: 'held' } })
    return (await read()) ?? Response.json({ error: 'not_found' }, { status: 404 })
  },
})
```

A customer may not change an appointment's status, and a browser `client`
grant applies to the whole table, not to one branch of its scope. So status
changes go through a function. Because the `owner()` branch also lets a
customer update their own row, the role check, not the scope, is what keeps
this function staff-only (`sw.groups` → `app-wide-roles`):

```ts
// api/appointments/status.ts — staff mark an appointment done or no-show
export default sw.endpoint({
  auth: 'required',
  body: { id: 'string', status: 'string' },
  handler: async ({ body, request }, sw) => {
    if (body.status !== 'done' && body.status !== 'no_show') return Response.json({ error: 'bad_status' }, { status: 400 })
    let staff = false
    let cursor: string | undefined
    do {
      const { data, error } = await sw.groups.appRoles.list(request, undefined, cursor ? { cursor } : undefined)
      // A failed read is never treated as "not staff": report it.
      if (error) return Response.json({ error: error.code }, { status: error.status })
      staff = data.items.some((grant) => grant.role === 'staff')
      cursor = staff ? undefined : data.next_cursor ?? undefined
    } while (cursor)
    if (!staff) return Response.json({ error: 'forbidden' }, { status: 403 })
    // Only a confirmed appointment can be marked; a held one is still being settled.
    const { changes } = await sw.db.update('appointments', { set: { status: body.status }, where: { id: body.id, status: 'confirmed' } })
    if (changes) return { ok: true }
    const { data } = await sw.db.from('appointments', { where: { id: body.id }, limit: 1 })
    return data[0]
      ? Response.json({ error: 'not_confirmed', status: data[0].status }, { status: 409 })
      : Response.json({ error: 'not_found' }, { status: 404 })
  },
})
```

A customer cancelling a confirmed appointment calls `sw.calendar.cancel`
with its reservation id, then moves the row from `confirmed` the same
conditional way; `release` applies only to a hold that is not yet confirmed. Grant the first staff member with
`somewhere auth roles grant <address> staff` (MCP `app_role_grant`); `sw.groups` → `app-wide-roles`
has the in-app path.

Related topics: `calendar`, `sw.db`, `sw.payments`, `sw.groups`.

Start Stripe checkout for an always-available catalog. This small recipe
intentionally stops at checkout creation: it does not fulfill an order, send a
confirmation, or reserve and decrement limited inventory.

This example intentionally keeps catalog rows private and returns only a checkout
URL. For a browsable catalog, declare publicRead with selected active rows/fields
and use the generated client. Anonymous results can be cached for up to
120 seconds. For a catalog that must be fresh, use a page handler with
`sw.db.server.from`, an active-products filter, selected public fields and
`Cache-Control: no-store`.

## Database schema

```ts
// db/schema.ts
import { schema, table, id, text, integer, boolean, timestamp, serverOnly } from 'somewhere/db';

export default schema({
  products: table({
    id: id(),
    name: text(),
    description: text({ nullable: true }),
    price_cents: integer(),
    image_url: text({ nullable: true }),
    active: boolean({ default: true }),
    created_at: timestamp({ default: 'now' }),
  }, { scope: serverOnly() }),
});
```

## Function: api/checkout.ts — create checkout

```ts
// api/checkout.ts
type CartItem = { product_id: string; quantity: number };

function isCartItem(item: unknown): item is CartItem {
  if (typeof item !== 'object' || item === null) return false;
  return 'product_id' in item && typeof item.product_id === 'string'
    && 'quantity' in item && typeof item.quantity === 'number'
    && Number.isInteger(item.quantity) && item.quantity >= 1 && item.quantity <= 99;
}

export default async function checkout(req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  if (req.method !== 'POST') {
    return new Response('Use POST.', { status: 405, headers: { Allow: 'POST' } });
  }
  const body: unknown = await req.json().catch(() => null);
  if (typeof body !== 'object' || body === null || !('items' in body)
    || !Array.isArray(body.items) || body.items.length < 1 || body.items.length > 20
    || !body.items.every(isCartItem) || !('customer_email' in body)
    || typeof body.customer_email !== 'string') {
    return Response.json({ error: 'invalid_cart' }, { status: 400 });
  }
  const { items, customer_email } = body;
  const ids = items.map(item => item.product_id);
  if (new Set(ids).size !== ids.length) {
    return Response.json({ error: 'duplicate_product' }, { status: 400 });
  }

  // Public endpoint: cap checkout sessions per caller.
  const ip = req.headers.get('cf-connecting-ip') || 'unknown';
  const allowance = await sw.rateLimit.check(`checkout:${ip}`, 10, 600);
  if (!allowance.allowed) {
    return Response.json({ error: 'too_many_checkouts', retry_at: allowance.reset }, { status: 429 });
  }

  // Read active products and their current prices from the server-only catalog.
  const { data: products } = await sw.db.server.from('products', {
    columns: ['id', 'name', 'price_cents'],
    where: { id: { in: ids }, active: true },
    limit: 20,
  });
  const byId = new Map(products.map(product => [product.id, product]));
  const lineItems: SomewherePaymentsLineItem[] = [];
  for (const item of items) {
    const product = byId.get(item.product_id);
    if (!product) return Response.json({ error: 'product_unavailable' }, { status: 400 });
    lineItems.push({ amount: product.price_cents, currency: 'usd', name: product.name, quantity: item.quantity });
  }

  const result = await sw.payments.checkout({
    mode: 'payment',
    line_items: lineItems,
    success_url: new URL('/thanks', req.url).toString(),
    cancel_url: new URL('/cart', req.url).toString(),
    customer_email,
  });
  if (!result.url) return Response.json({ error: 'checkout_unavailable' }, { status: 502 });
  return Response.json({ status: 'pending_payment', checkout_url: result.url });
}
```

## Notes

- **Inventory.** This recipe is for products that remain available. A limited-
  stock store needs a reservation design that handles expiry, payment failure,
  and compensation; do not add a guarded decrement to this flow and call it an
  inventory guarantee.
- **Payment mode.** To try the handler before connecting a payment account,
  set `env: 'dev'` in the `sw.payments.checkout` options. A production
  function defaults to live payments and requires a connected account. A
  preview checkout uses test payments, but its
  payment callbacks are withheld from your project webhook; test fulfillment in
  development.
- **Fulfillment.** This example creates checkout. When it settles, your
  project webhook receives `payment.completed` with its
  `checkout_session_id`, amount and currency
  ([Payment events](/docs/payments#webhook-events-what-each-flow-actually-emits));
  verify the signature and match the session, amount, currency and mode to
  your saved order. Save `session_id`, `amount_total_cents` and
  `stripe_mode` from the checkout response, and the currency your server
  created it with. Test and live events
  share the project webhook. Apply order and stock changes in the same
  transaction as a unique fulfillment record for the checkout session;
  duplicate deliveries then leave them unchanged. On duplicates too, call
  `sw.jobs.create` for confirmation email with the same
  `idempotency_key`; it returns the existing job or admits the missing one.
  Keep fulfillment out of this checkout handler.
- **Cart persistence.** Store the cart in the browser. For logged-in carts,
  gate behind `sw.auth.requireUser` and use a separately declared owner table.
- **Catalog discovery.** Create a product index once with
  `search_index_create`, then `search_upsert` whenever a product changes.

Related topics: `payments`, `sw.billing`, `sw.db`, `search`.

End users of your chatbot app keep multiple separate conversations,
like Claude.ai's sidebar. The conversation infrastructure already
exists on the platform — `sw.ai.forUser(userId).chat({ conversation_id })`
scopes history to the subject. This recipe shows the UI pattern that
wraps it.

For one conversation per signed-in user, keep one conversation_id and use
sw.ai.forUser(user.id).chat with that id. The list and switcher below are
needed only when a user can keep several conversations.

## Two ids per conversation

- `conversation_id` — the id YOUR app picks and passes to `chat`. `chat`
  echoes it back. Use it to continue a conversation.
- stored `id` — the platform's row id, returned by `conversations.list()`
  (each row also carries `client_conversation_id`, the id you picked).
  `conversations.get(id)` and `conversations.delete(id)` take THIS id —
  passing your own `conversation_id` to them finds nothing.

## Backend — four functions

The generated runtime types infer the inputs to each `sw.endpoint` handler.

```ts
// api/chat/send.ts — POST { conversation_id?, message } → { reply, conversation_id }
export default sw.endpoint({
  auth: 'required',
  handler: async ({ request: req, user }, sw) => {
    if (req.method !== 'POST') return new Response('Method not allowed', { status: 405 })
    let body: unknown
    try { body = await req.json() } catch { return new Response('Invalid JSON', { status: 400 }) }
    if (!body || typeof body !== 'object' || Array.isArray(body)
        || !('message' in body) || typeof body.message !== 'string' || !body.message.trim()) {
      return new Response('message required', { status: 400 })
    }
    const conversation_id = 'conversation_id' in body && typeof body.conversation_id === 'string' && body.conversation_id
      ? body.conversation_id
      : crypto.randomUUID()
    const r = await sw.ai.forUser(user.id).chat({
      provider: 'anthropic',
      conversation_id,
      system: 'You are a helpful assistant.',
      messages: [{ role: 'user', content: body.message }],
    })
    return Response.json({ reply: r.text, conversation_id })
  },
});
```

```ts
// api/chat/list.ts — GET → [{ id, conversation_id, updated_at, message_count }]
export default sw.endpoint({
  auth: 'required',
  handler: async ({ user }, sw) => {
    const { conversations } = await sw.ai.forUser(user.id).conversations.list({ limit: 50 })
    return Response.json(conversations.map(c => ({
      id: c.id,                                   // stored id → get / delete
      conversation_id: c.client_conversation_id,  // your id → send
      updated_at: c.updated_at,
      message_count: c.message_count,
    })))
  },
});
```

```ts
// api/chat/get.ts — GET ?id=<stored id from list> → { messages }
export default sw.endpoint({
  auth: 'required',
  handler: async ({ request: req, user }, sw) => {
    const id = new URL(req.url).searchParams.get('id')
    if (!id) return new Response('id required', { status: 400 })
    const conversation = await sw.ai.forUser(user.id).conversations.get(id)
    return Response.json({ messages: conversation.messages.map(m => ({ role: m.role, content: m.content })) })
  },
});
```

```ts
// api/chat/delete.ts — DELETE ?id=<stored id from list>
export default sw.endpoint({
  auth: 'required',
  handler: async ({ request: req, user }, sw) => {
    if (req.method !== 'DELETE') return new Response('Method not allowed', { status: 405 })
    const id = new URL(req.url).searchParams.get('id')
    if (!id) return new Response('id required', { status: 400 })
    await sw.ai.forUser(user.id).conversations.delete(id)
    return new Response(null, { status: 204 })
  },
});
```

## Frontend

The sidebar keeps both ids per row: `id` to load or delete, `conversation_id`
to keep chatting. After the first message of a new chat, reload the list to
pick up its stored id.

```tsx
// src/ChatApp.tsx
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from 'react';

type Block = { type: string; text?: string };
type Message = { role: string; content: string | Block[] };
type Row = { id: string; conversation_id: string | null; updated_at: string; message_count: number };

const text = (content: Message['content']): string =>
  typeof content === 'string' ? content : content.filter((b) => b.type === 'text').map((b) => b.text ?? '').join('');

async function getJson<T>(url: string, init?: RequestInit): Promise<T> {
  const res = await fetch(url, init);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Request failed (${res.status})`);
  return (await res.json()) as T;
}

export function ChatApp() {
  const [conversations, setConversations] = useState<Row[]>([]);
  const [current, setCurrent] = useState<Row | null>(null); // a list row; null = new chat
  const [messages, setMessages] = useState<Message[]>([]);
  const [input, setInput] = useState('');
  const [sending, setSending] = useState(false);
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);
  // The conversation on screen right now. A response that arrives after the
  // user switched conversations is dropped instead of overwriting the new one.
  const shown = useRef<string | null>(null);
  const fail = (e: unknown) => setError(e instanceof Error ? e.message : String(e));

  const refresh = () => getJson<Row[]>('/api/chat/list');
  useEffect(() => { refresh().then(setConversations, fail); }, []);
  useEffect(() => {
    shown.current = current?.id ?? null;
    if (!current) { setMessages([]); return; }
    let stale = false;
    getJson<{ messages: Message[] }>(`/api/chat/get?id=${encodeURIComponent(current.id)}`)
      .then((d) => { if (!stale) setMessages(d.messages); }, (e) => { if (!stale) fail(e); });
    return () => { stale = true; };
  }, [current?.id]);

  async function send() {
    const message = input.trim();
    if (!message || sending) return; // one send at a time: Enter twice sends once
    const from = current;
    const optimistic: Message[] = [...messages, { role: 'user', content: message }];
    setMessages(optimistic); setInput(''); setError(null); setSending(true);
    try {
      const r = await getJson<{ reply: string; conversation_id: string }>('/api/chat/send', {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
        body: JSON.stringify({ conversation_id: from?.conversation_id ?? undefined, message }),
      });
      const rows = await refresh();
      setConversations(rows);
      if (shown.current !== (from?.id ?? null)) return; // the user moved on; the reply is stored server-side
      setMessages([...optimistic, { role: 'assistant', content: r.reply }]);
      if (!from) setCurrent(rows.find((c) => c.conversation_id === r.conversation_id) ?? null);
    } catch (e) {
      if (shown.current === (from?.id ?? null)) fail(e);
    } finally {
      setSending(false);
    }
  }

  return (
    <div style={{ display: 'flex' }}>
      <aside style={{ width: 240, borderRight: '1px solid #eee' }}>
        <button type="button" onClick={() => setCurrent(null)}>+ New chat</button>
        {conversations.map((c) => (
          <button type="button" key={c.id} onClick={() => setCurrent(c)}
                  style={{ display: 'block', padding: 8, background: current?.id === c.id ? '#f0f0f0' : 'transparent' }}>
            {new Date(c.updated_at).toLocaleString()} · {c.message_count} messages
          </button>
        ))}
      </aside>
      <main style={{ flex: 1, padding: 16 }}>
        {messages.map((m, i) => <p key={i}><strong>{m.role}:</strong> {text(m.content)}</p>)}
        {error ? <p role="alert">{error}</p> : null}
        <input value={input} disabled={sending} onChange={(e) => setInput(e.target.value)}
               onKeyDown={(e) => { if (e.key === 'Enter') void send(); }} />
      </main>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## What you get for free

- **History server-side.** You never store messages in localStorage —
  reloading the page restores the full transcript via conversations.get.
- **Subject scoping.** sw.ai.forUser(userId) means conversation_ids are
  isolated per user; a leaked id from user A doesn't load user B's
  history.
- **Compaction.** Long conversations auto-compact via the
  history_max_messages / history_max_tokens caps; pass
  compaction:'summarize' to fold dropped turns into a rolling summary.

Related topics: `sw.ai`, `sw.auth`.

**A recipe, not a separate primitive.** It combines a `cron` schedule (or a
job), a function that writes with `sw.db.server`, and a declared live view
(`docs({ topic: 'live' })`). You write the poller.

The golden path for any app that shows the SAME live value to many
viewers at once: live scores, a price ticker, a leaderboard, an auction
count, a status board. There is a right way and a wrong way, and the
wrong way is the one most agents reach for first.

**Anti-pattern (don't): per-client polling.** Every viewer hits the
source on a timer. A World-Cup-style live-scores page where 5,000
viewers each poll the source every 12 seconds makes ~25,000 calls/minute
to that source — your rate limits trip, the source may ban you, and the
cost scales with your audience. Worse with every viewer you add.

**Pattern (do): one server-side poller, one declared live view.** Fetch
the source ONCE on the server per tick, write the snapshot to your
database, and let every client's declared live view re-read it. Load on
the source is O(1) — the same single fetch whether one person or a
million are watching.

## Step 0 — taking over a project you didn't build

Adding live data usually means editing an app you didn't write. Orient
first, with the dependable sequence:

  1. project_files_list { project_id }  — the file tree (paths only).
  2. project_grep { project_id, pattern } — find where things live
     (e.g. the polling code, the page that renders the value).
  3. project_file_read { project_id, path } — read the few files that
     matched.

## The pattern, end to end

  1. A scheduled function (cron) fetches the live source ONCE per tick.
  2. It writes the snapshot to a table with the structured builder, so a
     client connecting mid-stream reads the current value immediately and
     the write invalidates every subscribed view.
  3. A second function DECLARES the live view over that table.
  4. Each client calls that function once for first paint, then re-reads
     it on every invalidation. No client ever polls the source.

Declare the snapshot table in `db/schema.ts` — it is the same value for
everyone, so `shared()`. `players` is the reference table the roster job
below fills; only your functions touch it, so `serverOnly()`:

```typescript
// db/schema.ts
import { schema, table, id, text, integer, timestamp, shared, serverOnly } from 'somewhere/db'

export default schema({
  live_snapshot: table({
    id: id(), key: text({ unique: true }), json: text(),
    updated_at: timestamp({ default: 'now' }),
  }, { scope: shared() }),
  players: table({
    id: id(), key: text({ unique: true }), team: text(), name: text(),
    number: integer({ nullable: true }), position: text({ nullable: true }),
  }, { scope: serverOnly() }),
})
```

## Server — the poller (a scheduled function)

```typescript
// api/poll-scores.ts — fetch once, write the snapshot. Runs only when the scheduler calls it.
export default async function (req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  if (!(await sw.jobs.verifyInvocation(req))) return new Response('forbidden', { status: 403 })

  const res = await fetch('https://example.com/api/scores/live')
  if (!res.ok) return new Response('source returned ' + res.status, { status: 502 })
  const scores = await res.json()

  // A scheduled run has no signed-in user, so it writes with server authority
  // and names the author explicitly: null marks a system row no app user wrote.
  // The composed write is what invalidates every live view over this table;
  // onConflict: 'update' replaces the row for the same key instead of adding one.
  // Raw sw.db.query never feeds a live view — and on a project with a
  // db/schema.ts it cannot write at all.
  await sw.db.server.insert('live_snapshot',
    { key: 'scores', json: JSON.stringify(scores), _sw_author_id: null },
    { onConflict: 'update' })
  return Response.json({ ok: true })
}
```

## Server — the declared view the browser subscribes to

```typescript
// api/scores.ts
export default async function (_req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext) {
  return Response.json(await sw.rooms.live('scores.current', sw.db.from('live_snapshot', {
    where: { key: 'scores' },
    columns: ['id', 'key', 'json'],
    order: [['id', 'asc']],
    limit: 1,
  })))
}
```

Viewers read this as signed-in users: a `shared()` read with no signed-in
user is refused `401 AUTH_REQUIRED`, and live views are the signed-in
browser path (`docs({ topic: 'live' })`).

Schedule the poller to refresh once a minute:

```text
cron_create({ project_id: "my-app", schedule: "*/5 * * * *", handler: "/api/poll-scores" })
```

## Client — read once, then subscribe (never poll)

```js
import { watchLive } from '/__sw/rooms/client.js'

// First paint AND every update come from the same call: watchLive loads
// through your function, then re-loads whenever the platform says the
// declared view may be stale. The source is never touched from the browser.
watchLive(
  () => fetch('/api/scores', { credentials: 'include' }).then(r => r.json()),
  (rows) => render(JSON.parse(rows[0].json)),
)
```

Every frame on that socket is control only — "this view may now be stale" —
so the browser always re-reads through your function and never applies a row
off the wire. `watchLive` also refreshes on reconnect, on tab focus, when the network
comes back and every 60 seconds, so a lost notice self-heals.

Caller-chosen channels are not an option here. The supported browser
path is the signed invalidation channel created by `sw.rooms.live`; see
`docs({ topic: 'rooms' })` under "Live lists".

## Sub-minute updates (live sports, finance)

**Cron cannot go sub-minute — a hard floor.** A 5-field cron expression has
no seconds field, so you cannot schedule "every 10 seconds," and your plan
may set a wider minimum than a minute on top of that (`cron_list` reports
it as `policy.min_interval_minutes`). For sub-minute live data you do NOT
lean on cron for the cadence:

- If you RECEIVE the data (a webhook, an inbound feed, a user action),
  write the snapshot the instant it arrives — the composed write
  invalidates every subscribed view immediately, with no schedule at all.
- If you must PULL it, run the cron poller at the tightest interval your
  plan allows to keep the snapshot fresh; anything arriving faster is
  written from wherever it enters your system.

Either way the rule holds: every viewer re-reads one snapshot through your
own function — the source is hit O(1), never once-per-viewer.

## Reference data: enrich into a table, don't hardcode it

Seed/reference data — a squad roster, a product catalog, a venue list —
does NOT belong inline in your frontend. Hardcoding it means every
correction is a redeploy. Instead, write a RE-RUNNABLE job that builds
the dataset into `sw.db` (or a `sw.fs` JSON file), and have the page
read it at runtime:

```typescript
// api/jobs/build-rosters.ts — fetch sources, extract to a schema, store. Re-runnable.
export default async function (req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  if (!(await sw.jobs.verifyInvocation(req))) return new Response('forbidden', { status: 403 })
  const res = await fetch('https://example.com/teams')
  if (!res.ok) return new Response('source returned ' + res.status, { status: 502 })
  const raw = await res.text()
  const result = await sw.ai.chat({
    messages: [{ role: 'user', content:
      `Extract every player from this page as a JSON array of
       {team, name, number, position}. Return ONLY the JSON.\n\n${raw}` }],
  })
  const players = JSON.parse(result.text)
  if (!Array.isArray(players)) return new Response('extraction did not return a list', { status: 502 })
  // players is the serverOnly() table in db/schema.ts above; a job has no
  // signed-in user, so it writes with server authority. The upsert on the
  // unique key replaces a player's row on the next run instead of adding one.
  let upserted = 0
  for (const p of players) {
    if (typeof p?.team !== 'string' || typeof p?.name !== 'string') continue
    await sw.db.server.insert('players', {
      key: `${p.team}:${p.name}`, team: p.team, name: p.name,
      number: Number.isInteger(p.number) ? p.number : null,
      position: typeof p.position === 'string' ? p.position : null,
    }, { onConflict: 'update' })
    upserted++
  }
  return Response.json({ upserted })
}
```

Run it as a job (`sw.jobs.create({ handler: '/api/jobs/build-rosters' })`) or
on a cron (`cron_create`) to refresh. The frontend
fetches `/api/players`, so correcting a roster updates the live app
with NO redeploy. Same shape for any "fetch sources → extract to a
schema → serve" job.

## Start with managed access

A Somewhere app is one project: the React UI in `src/`, server functions in
`api/`, tables in `db/schema.ts`, and sign-in through the starter's auth
route. Pages read and write declared tables through the generated
`somewhere:data` client: the table's scope and `client` block decide whose rows
and which columns, so normal data needs no API file (`declared-data`). A
public enquiry to a published profile uses declared `client.intake`
(`declared-data` → `send-an-enquiry-to-a-public-profile`). A function
holds only what the declaration cannot express: an authorized exceptional
write, an external API call or a job. Mix the two: keep `owner()`/`member()`/`group()`
and the client for normal access; add a function only for that operation or
side effect (`recipe-signed-in-app`). One deploy publishes the UI, the
functions and the schema together.

## Calling a function

When a page does call a function, the request flows like this: the page calls a same-origin
`/api/…` function with its session cookie, the function gets the verified
user from `sw.auth.fromRequest(req)`, and on an `owner()` table
`sw.db.from` and `sw.db.insert` read and write only that user's rows.

The REST pattern below is that path in full: an `owner()` posts table and one
function that lists and creates the caller's posts. Raw SQL through
`sw.db.server.*` runs as written, so authorize the caller and filter by user
yourself there. The other patterns are working sketches to lift and adapt:
chat with history, file upload, background work and an external API proxy.

## Server-only fallback when a rule cannot be declared

Do not mark data people read or change through your app serverOnly() and
hand-write its access checks. Declare who may read and write it; the platform
enforces that for you.

Server-only is right for:
- Tables only server code touches: job state, import staging, audit records, counters.
- Membership/grant ledgers: never grant browser writes. grant() ledgers retain the
  owner() scope required by that policy, with no client write grants.
- A rule the declarations cannot express; keep other operations declared:
  - Arbitrary predicates (such as checking the current time): use a checked endpoint.
  - Different writable fields by role on one table: split tables or check that write.
Server-assigned roles and role-filtered member() operations are declarable; they
are not reasons to make app data server-only.

For a typical app, declare owner() or group() scopes and use the generated
browser client. A team is a built-in group, not server-only team/task tables:
`somewhere docs sw.groups --section team-example`.
If just one operation needs an extra check, keep the table declared and use
sw.db.server only for that authorized operation.

Use this fallback only when every operation requires a rule the declarations
cannot express. This waitlist keeps email addresses private while public
functions accept signups and expose only a count. Declare it in db/schema.ts;
functions authorize their own access through sw.db.server. No separate migration
command is needed.

```ts
// db/schema.ts
import { schema, table, id, text, timestamp, serverOnly } from 'somewhere/db';
export default schema({
  waitlist_signups: table({
    id: id(),
    email: text({ unique: true }),
    name: text({ nullable: true }),
    created_at: timestamp({ default: 'now' }),
  }, { scope: serverOnly() }),
});
```

```ts
// api/join.ts — intentionally public signup; validate before server writes
export default async function (req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext) {
  if (req.method !== 'POST') return new Response('Method not allowed', { status: 405 });
  const input = await req.json().catch(() => null);
  if (input === null || typeof input !== 'object' || Array.isArray(input)) {
    return Response.json({ error: 'JSON object required' }, { status: 400 });
  }
  if (typeof input.email !== 'string' || !/^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(input.email.trim())) {
    return Response.json({ error: 'Valid email required' }, { status: 400 });
  }
  if (input.name != null && typeof input.name !== 'string') {
    return Response.json({ error: 'Name must be text' }, { status: 400 });
  }
  await sw.db.server.insert('waitlist_signups', {
    email: input.email.trim().toLowerCase(), name: input.name ?? null,
  }, { onConflict: 'ignore' });
  return Response.json({ ok: true });
}
// api/count.ts — intentionally public aggregate; never returns signup rows
export default async function (req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext) {
  if (req.method !== 'GET') return new Response('Method not allowed', { status: 405 });
  const { data: count } = await sw.db.server.count('waitlist_signups');
  return Response.json({ count });
}
```

Deploy these source files with your page. `unique: true` enforces email
uniqueness; `onConflict: 'ignore'` keeps a repeated signup unchanged.
Columns are required by default; `nullable: true` permits null. Column
helpers accept `nullable`, `default`, `unique`, `references`,
`onDelete`, and `renamedFrom`; `id()` instead accepts only `uuid`. On a new
table a bare `id()` is a random text key (128-bit hex, not a UUID); a table
that already has integer ids keeps them. `id({ uuid: false })` is an integer
key. An id never grants access: scope and grants decide.
Unknown options are rejected. `count` returns its number in `.data`,
while `from` returns rows in `.data`. Server authority bypasses declared
row permissions: authorize private/admin operations before using it. This
example intentionally allows public signup and a public total.

## Two ways to use the platform

There are two distinct entry points. Pick based on where your code runs.

**Outside the platform** (your laptop, CI, a different host):
- Auth: `Authorization: Bearer smt_...` developer key (or app-user JWT for end-user calls)
- Surface: REST at `https://api.somewhere.cloud/v1/*` and MCP at `https://mcp.somewhere.cloud/mcp`
- Use case: deploy scripts, admin tooling, agent calls, webhooks from third parties

**Inside a deployed function** (code running on the platform):
- Auth: none — the binding is pre-scoped to your project
- Surface: `sw.db`, `sw.fs`, `sw.ai`, `sw.email`, `sw.auth`, `sw.env`, `sw.jobs`, `sw.logs`
- Use case: API endpoints, server-side logic, AI proxies, file uploads, scheduled work

When you write a function, default to `sw.*`. Only fall back to the
REST surface when you have a specific reason (calling another project,
calling from outside).

## Pattern: Chat app with auth + history

Keep the starter's sign-in route (see `sw.auth`). This function uses the free default model
and saves each conversation under the verified user; no account or message
tables need to be declared by the app.

```typescript
// api/chat.ts
export default async function (req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  if (req.method !== 'POST') return new Response('Method not allowed', { status: 405 });
  const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(req);
  if (!user) return new Response('Unauthorized', { status: 401 });

  const input: unknown = await req.json().catch(() => null);
  if (!input || typeof input !== 'object' || Array.isArray(input)
      || !('message' in input) || typeof input.message !== 'string'
      || !input.message.trim() || input.message.length > 4000) {
    return Response.json({ error: 'Send a message of 1 to 4000 characters.' }, { status: 400 });
  }
  const priorId = 'conversation_id' in input ? input.conversation_id : undefined;
  if (priorId !== undefined && (typeof priorId !== 'string' || !priorId.trim() || priorId.length > 128)) {
    return Response.json({ error: 'Invalid conversation ID.' }, { status: 400 });
  }
  // This app allows each user 20 messages per hour.
  const allowance = await sw.rateLimit.check(`chat:${user.id}`, 20, 3600);
  if (!allowance.allowed) return Response.json({ error: 'Try again later.' }, { status: 429 });

  const conversationId = priorId ?? crypto.randomUUID();
  try {
    const result = await sw.ai.chat({
      conversation_id: conversationId,
      messages: [{ role: 'user', content: input.message }],
    });
    return Response.json({ conversation_id: conversationId, reply: result.text });
  } catch (error) {
    if (error && typeof error === 'object' && 'status' in error && error.status === 429) {
      const code = 'code' in error && typeof error.code === 'string' ? error.code : 'RATE_LIMITED';
      return Response.json({ error: code }, { status: 429 });
    }
    throw error;
  }
}
```

Sign in and POST `{ "message": "Hello" }` to `/api/chat`. Keep the returned
`conversation_id` and send it with the next message. The platform loads the
retained history for that user and conversation before calling the model.
The free default stops at its limits without charging. Its rate limit is shared
across your projects; see `sw.ai` for the allowance and limit responses.
For a model choice, use `ai_catalog`; for tools and multi-step work, see
`sw.agent`.

## Pattern: REST API with end-user auth

Schema and function (`api/my-posts.ts`):
```typescript
// db/schema.ts
import { id, owner, schema, table, text, timestamp } from 'somewhere/db';

export default schema({
  posts: table(
    { id: id(), title: text(), body: text(), created_at: timestamp({ default: 'now' }) },
    { scope: owner() },
  ),
});
```

```typescript
// api/my-posts.ts
export default async function (req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext) {
  const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(req)   // session cookie or Bearer token
  if (!user) return Response.json({ error: 'Unauthorized' }, { status: 401 })

  if (req.method === 'GET') {
    const { data } = await sw.db.from('posts', { order: [['created_at', 'desc']] })
    return Response.json(data)
  }

  if (req.method === 'POST') {
    const input = await req.json().catch(() => null)
    if (input === null || typeof input !== 'object' || typeof input.title !== 'string' || typeof input.body !== 'string') {
      return Response.json({ error: 'title and body must be text' }, { status: 400 })
    }
    const { data } = await sw.db.insert('posts', { title: input.title, body: input.body })
    return Response.json(data[0], { status: 201 })
  }
  return new Response('method not allowed', { status: 405 })
}
```

With `posts` declared `scope: owner()` in `db/schema.ts`, the platform
scopes reads to the caller and sets the owner on insert; there is no `WHERE user_id` to write or forget. Raw SQL is
different: in a SQL-mode project, or through `sw.db.server.*` after your
own check, the query runs as written and you own the filter.

## Pattern: Files owned by the signed-in user

Declare a collection and upload from the browser. The platform checks the
session and collection rules; file bytes go straight to storage.

```typescript
// db/schema.ts
import { files, owner, schema } from 'somewhere/db';

export default schema({}, {
  files: {
    documents: files({
      path: '/documents',
      scope: owner(),
      client: { read: true, upload: true },
      limits: { maxSize: '10MB', types: ['application/pdf', 'image/*', 'text/*'] },
    }),
  },
});
```

```typescript
// src/services/documents.ts
import { files } from 'somewhere:files';

export async function uploadDocument(file: File): Promise<string> {
  const { data, error } = await files.documents.upload(file);
  if (error) throw new Error(error.message);
  return files.documents.url(data.id);
}
```

Call `uploadDocument(file)` after sign-in and use the returned URL in a link
or image. The URL checks the requesting session each time; another user
cannot open an owner-only document. Keep existing tables when adding this
collection to your schema. For progress, sharing and replacement, see
`recipe-file-uploads` and `declared-files`.

## Pattern: Background work

Inline jobs (return immediately, work later):
```typescript
// api/welcome.ts — the signed-in user queues their own welcome email
export default async function (req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(req)
  if (!user) return new Response('Unauthorized', { status: 401 })
  const job = await sw.jobs.create({
    handler: '/api/jobs/send-welcome',
    payload: { user_id: user.id, email: user.email },
  })
  return Response.json({ queued: true, job_id: job.job_id })
}
```

Handler (`api/jobs/send-welcome.ts`) — a public URL, so it verifies the
platform's signature before it trusts the payload:
```typescript
// api/jobs/send-welcome.ts
export default async function (req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext): Promise<Response> {
  if (!(await sw.jobs.verifyInvocation(req))) return new Response('forbidden', { status: 403 })
  const { user_id, email } = await req.json()
  await sw.email.send({
    to: email,
    subject: 'Welcome',
    html: '<h1>Hi!</h1>',
    // Stored with the sent message, so a retried run — or a second job for the
    // same user and address — returns that message instead of sending again.
    idempotency_key: `welcome:${user_id}:${email}`,
  })
  return Response.json({ ok: true })
}
```

The handler is a deployed function under `api/`, called with the payload as
its JSON body; a failed run is retried, so keep it idempotent
(`docs({ topic: 'sw.jobs' })`).

A fire-and-forget side effect is a job too: create it and never read its
status.

For scheduled work use `cron_create` to point a cron expression at
a function path.

## Pattern: External API proxy (hide your key)

```typescript
// api/openai-proxy.ts — keeps OPENAI_API_KEY off the client
export default async function (req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext) {
  const user = await sw.auth.fromRequest(req)   // session cookie or Bearer token
  if (!user) return new Response('Unauthorized', { status: 401 })

  const body = await req.json()
  const r = await fetch('https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {
      Authorization: `Bearer ${sw.env.OPENAI_API_KEY}`,
      'Content-Type': 'application/json'
    },
    body: JSON.stringify(body)
  })
  return new Response(r.body, { status: r.status, headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } })
}
```

Set `OPENAI_API_KEY` as an env var on the project. The browser never
sees it. Same pattern for Stripe, Twilio, anything.

For built-in models (chat, image generation, TTS, embeddings, etc.)
prefer `sw.ai.*` — billing is metered through the platform and you
don't manage upstream keys at all.

The platform owns the hard part server-side: social OAuth clients, JWT
validation (`sw.auth.fromRequest`), and session refresh. The happy path puts
ZERO token/session machinery in the browser: **httpOnly cookie sessions**. No tokens in JS,
nothing in localStorage, nothing for XSS to steal — and expected failures
(wrong password, duplicate email, weak password) come back as structured 4xx
with the real message, never an opaque 500.

The whole contract is: **browser → cookie session; non-browser/script/native
client → bearer token**. Packages are optional adapters to that contract.
Direct bearer-backed database/file access from a browser is compatibility mode,
not another recommended path.

Read first: the default sign-in path the starter ships is `sw.auth` → `auth-sign-in-is-already-part-of-your-app`. This topic covers browser customization.

## 1. The backend — one line

`login` / `signup` are developer-key-gated (the browser can't call them), so
one server route mediates them and sets the session cookies. The SDK ships that
route; `somewhere init` writes it:

```ts
// api/auth/[...path].ts
export { somewhereAuth as default } from '@somewhere-cloud/sdk/server';
```

It serves `POST /signup`, `/login`, `/logout`, `/magic-link`,
`/magic-link/verify`, `GET /me`, and the Google/GitHub/Discord
`GET /<provider>-url` + `POST /<provider>` code exchange, and `POST /mfa/challenge`,
the email sign-in code step: in the page, `auth.completeMfa({ code })` finishes a
sign-in that rejected with code `MFA_REQUIRED` (`sw.auth` → `email-sign-in-codes`).
A custom server-side OAuth `/callback` redirect uses section 3.

## 2. The page — the SDK client

```ts
import { createSomewhereAuth, type User } from '@somewhere-cloud/sdk/auth';   // 0.14.1+

export const auth = createSomewhereAuth();   // talks to /api/auth, cookie mode

// Failures surface as AuthError with the real message ("Wrong email or password.").
export function signUp(email: string, password: string): Promise<User> {
  return auth.signUp({ email, password });
}

export function signIn(email: string, password: string): Promise<User> {
  return auth.signIn({ email, password });
}

export function currentUser(): Promise<User | null> {
  return auth.getUser();                     // probes /api/auth/me; null when signed out
}

export function signOut(): Promise<void> {
  return auth.signOut();
}
```

When you use the client directly, call `auth.getUser()` at startup to resolve
the current session on SDK versions that need an initial check. Reading
`getState()` alone only reads the cached snapshot.

`createSomewhereAuth` holds no tokens in cookie mode: `getSession()` is
`null`, `getCachedUser()` paints instantly, and a network blip never
reads as a logout (only a definitive 401 does).

Await `signOut()` in the click handler before navigating or reloading. While it
is pending, keep the current screen or show a disabled signing-out state.
Do not use the appearance of a signed-out form as proof that the server session
has ended. After awaiting, check `auth.getState().signOutUnconfirmed`; show a retry
message when true. `signOut()` can settle without confirming server logout.

Awaiting the call orders navigation after it; it does not establish that a
concurrent session read cannot race with logout. Verify sign-out followed by
immediate reload on the SDK version deployed in your app.

The starter provides the sign-out UI. The client pairs with the handler in
section 1: it treats a sign-in as a cookie session only when the handler
answers `cookie_session: true`, which the packaged handler does. Calls to
your own `api/*` functions just send `credentials: 'include'`.

Password sign-in already includes automatic per-IP and per-email attempt
limits and an account lockout. The SDK route above uses those same controls;
an extra `sw.rateLimit` wrapper is optional. Exact limits and retry behavior:
`somewhere docs sw.auth --section automatic-password-sign-in-protection`.

## React auth state

Read `docs({ topic: 'auth-client', section: 'react-auth-state' })` for the SDK's
React state contract. Extend the starter's existing provider and auth client.
`SomewhereAuthProvider` from `@somewhere-cloud/sdk/react` accepts `client={auth}`
for a shared `createSomewhereAuth()` instance. Inside it, `useAuthState()`
returns `status`, `user`, `signingOut` and `signOutUnconfirmed`.
The React hook exposes `useAuthState().recheck()`. To refresh from the
client on all supported SDK versions, call `auth.getUser()`.

Gate private UI on `status === 'authenticated'`. Render pending UI for
`checking`, a retry for `indeterminate`, and sign-in for `signed-out`.
While `signingOut` is true, disable repeat sign-out and navigation. Check
`signOutUnconfirmed` after the call and offer a retry when true. These are UI
state controls; server routes still authorize each request.

Outside React, `auth.getState()` reads the snapshot and
`auth.onChange(listener)` subscribes to changes and returns an unsubscribe
function. Use the subscription to render from the confirmed state. Browser
clients that publish `data-sw-auth` start checking the session when created
and reuse that check if you call `auth.getUser()` while it is in flight.
`getCachedUser()` is presentation data. Keep authorization in your server routes.

### Wait for auth in a browser test

When the browser SDK publishes `data-sw-auth` on `<html>`, wait for its value.
`checking` means the client is resolving the session, `indeterminate` means
the check failed, and `signed-in` or `signed-out` means it has confirmed
the state. The first confirmed state also fires `sw-auth-ready` on
`document.documentElement`. Wait for a confirmed state, then assert on the
specific page content you need.

In Playwright, including specs run by `somewhere test`, use
`await page.locator('html[data-sw-auth="signed-in"], html[data-sw-auth="signed-out"]').waitFor({ state: 'attached' })`.
To inspect a confirmed page with the CLI, use
`somewhere browser --wait 'html[data-sw-auth="signed-in"], html[data-sw-auth="signed-out"]'`.

## 3. Without the SDK — one backend file (paste exactly this)

The same route written by hand. Pair it with plain `fetch` (section 5) or
`createClient(...).auth` (section 4), not `createSomewhereAuth`. Writing it
from a shell, quote the path: `cat > 'api/auth/[...path].js'`.

```js
// api/auth/[...path].js
export default async function (req, sw) {
  const url = new URL(req.url);
  const sub = url.pathname.replace(/.*\/auth/, '') || '/';
  /**
   * @param {unknown} d
   * @param {number} [s]
   */
  const json = (d, s) => Response.json(d, { status: s || 200 });
  const body = async () => { try { return await req.json(); } catch (e) { return {}; } };
  // Returns { user } after staging cookies. An account with email sign-in codes gets
  // { mfa_required, mfa_token } and no session; /mfa completes it with the emailed code.
  if (req.method === 'POST' && sub === '/login')  { const b = await body(); return json(await sw.auth.loginWithCookie(req, b.email, b.password)); }
  if (req.method === 'POST' && sub === '/mfa') {
    const b = await body();
    if (b.resend) return json(await sw.auth.mfa.resend({ mfa_token: b.mfa_token }));
    return json(await sw.auth.mfa.challengeWithCookie({ mfa_token: b.mfa_token, code: b.code }));
  }
  if (req.method === 'POST' && sub === '/signup') { const b = await body(); return json(await sw.auth.signupWithCookie(req, b)); }
  if (req.method === 'POST' && sub === '/magic-link') {
    const b = await body();
    return json(await sw.auth.signInWithOtp({ email: b.email, redirect_uri: b.redirect_uri }));
  }
  if (req.method === 'POST' && sub === '/magic-link/verify') {
    // Resolves the issued session itself: { user, access_token, refresh_token, ... }.
    const issued = await sw.auth.verifyOtp(await body());
    // A copied account whose second factor is not available here gets a ticket and no session. An account with
    // email sign-in codes is refused (403): it signs in with its password and a code.
    if (issued.mfa_required) return json({ error: 'SIGN_IN_INCOMPLETE' }, 409);
    sw.auth.setSessionCookies(issued.access_token, issued.refresh_token);
    return json({ user: issued.user, cookie_session: true });
  }
  if (req.method === 'GET'  && sub === '/callback') return sw.auth.googleCallbackWithCookie(req, '/');  // completed sign-in: 302
  if (req.method === 'POST' && sub === '/logout')   return json(await sw.auth.logoutWithCookie(req));
  if (req.method === 'GET'  && sub === '/me')       return json({ user: await sw.auth.fromRequest(req) });
  return json({ error: 'NOT_FOUND' }, 404);
}
```

No try/catch needed: expected auth failures surface automatically as
structured 4xx — `{ error: 'INVALID_CREDENTIALS', message: 'Wrong email or
password.' }` and friends — so your UI can show `body.message` directly.
Real bugs still return opaque 500s. (The cookie helpers also accept an
options object: `loginWithCookie(req, { email, password })` works too, and
`signupWithCookie` additionally accepts `{ email, password, display_name?,
locale?, timezone?, turnstile_token? }` — or pass those fields as the fourth
argument after positional email/password, or call it with just that object
and no `req` at all.)

## Response shapes — one contract

Every auth success that carries a user wraps it: `{ "user": { ... } }`.
Success is never a bare user object and never `null` — if the platform
ever failed to produce a user on sign-in, the helper throws
`AUTH_RESPONSE_MALFORMED` instead of returning an ambiguous 200. Errors are
structured `{ error, message }` responses. The platform returns
`503 AUTH_SIGN_IN_UNAVAILABLE` when a database it needed was briefly
unreachable. No credentials were returned and no sign-in write was retried.
With `retry: false`, a session or a failed-attempt count may already have
been recorded. With `retry: true`, nothing was recorded. Either way, show
the message and let the person try again; it is not a wrong password.

The SDK handler can also return `501 COOKIE_SESSION_UNAVAILABLE` if its
runtime cannot attach the required session cookies. It refuses the sign-in
response instead of exposing tokens in a browser response; inspect the
runtime capability error rather than treating it as a password failure.

- `POST /api/auth/login` and `/signup` →
  `{ "user": { "id", "email", "role", "display_name" } }` — the compact
  session identity. The session rides on httpOnly `Set-Cookie` headers; the
  cookie helpers put no token material in the body.
- `GET /api/auth/me` → `{ "user": { ... } }` — the signed-in user. The
  zero-dependency handler (section 3) returns the verified session identity; the
  SDK `somewhereAuth` handler returns the full stored profile
  (`email_verified`, `created_at`, `last_login_at`, `metadata`,
  `locale`, `timezone`, …; no billing fields) — a superset of
  the sign-in shape. Read `data.user` either way.
- `POST /api/auth/logout` → `{ "ok": true }` after confirmed sign-out
  or local cleanup when no session cookie was sent. A non-2xx response means
  sign-out failed; the session may still be active.
- Explicit token mode (`sw.auth.login` / `signup` called
  server-side) additionally carries `access_token`, `refresh_token`,
  `session_token`, and `expires_in` alongside `user`.
  The starter's SDK browser flow uses cookies; its default sign-in response
  body contains no session tokens. Explicit token mode is for clients that
  manage their own session, as described below.

## 4. The root SDK client — createClient

```ts
import { createClient, type User } from '@somewhere-cloud/sdk'   // 0.14.1+

// Same origin, no credential: posts to your /api/auth routes (section 1 or 3),
// and the session is httpOnly cookies.
const client = createClient(window.location.origin, undefined, { fetch: window.fetch.bind(window) })

// Each call returns { data, error }; these throw the SDK's own error
// (error.message is the real cause, e.g. "Wrong email or password.").
export async function signIn(email: string, password: string): Promise<User | null> {
  const { data, error } = await client.auth.signIn({ email, password })
  if (error) throw error
  return data ? data.user : null
}

export async function signUp(email: string, password: string): Promise<User | null> {
  const { data, error } = await client.auth.signUp({ email, password })
  if (error) throw error
  return data ? data.user : null
}

export async function sendMagicLink(email: string): Promise<void> {
  const { error } = await client.auth.sendMagicLink({ email })
  if (error) throw error
}

export async function verifyMagicLink(token: string): Promise<User | null> {
  const { data, error } = await client.auth.verifyMagicLink({ token })
  if (error) throw error
  return data ? data.user : null
}

export async function currentUser(): Promise<User | null> {
  const { data, error } = await client.auth.getUser()   // probes /api/auth/me
  if (error) throw error
  return data ? data.user : null
}

export async function signOut(): Promise<void> {
  const { error } = await client.auth.signOut()
  if (error) throw error
}

// Google — zero token-handoff code: the platform redirects back through
// your /callback route, which sets the cookies and 302s home.
export async function signInWithGoogle(): Promise<void> {
  const { data, error } = await client.auth.signInWithOAuth({ provider: 'google' })
  if (error) throw error
  if (!data) throw new Error('signInWithOAuth returned neither a sign-in URL nor an error')
  window.location.href = data.url
}
```

In cookie mode the SDK holds no tokens: `getSession()` returns
`{ cookie_session: true, user }`, a network blip never reads as a logout
(only a definitive 401 does), and `functions.invoke` rides the cookie
automatically.

The browser constructor needs no bearer or developer key. Do not ship an
`smt_` key or invent a placeholder browser credential. Cookie sign-in does
not credential the SDK's direct `from()`, storage, or channel calls.
Use the generated `somewhere:data` client for declared browser database
operations. File access and business logic go through same-origin functions.

## 5. Without the SDK — plain fetch

If you cannot use the SDK, nothing to install — the cookie does the work:

```js
// Sign in (sign up is the same against /api/auth/signup):
const res = await fetch('/api/auth/login', {
  method: 'POST', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify({ email, password }), credentials: 'include',
});
if (!res.ok) showMessage((await res.json()).message);   // "Wrong email or password."

// Who is signed in? (any page load)
const me = await (await fetch('/api/auth/me', { credentials: 'include' })).json();

// Every authed request — just include credentials:
const r = await fetch('/api/whatever', { credentials: 'include' });

// Sign out: show an unconfirmed outcome instead of assuming success.
try {
  const out = await fetch('/api/auth/logout', { method: 'POST', credentials: 'include' });
  if (!out.ok) showMessage("We couldn't confirm sign-out; the session may still be active.");
} catch {
  showMessage("We couldn't reach the server to confirm sign-out.");
}
```

## 6. React — one hook (plain fetch)

```jsx
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
export function useUser() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    let on = true;
    fetch('/api/auth/me', { credentials: 'include' })
      .then((r) => r.json()).then((d) => { if (on) setUser(d.user || null); })
      .catch(() => {});   // network blip — never treat as logged out
    return () => { on = false; };
  }, []);
  return user;
}
```

## Package status: one SDK, one version

Install `@somewhere-cloud/sdk`. Its tree-shakeable `/auth`, `/react`, and
`/server` subpaths contain the framework-agnostic cookie client, React
hooks/gates, OAuth callback UI, billing components, and the handler for the
same `/api/auth` contract above. The contract matrix verifies every
SDK-exported client method against that handler, including
`sendMagicLink` / `verifyMagicLink` delegation to
`sw.auth.signInWithOtp` / `sw.auth.verifyOtp`.

Header mode is only for non-browser integrations.

The cookies are HttpOnly + Secure (invisible to JS — XSS can't steal them),
SameSite=Lax, Path=/, 30-day lifetime. `sw.auth.fromRequest` auto-refreshes
the session on every call and re-issues the cookie, so users stay logged in
across browser restarts and you never write a line of refresh logic.

A cookie-session write (POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE) must carry a normal browser
`Origin` header — a plain `fetch()` from a page already sends one, so
there's nothing to add. A write with no `Origin` at all (not a real
browser request) gets a 400 `ORIGIN_REQUIRED`. Reads and Bearer-token
requests are unaffected — don't hand-write CORS headers to work around
this.

A non-browser caller (a script, an agent, a server-side caller) doing a
cookie-authenticated write has two supported paths: send the `Origin`
header yourself naming your app's own origin, or stop sending the session
cookie and pass the user's token in an `Authorization: Bearer <token>`
header instead. For custom functions using `sw.auth.fromRequest`, the
bearer path is not origin-checked and fits non-browser callers. The generated
data endpoint `/__sw/data` has a separate rule: every call, including
Bearer-authenticated list/get reads, requires `Origin` set to the app's
own origin. There is no CSRF-token alternative.

## Advanced — manual token mode (native apps / non-browser clients)

Browser apps use the cookie sessions above. This section is for a script, CLI
or native app, where there is no browser cookie jar. The example holds one
session in one process's memory. Await each request before starting the next
so token rotations stay in order. The three session rules are below.

### The session client — `src/session.ts`

```ts
// src/session.ts — for a script, CLI or native app; tokens live in this process's memory
type Session = { accessToken: string; refreshToken: string }
type User = { id: string; email: string }

const APP = 'https://my-app.somewhere.site'   // your app's fixed URL, never one taken from input
const ORIGIN = new URL(APP).origin
let session: Session | null = null

// Tokens and credentials go only to APP: anything resolving elsewhere is refused before a request.
function appUrl(path: string): URL {
  const url = new URL(path, APP)
  if (url.origin !== ORIGIN || url.username || url.password) {
    throw new Error(`Refusing to send the session outside ${ORIGIN}`)
  }
  return url
}

function field(data: unknown, key: string): unknown {
  return typeof data === 'object' && data !== null ? Reflect.get(data, key) : undefined
}
function readUser(data: unknown): User | null {
  const id = field(data, 'id')
  const email = field(data, 'email')
  return typeof id === 'string' && typeof email === 'string' ? { id, email } : null
}
function failure(data: unknown, fallback: string): Error {
  const message = field(data, 'message')
  const code = field(data, 'error')
  return new Error(typeof message === 'string' ? message : typeof code === 'string' ? code : fallback)
}

async function exchange(path: '/login' | '/signup', email: string, password: string): Promise<User | null> {
  const res = await fetch(appUrl('/api/auth-token' + path), {
    method: 'POST', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }, body: JSON.stringify({ email, password }),
    redirect: 'error',   // the password never follows a redirect
  })
  const data: unknown = await res.json().catch(() => null)
  if (!res.ok) throw failure(data, 'Sign-in failed')
  const access = field(data, 'token') ?? field(data, 'access_token')
  const refresh = field(data, 'refresh_token')
  if (typeof access !== 'string' || !access || typeof refresh !== 'string' || !refresh) {
    throw failure(data, 'Sign-in needs another step')   // no token pair in this answer
  }
  session = { accessToken: access, refreshToken: refresh }   // both tokens as one value
  return readUser(field(data, 'user'))
}
export const signIn = (email: string, password: string) => exchange('/login', email, password)
export const signUp = (email: string, password: string) => exchange('/signup', email, password)
export function getSession(): Session | null { return session }

// Use this for every request to your backend that needs the user.
export async function authFetch(path: string, init: RequestInit = {}): Promise<Response> {
  const url = appUrl(path)   // a foreign or credentialed URL throws here, before any request
  const headers = new Headers(init.headers)
  if (session) {
    headers.set('Authorization', `Bearer ${session.accessToken}`)
    headers.set('X-Refresh-Token', session.refreshToken)   // lets the server refresh an expired token
  }
  // The tokens never follow a redirect. A network error rejects here; the session stays.
  const res = await fetch(url, { ...init, headers, redirect: 'error' })
  const access = res.headers.get('X-New-Access-Token')
  const refresh = res.headers.get('X-New-Refresh-Token')
  if (access && refresh) session = { accessToken: access, refreshToken: refresh }   // rotate both or neither
  if (res.status === 401) session = null      // the server already tried to refresh: the session is over
  return res
}

export async function getUser(): Promise<User | null> {
  if (!session) return null
  const res = await authFetch('/api/auth-token/me')
  if (res.status === 401) return null
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Could not load the user (${res.status})`)   // a 5xx keeps the session
  return readUser(field(await res.json(), 'user'))
}

// Revoke on the server with the refresh token first; forget the pair only after it answers.
export async function signOut(): Promise<void> {
  if (!session) return
  const res = await fetch(appUrl('/api/auth-token/logout'), {
    method: 'POST', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ refresh_token: session.refreshToken }),
    redirect: 'error',
  })
  const data: unknown = await res.json().catch(() => null)
  const revoked = res.ok && field(data, 'logged_out') === true
  const absent = res.status === 404 && field(data, 'error') === 'SESSION_NOT_FOUND'   // nothing left to revoke
  if (!revoked && !absent) throw failure(data, 'Sign-out failed')   // a network error rejects first; retry later
  session = null
}
```

Set `APP` to your deployed app's URL: its subdomain or your custom domain. The
pair lives in this process's memory, so a script signs in again on its next
run. A native app that should stay signed in across launches keeps the pair in
the operating system's secure store (Keychain, Android Keystore or Windows
Credential Manager), saved as one value and updated after every rotation.
Google and other OAuth sign-ins are browser redirects: use them through the
cookie flow above (section 4) or `sw.auth`.

### The backend — one function that wires it to `sw.auth`

A thin server function mediates the developer-key-gated calls and returns
the token pair to the client (this is the manual-mode sibling of the cookie
handler above, mounted at a different path so the examples are not pasted over
each other):

```js
// api/auth-token/[...path].js
export default async function (req, sw) {
  const url = new URL(req.url);
  const parts = url.pathname.split('/auth-token');
  const sub = parts.length > 1 ? (parts[parts.length - 1] || '/') : '/';
  /**
   * @param {unknown} d
   * @param {number} [s]
   */
  const json = function (d, s) { return Response.json(d, { status: s || 200 }); };
  const body = async function () { try { return await req.json(); } catch (e) { return {}; } };
  try {
    if (req.method === 'POST' && sub === '/login')      return json(await sw.auth.login(await body()));
    if (req.method === 'POST' && sub === '/signup')     return json(await sw.auth.signup(await body()));
    if (req.method === 'POST' && sub === '/logout') {
      const b = await body();
      if (typeof b.refresh_token !== 'string' || !b.refresh_token) {
        return json({ error: 'VALIDATION_ERROR', message: 'refresh_token is required.' }, 400);
      }
      try {
        // Revokes the session this refresh token belongs to, with every token of that session.
        return json(await sw.auth.logout({ refresh_token: b.refresh_token }));   // { logged_out: true }
      } catch (e) {
        if (typeof e === 'object' && e !== null && 'code' in e && e.code === 'SESSION_NOT_FOUND') {
          return json({ error: 'SESSION_NOT_FOUND', message: 'No live session matched this refresh token; nothing was signed out.' }, 404);
        }
        throw e;
      }
    }
    if (req.method === 'GET'  && sub === '/me')         return json({ user: await sw.auth.fromRequest(req) });
    return json({ error: 'NOT_FOUND' }, 404);
  } catch (e) { return json({ error: 'AUTH_ERROR', message: e instanceof Error ? e.message : String(e) }, 400); }
}
```

### The three rules

- **Keep the pair as one value** — in memory, and in any secure store you add.
  Saving the access token without the refresh token (or the reverse) leaves a
  pair the server refuses, and the user is signed out.
- **A network error leaves the session in place** — `fetch` rejects before the
  session changes, so the same tokens are there for the retry.
- **Rotate only when both `X-New-*` headers are present** — a partial header set
  means no rotation happened.


## The package does not create another path

The SDK is the default: a one-line backend route plus `createSomewhereAuth`.
It is an adapter to the same `/api/auth` cookie contract that the pasted
file plus plain `fetch` implements without a package. Use the SDK for API shape or UI helpers, never to introduce
browser-held credentials.

Structured logs written to the project's log stream.
Readable via the `project_logs` MCP tool or dashboard Logs page.

For reading logs and errors while you debug, start with `browser` → `read-what-happened`.

## Write logs

```ts
// api/log-example.ts — one line at each level; await every write
export default async function (req: Request, sw: SomewhereRuntimeContext) {
  const started = Date.now();
  const config = { region: 'eu', retries: 3 };
  await sw.logs.debug('loaded config', { keys: Object.keys(config) });

  let body: unknown;
  try {
    body = await req.json();
  } catch (err) {
    await sw.logs.error('unreadable request body', { error: err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err) });
    return Response.json({ error: 'Send a JSON body.' }, { status: 400 });
  }
  await sw.logs.info('request received', { has_body: body !== null });

  const ms = Date.now() - started;
  if (ms > 500) await sw.logs.warn('slow request', { ms });
  return Response.json({ ok: true });
}
```

All four levels accept (message: string, data?: object) and return a promise
that resolves once the platform has accepted the line. Await it: a log call
still pending when your function returns can be lost. Prefer `sw.logs` to
`console.*` for anything you need to read back. `data` is stored as JSON.

Each project has a daily log quota that resets at midnight UTC. Past it, the
write is refused with 429 `LOG_QUOTA_EXCEEDED`, the message names the daily
limit, and the awaited call throws an Error carrying that `.code` and
`.status`. When ingestion is saturated, the call throws 503
`LOG_INGEST_UNAVAILABLE`; retry shortly. Other ceilings: docs({ topic: 'limits' }).

## Retention
Application log retention follows the project owner’s plan:
- Free: 7 days.
- Builder: 30 days.
- Pro: 90 days.
- Scale: 365 days.

Hourly cleanup deletes expired entries in batches; a backlog continues on the next run.
Deleted entries have no archive.

## Reading logs
From a shell: `somewhere logs` (and `somewhere errors` for failures). Over MCP:
the project_logs tool with project_id. Inside a function:
`await sw.logs.tail({ level: 'error', search: 'payment', limit: 20 })`.
Both return newest first and take the same filters: `level`, `source`,
`search` (lines whose message contains the text; `data` is not searched),
`trace_id` (one request's lines) and `limit`. In a preview, `sw.logs.tail`
is refused with `DRAFT_PLATFORM_CAPABILITY_DENIED` (logs are not separated by
preview); read them with `somewhere logs` instead.

