## job-create

**Will work outlive the user request, need retries, or need a status later?** `job_create` queues a durable background job. The platform POSTs the payload to your handler URL and retries on failure (at-least-once, up to 5 attempts with exponential backoff). Handlers are expected to be idempotent.

It suits work that should not block a user request: image processing, report generation, outbound email batches, webhook deliveries. `run_at` with an ISO-8601 time and explicit time zone schedules a future start. The job reports `scheduled` while waiting and reserves a slot in the account's job limit; cancel it to free that slot. It starts at or after that time.

**Example:**

Example transcript or fragment

```text
{
  "project_id": "my-saas",
  "handler": "https://my-saas.somewhere.site/api/jobs/process-upload",
  "payload": { "upload_id": "u123", "user_id": "u_alice" },
  "timeout_seconds": 600
}
// Returns: { "job_id": "j_abc", "status": "queued" }
```

## job-get

Get background job details. A `job_id` returns one job; `project_id` (without job_id) lists recent jobs. Optionally filter by status.

**Example:**

Example transcript or fragment

```text
// Single job:
{ "project_id": "my-saas", "job_id": "j_abc" }

// List recent:
{ "project_id": "my-saas", "status": "failed" }
```

